/* Catechism in a Year: the 365-day plan. Notes written by Claude; no Catechism text is reproduced.
   Fields: n day, t title, s/e paragraph range, c range label, ph phase, k key idea, w word study,
   sc scripture groups [label, YouVersion USFM ref], ec ecumenical note, f reflection,
   v Vatican pages [from, to, page code]; review days: r, q question, items [day, title]. */
const PHASES = ["Revelation, Tradition & Authority", "The Church", "Last Things, Mary & the Incarnation", "Christ, the Spirit & Grace", "Sacraments of Initiation & Healing", "Sacraments of Service", "God the Father & Creation", "Life in Christ", "The Ten Commandments", "Christian Prayer"];
const PLAN = [{"n":1,"t":"Why a Catechism?","s":1,"e":10,"c":"1–10","ph":1,"k":"Human life exists to know and love God. The Church hands on that knowledge through catechesis, and this Catechism (1992) is a reference text for the whole faith, meant to serve local teaching rather than replace it.","w":"katēchein (κατηχεῖν), 'to sound down / instruct by voice'. Catechesis is the faith echoed from teacher to learner (Luke 1:4; Gal 6:6).","sc":[[["John 17:3","JHN.17.3"]],[["Matt 28:19-20","MAT.28.19-20"]],[["Luke 1:1-4","LUK.1.1-4"]]],"ec":"The Reformers wrote catechisms too (Luther's Small Catechism, Heidelberg, the BCP Catechism). Those are mainly question-and-answer texts to memorize. The CCC is a full reference compendium, footnoted to Scripture, the Fathers and the councils.","f":"What teaching first shaped your faith, and what did it emphasize or leave out?","v":[[1,3,"P2"],[4,10,"P3"]]},{"n":2,"t":"How the Catechism Is Built","s":11,"e":25,"c":"11–25","ph":1,"k":"The Catechism has four pillars: the Creed (what we believe), the Sacraments (how we celebrate), Life in Christ (how we live), and Prayer (how we pray). They mirror the ancient catechumenate, and the whole is ordered to love that never ends (25).","w":"agapē (ἀγάπη), self-giving love. It is the goal the Catechism says all teaching must serve.","sc":[[["Acts 2:42","ACT.2.42"]],[["1 Cor 13:8-13","1CO.13.8-13"]]],"ec":"Classic Anglican catechesis is built on the same pillars: Creed, Commandments, Lord's Prayer and sacraments. That is shared ancient heritage, not a Roman novelty.","f":"Which of the four pillars feels most familiar to you, and which is the newest ground?","v":[[11,12,"P4"],[13,17,"P5"],[18,22,"P6"],[23,25,"P7"]]},{"n":3,"t":"The Desire for God","s":26,"e":30,"c":"26–30","ph":1,"k":"Faith is a response to God who speaks first. The longing for God is written into the human heart, which is why people of every culture seek him (Augustine's restless heart).","w":"capax Dei (Latin), 'capable of God'. Humans are made open to receive the infinite.","sc":[[["Acts 17:26-28","ACT.17.26-28"]],[["Ps 42:1-2","PSA.42.1-2"]],[["Ps 105:3-4","PSA.105.3-4"]]],"ec":"Protestants who love Augustine share this. The Catholic emphasis that this longing survives the Fall (nature wounded, not destroyed) will matter later in the teaching on grace.","f":"Where have you recognized the 'restless heart' in your own story?","v":[[26,26,"P8"],[27,30,"P9"]]},{"n":4,"t":"Ways of Knowing God","s":31,"e":38,"c":"31–38","ph":1,"k":"Creation and the human person offer converging 'ways' to God. Reason can know God's existence with certainty (Vatican I), yet revelation is still needed to know him clearly and without error.","w":"via (Latin), 'way'. This recalls Aquinas's 'five ways', which are convergent arguments rather than lab proofs.","sc":[[["Rom 1:19-20","ROM.1.19-20"]],[["Wis 13:1-9","WIS.13.1-9"]]],"ec":"Some Reformed theology (e.g. Barth) distrusts natural theology. The Anglican Richard Hooker is much closer to the Catholic confidence in reason. Note that Wisdom 13 is a deuterocanonical text.","f":"Which 'way' to God has carried the most weight for you: creation, conscience, beauty, or reason?","v":[[31,35,"PA"],[36,38,"PB"]]},{"n":5,"t":"How Can We Speak About God?","s":39,"e":49,"c":"39–49","ph":1,"k":"Human words can truly but never fully speak of God. Every likeness between creature and Creator holds within a still greater unlikeness (Lateran IV), so our speech about him is analogical.","w":"analogia (ἀναλογία), 'proportion'. Words like 'Father' or 'good' apply to God really, but in a higher way.","sc":[[["Isa 55:8-9","ISA.55.8-9"]],[["Wis 13:5","WIS.13.5"]]],"ec":"Barth famously called the 'analogy of being' the invention of Antichrist. Catholic teaching holds that creaturely language, purified, can genuinely reach God.","f":"Which name for God helps you most, and where does it fall short?","v":[[39,43,"PC"],[44,49,"PD"]]},{"n":6,"t":"God Reveals Himself","s":50,"e":58,"c":"50–58","ph":1,"k":"God freely makes himself known, not only facts about himself. He does this through deeds and words, in stages: first to our first parents, then in the covenant with Noah and all creation.","w":"apokalypsis (ἀποκάλυψις), 'unveiling'. Revelation is God drawing back the veil on himself.","sc":[[["Eph 1:9-10","EPH.1.9-10"]],[["Heb 1:1-2","HEB.1.1-2"]],[["Gen 9:8-17","GEN.9.8-17"]]],"ec":"This is broadly shared. The Catholic accent (from Dei Verbum) is that revelation is personal self-gift, not only propositions.","f":"How does reading Scripture as God giving himself, not just information, change how you read it?","v":[[50,50,"PE"],[51,53,"PF"],[54,58,"PG"]]},{"n":7,"t":"Abraham, Israel and the Prophets","s":59,"e":64,"c":"59–64","ph":1,"k":"God forms a people through Abraham, the Exodus, the Law and the prophets, preparing for Christ. Holy women such as Sarah, Ruth, Esther and Mary keep Israel's hope alive.","w":"berit (בְּרִית), 'covenant'. It is a binding, family-making bond, not merely a contract.","sc":[[["Gen 12:1-3","GEN.12.1-3"]],[["Exod 19:5-6","EXO.19.5-6"]],[["Jer 31:31-34","JER.31.31-34"]]],"ec":"Covenant theology is common ground. Catholic teaching since Nostra Aetate stresses that God's gifts to Israel are irrevocable (Rom 11:29).","f":"Which Old Testament covenant figure do you most identify with in this season, and why?","v":[[59,64,"PG"]]},{"n":8,"t":"Christ: God's Final Word","s":65,"e":73,"c":"65–73","ph":1,"k":"Christ is the Father's complete and final Word, so no new public revelation is to be expected. Private revelations (even approved ones) add nothing to the deposit of faith and are discerned by the Church.","w":"logos (λόγος), 'word, reason'. The one Word in whom the Father says everything (John 1:1).","sc":[[["Heb 1:1-2","HEB.1.1-2"]],[["John 1:14","JHN.1.14"],["18","JHN.1.18"]],[["1 Thess 5:19-21","1TH.5.19-21"]]],"ec":"Both traditions affirm the sufficiency of revelation in Christ. The private-revelation principle (67) is a useful frame for testing prophetic words in charismatic settings: they may help, but they never correct the deposit.","f":"How should paragraph 67 shape the way you weigh prophetic words or private revelations?","v":[[65,67,"PH"],[68,73,"PI"]]},{"n":9,"t":"Apostolic Tradition","s":74,"e":79,"c":"74–79","ph":1,"k":"Christ commanded the apostles to preach. The Gospel was handed on orally, in writing, and through the successors they appointed. Tradition is this living handing-on carried out in the Holy Spirit.","w":"paradosis (παράδοσις), 'handing over'. Paul uses it positively of what he received and passed on (1 Cor 11:23).","sc":[[["2 Thess 2:15","2TH.2.15"]],[["1 Cor 11:2","1CO.11.2"],["23","1CO.11.23"]],[["2 Tim 2:2","2TI.2.2"]]],"ec":"Many evangelicals hear 'tradition' through Mark 7:8 (human traditions). Catholic teaching agrees that human traditions can mislead, and distinguishes them from apostolic Tradition (83). Article VI of the Anglican Articles subordinates all tradition to Scripture.","f":"What unwritten traditions shaped how you were taught to read the Bible?","v":[[74,74,"PJ"],[75,79,"PK"]]},{"n":10,"t":"Scripture and Tradition: One Source","s":80,"e":83,"c":"80–83","ph":1,"k":"Scripture and Tradition flow from the same divine wellspring and are honored with equal reverence. Apostolic Tradition is distinct from the Church's changeable customs and disciplines.","w":"traditio vs. traditiones (Latin). Capital-T Tradition (the apostolic deposit) is distinct from lower-case traditions (customs).","sc":[[["John 14:26","JHN.14.26"]],[["16:13","JHN.16.13"]],[["2 Tim 1:13-14","2TI.1.13-14"]]],"ec":"This is the sharpest contrast with sola scriptura. Many Anglicans hold prima scriptura (Scripture first, with tradition and reason). Vatican II avoided a strict 'two-source' theory: one revelation, two modes of transmission.","f":"Is any hesitation you feel about Tradition about authority, or about trusting human transmission?","v":[[80,83,"PL"]]},{"n":11,"t":"The Magisterium","s":84,"e":90,"c":"84–90","ph":1,"k":"The deposit of faith belongs to the whole Church, but its authentic interpretation is entrusted to the bishops in communion with the Pope. The Magisterium is not above the Word of God; it serves it. Not all truths are equally central (the 'hierarchy of truths').","w":"magisterium (Latin, from magister, 'teacher'). It means the teaching office.","sc":[[["Luke 10:16","LUK.10.16"]],[["Matt 16:18-19","MAT.16.18-19"]],[["1 Tim 3:15","1TI.3.15"]]],"ec":"This is new terrain for most Protestants. Anglican Article XXI holds that councils 'may err'. The Catholic claim is that the Spirit preserves the Church's definitive teaching from error, not that its teachers are sinless.","f":"Paragraph 86 calls the Magisterium the servant of the Word. What would that service look like in practice?","v":[[84,90,"PM"]]},{"n":12,"t":"Sense of the Faith and Growth in Understanding","s":91,"e":100,"c":"91–100","ph":1,"k":"The whole People of God, anointed by the Spirit, cannot err in belief when it shows universal agreement (sensus fidei). Understanding deepens over time through contemplation, study, spiritual experience and preaching.","w":"chrisma (χρῖσμα), 'anointing' (1 John 2:20, 27). The Spirit's gift to every believer.","sc":[[["1 John 2:20","1JN.2.20"],["27","1JN.2.27"]],[["Luke 2:19","LUK.2.19"],["51","LUK.2.51"]]],"ec":"Newman, an Anglican convert, framed this as the 'development of doctrine'. Truth grows like a seed without changing its nature, which is one answer to 'where is that in the Bible?'","f":"Name a truth you believed for years but only recently understood more deeply.","v":[[91,95,"PM"],[96,100,"PN"]]},{"n":13,"t":"Scripture: God's Word in Human Words","s":101,"e":108,"c":"101–108","ph":1,"k":"God is the author of Scripture, and the human writers are true authors too. Scripture teaches without error the truth God willed for our salvation. Christianity is not a 'religion of the book' but of the living Word, Christ.","w":"theopneustos (θεόπνευστος), 'God-breathed' (2 Tim 3:16).","sc":[[["2 Tim 3:16-17","2TI.3.16-17"]],[["2 Pet 1:20-21","2PE.1.20-21"]],[["Luke 24:45","LUK.24.45"]]],"ec":"There is strong common ground with evangelical regard for Scripture. Note how paragraph 107 frames inerrancy: truth 'for the sake of our salvation'.","f":"What does 'not a religion of the book' mean for your Bible study habits?","v":[[101,104,"PO"],[105,108,"PP"]]},{"n":14,"t":"Interpreting Scripture: The Senses","s":109,"e":119,"c":"109–119","ph":1,"k":"Read Scripture for what its human authors intended, and in the Spirit: attending to the unity of the whole Bible, the living Tradition, and the coherence of the faith. Beyond the literal sense lie the spiritual senses: allegorical (Christ), moral (how to act), and anagogical (our final destiny).","w":"anagōgē (ἀναγωγή), 'leading upward'. It is the sense that points to heaven.","sc":[[["Luke 24:25-27","LUK.24.25-27"],["44-45","LUK.24.44-45"]],[["1 Cor 10:1-11","1CO.10.1-11"]]],"ec":"Brethren-style typology (tabernacle studies, for example) is close kin to the spiritual senses. The Catholic difference is the explicit rule to read within the living Tradition (113).","f":"Take a passage you know well. What are its literal, allegorical, moral and anagogical senses?","v":[[109,119,"PQ"]]},{"n":15,"t":"The Canon and the Two Testaments","s":120,"e":130,"c":"120–130","ph":1,"k":"Apostolic Tradition discerned which books belong in the canon: 46 Old Testament and 27 New Testament books. The Old Testament is never revoked, and the two Testaments illuminate each other through typology.","w":"kanōn (κανών), 'measuring rod, rule'.","sc":[[["Rom 15:4","ROM.15.4"]],[["1 Cor 10:11","1CO.10.11"]],[["Luke 24:27","LUK.24.27"]]],"ec":"The deuterocanonical books are canonical Scripture for Catholics. Anglican Article VI reads them 'for example of life' but not for doctrine, and the Ethiopian Orthodox canon is larger still.","f":"Who decided which books are in your Bible, and on what authority?","v":[[120,130,"PR"]]},{"n":16,"t":"Scripture in the Life of the Church","s":131,"e":141,"c":"131–141","ph":1,"k":"God's Word has power to nourish and renew. The study of Scripture is the 'soul of theology', and the Church urges all the faithful to read it often. As Jerome put it, ignorance of Scripture is ignorance of Christ.","w":"lectio divina (Latin), 'divine reading'. It is slow, prayerful reading that moves to meditation, prayer and contemplation.","sc":[[["Heb 4:12","HEB.4.12"]],[["Ps 119:105","PSA.119.105"]],[["Isa 55:10-11","ISA.55.10-11"]]],"ec":"Here evangelical love of Bible study and Catholic practice meet fully. Vatican II strongly encouraged Bible reading for every believer.","f":"Which Bible habit from your earlier formation do you want to carry with you into Catholic life?","v":[[131,133,"PS"],[134,141,"PT"]]},{"n":17,"t":"The Obedience of Faith","s":142,"e":149,"c":"142–149","ph":1,"k":"Faith is our response to God's self-revelation: a free surrender of mind and will. Abraham and Mary are its great models.","w":"hypakoē pisteōs (ὑπακοὴ πίστεως), 'obedience of faith' (Rom 1:5). Latin ob-audire means 'to listen toward'.","sc":[[["Rom 1:5","ROM.1.5"]],[["Heb 11:8","HEB.11.8"],["17","HEB.11.17"]],[["Luke 1:38","LUK.1.38"],["45","LUK.1.45"]]],"ec":"Catholic faith includes both trust and assent to revealed truth. Luther stressed faith as trust (fiducia). Catholics hold the two together.","f":"Is God asking of you the kind of obedience Abraham showed, or the kind Mary showed?","v":[[142,143,"PU"],[144,149,"PV"]]},{"n":18,"t":"What Faith Is Like","s":150,"e":165,"c":"150–165","ph":1,"k":"Faith is personal adherence to God and assent to his truth. It is a grace and also a free human act; it is certain, seeks understanding, is necessary for salvation, and anticipates heaven. It can also be lost, so it must be nourished.","w":"fides quaerens intellectum (Latin, Anselm), 'faith seeking understanding'.","sc":[[["Heb 11:1","HEB.11.1"],["6","HEB.11.6"]],[["Mark 9:24","MRK.9.24"]],[["1 Tim 1:18-19","1TI.1.18-19"]]],"ec":"Paragraph 162 (faith can be lost) contrasts with 'once saved, always saved', a view common in Brethren and Baptist circles.","f":"How are certainty and searching held together in your faith right now?","v":[[150,152,"PW"],[153,165,"PX"]]},{"n":19,"t":"We Believe: The Church's Faith","s":166,"e":184,"c":"166–184","ph":1,"k":"Faith is personal but never solitary. We say 'I believe' within the Church's 'we believe'. Salvation comes from God alone, but we receive the faith through the Church, our mother in faith.","w":"credo (Latin), 'I believe'. Traditionally linked to cor dare, 'to give one's heart'.","sc":[[["Eph 4:4-6","EPH.4.4-6"]],[["Jude 3","JUD.3"]],[["Rom 10:14-17","ROM.10.14-17"]]],"ec":"The phrase 'Mother Church' goes back to Cyprian. It challenges a purely individual 'me and my Bible' faith, without denying personal conversion.","f":"Who handed the faith on to you, and how would you thank them?","v":[[166,167,"PY"],[168,169,"PZ"],[170,171,"P10"],[172,175,"P11"],[176,184,"P12"]]},{"n":20,"t":"The Church: Names and Images","s":748,"e":757,"c":"748–757","ph":2,"k":"Christ is the light of the nations, and the Church reflects his light like the moon. Scripture describes the Church through many images: sheepfold, field, building, temple, bride and mother.","w":"ekklēsia (ἐκκλησία), 'those called out', translating Hebrew qahal (assembly). 'Church' comes from kyriakē, 'belonging to the Lord'.","sc":[[["Eph 5:25-27","EPH.5.25-27"]],[["John 10:1-16","JHN.10.1-16"]],[["1 Cor 3:9-17","1CO.3.9-17"]]],"ec":"Brethren ecclesiology centers on the gathered local assembly. Catholic teaching sees the local and universal Church as one reality.","f":"Which image of the Church draws you most, and which do you find hardest?","v":[[748,750,"P26"],[751,757,"P27"]]},{"n":21,"t":"The Church's Origin","s":758,"e":769,"c":"758–769","ph":2,"k":"The Church was planned by the Father, prepared in Israel, founded by Christ (the Twelve, with Peter at their head), revealed by the Spirit at Pentecost, and will be completed in glory. She was born from the pierced side of Christ.","w":"qahal (קָהָל), 'assembly'. It is Israel gathered by God, the forerunner of the Church.","sc":[[["Matt 16:18","MAT.16.18"]],[["John 19:34","JHN.19.34"]],[["Acts 2:1-4","ACT.2.1-4"]]],"ec":"Catholic teaching holds that Christ intended a visible, structured Church. Many Protestants see the true Church as an invisible fellowship of all believers.","f":"Does a Church with a visible structure feel to you like good news or like a constraint? Why?","v":[[758,769,"P27"]]},{"n":22,"t":"The Church as Mystery and Sacrament","s":770,"e":780,"c":"770–780","ph":2,"k":"The Church is both visible society and spiritual communion, one complex reality. She is the 'universal sacrament of salvation', a sign and instrument of union with God and of human unity.","w":"mystērion (μυστήριον), rendered in Latin as sacramentum. It means a hidden reality made visible.","sc":[[["Eph 3:9-11","EPH.3.9-11"]],[["Col 1:24-27","COL.1.24-27"]]],"ec":"Calling the Church a 'sacrament' is distinctively Catholic. It does not mean the Church replaces Christ; it means she is his instrument.","f":"How can a flawed, visible community also be a true sign of union with God?","v":[[770,780,"P27"]]},{"n":23,"t":"The People of God","s":781,"e":786,"c":"781–786","ph":2,"k":"Through Baptism, God's people share in Christ's threefold office as priest, prophet and king. Membership comes by faith and Baptism, not by birth.","w":"laos (λαός), 'people'. It is the root of 'laity'.","sc":[[["1 Pet 2:9-10","1PE.2.9-10"]],[["Exod 19:6","EXO.19.6"]],[["Rev 1:5-6","REV.1.5-6"]]],"ec":"The 'priesthood of all believers' is affirmed here (784). Catholics also hold a distinct ministerial priesthood (see Holy Orders later).","f":"How will you live out your baptismal role as priest, prophet and king this week?","v":[[781,786,"P28"]]},{"n":24,"t":"The Body of Christ and the Bride","s":787,"e":796,"c":"787–796","ph":2,"k":"Christ unites believers to himself as a body to its head. He nourishes the Church by his Spirit and loves her as a bridegroom loves his bride. Augustine called head and members together 'the whole Christ'.","w":"sōma (σῶμα), 'body'. For Paul this is more than a metaphor: a real union.","sc":[[["1 Cor 12:12-27","1CO.12.12-27"]],[["Eph 5:29-32","EPH.5.29-32"]],[["Acts 9:4","ACT.9.4"]]],"ec":"Augustine's 'whole Christ' (totus Christus) goes further than most Protestant readings. Christ asked Saul why he was persecuting him, not why he was persecuting his followers.","f":"What changes if Christ takes how his Church is treated as how he himself is treated?","v":[[787,796,"P28"]]},{"n":25,"t":"Temple of the Spirit; Charisms","s":797,"e":810,"c":"797–810","ph":2,"k":"The Holy Spirit is like the soul of the Body. Charisms, whether extraordinary or simple, are graces for building up the Church. They are to be received gratefully and discerned by the Church's pastors.","w":"charisma (χάρισμα), 'grace-gift'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 12:4-11","1CO.12.4-11"]],[["1 Thess 5:19-21","1TH.5.19-21"]],[["Eph 2:21-22","EPH.2.21-22"]]],"ec":"Pentecostal and charismatic experience finds a real home here. The Catholic accent is that charisms serve charity and communion, under pastoral discernment.","f":"Where have you seen charisms build a community up, or divide it?","v":[[797,810,"P28"]]},{"n":26,"t":"The Church Is One","s":811,"e":816,"c":"811–816","ph":2,"k":"The Church's unity comes from the Trinity. It is held by visible bonds: one faith, one shared worship and sacraments, and apostolic succession. The one Church of Christ 'subsists in' the Catholic Church.","w":"subsistit in (Latin, Lumen Gentium 8), 'subsists in'. This wording avoids saying that no elements of the Church exist elsewhere.","sc":[[["John 17:21","JHN.17.21"]],[["Eph 4:3-6","EPH.4.3-6"]]],"ec":"'Subsists in' was chosen deliberately to acknowledge real ecclesial elements outside the Catholic Church while still claiming that the fullness is found in her.","f":"What do you make of the claim that the one Church 'subsists in' the Catholic Church?","v":[[811,816,"P29"]]},{"n":27,"t":"Wounds to Unity and Ecumenism","s":817,"e":822,"c":"817–822","ph":2,"k":"Schism and heresy have wounded unity. People born into separated communities are not charged with the sin of separation and are embraced as brethren. Scripture, grace, the virtues and the Spirit's gifts found outside the visible Church rightly belong to Christ's one Church.","w":"schisma (σχίσμα), 'tear, rip'.","sc":[[["John 17:20-23","JHN.17.20-23"]],[["1 Cor 1:10-13","1CO.1.10-13"]]],"ec":"This is generous toward Protestant Christians. Ecumenism is framed as a duty, not an option (820-822).","f":"What good have you received from earlier Christian communities that you carry forward?","v":[[817,822,"P29"]]},{"n":28,"t":"The Church Is Holy","s":823,"e":829,"c":"823–829","ph":2,"k":"The Church is holy because Christ sanctifies her, yet she embraces sinners and is always in need of purification. The saints, and Mary above all, show what she already is.","w":"qadosh (קָדוֹשׁ) / hagios (ἅγιος), 'set apart for God'.","sc":[[["Eph 5:25-27","EPH.5.25-27"]],[["Lev 19:2","LEV.19.2"]],[["1 Pet 1:15-16","1PE.1.15-16"]]],"ec":"Catholic teaching describes a holy Church made up of sinners. This differs from the 'pure assembly' ideal and strict discipline found in some Brethren traditions.","f":"How do you hold together the Church's holiness and her scandals?","v":[[823,829,"P29"]]},{"n":29,"t":"The Church Is Catholic","s":830,"e":835,"c":"830–835","ph":2,"k":"'Catholic' means whole and universal: the Church has the fullness of the means of salvation and is sent to all peoples. Each diocese is fully the Church, but only in communion with the others and with Rome.","w":"kath' holou (καθ' ὅλου), 'according to the whole'.","sc":[[["Matt 28:19","MAT.28.19"]],[["Eph 1:22-23","EPH.1.22-23"]]],"ec":"Anglicans also confess the 'catholic' Church. Rome adds visible communion with the bishop of Rome as part of what catholicity means.","f":"In what ways has your faith been too local, and how does 'catholic' widen it?","v":[[830,835,"P29"]]},{"n":30,"r":1,"q":"Revelation reaches us through Scripture, Tradition and the Magisterium. Which do you find hardest to trust right now, and why?","items":[[1,"Why a Catechism?"],[2,"How the Catechism Is Built"],[3,"The Desire for God"],[4,"Ways of Knowing God"],[5,"How Can We Speak About God?"],[6,"God Reveals Himself"],[7,"Abraham, Israel and the Prophets"],[8,"Christ: God's Final Word"],[9,"Apostolic Tradition"],[10,"Scripture and Tradition: One Source"],[11,"The Magisterium"],[12,"Sense of the Faith and Growth in Understanding"],[13,"Scripture: God's Word in Human Words"],[14,"Interpreting Scripture: The Senses"],[15,"The Canon and the Two Testaments"],[16,"Scripture in the Life of the Church"],[17,"The Obedience of Faith"],[18,"What Faith Is Like"],[19,"We Believe: The Church's Faith"],[20,"The Church: Names and Images"],[21,"The Church's Origin"],[22,"The Church as Mystery and Sacrament"],[23,"The People of God"],[24,"The Body of Christ and the Bride"],[25,"Temple of the Spirit; Charisms"],[26,"The Church Is One"],[27,"Wounds to Unity and Ecumenism"],[28,"The Church Is Holy"],[29,"The Church Is Catholic"]]},{"n":31,"t":"Who Belongs to the Church?","s":836,"e":838,"c":"836–838","ph":2,"k":"Full incorporation means professing the whole faith and sharing the sacraments and the Church's government. Catechumens are already joined to the Church by their desire. The baptized outside her visible communion are in real but imperfect communion.","w":"communio plena (Latin), 'full communion'. It is the goal of reception into the Church.","sc":[[["Eph 2:19","EPH.2.19"]],[["John 10:16","JHN.10.16"]]],"ec":"Valid Trinitarian Protestant baptism is not repeated. Reception into the Church completes communion rather than starting over.","f":"What does 'completing' rather than 'starting over' mean for your own journey?","v":[[836,838,"P29"]]},{"n":32,"t":"The Church and Non-Christians","s":839,"e":845,"c":"839–845","ph":2,"k":"The Church honors God's unrevoked covenant with the Jewish people. She notes that Muslims adore the one God with us, and sees elements of truth in other religions as preparation for the Gospel. Christ remains the one Savior of all.","w":"praeparatio evangelica (Latin, Eusebius), 'preparation for the Gospel'.","sc":[[["Rom 9:4-5","ROM.9.4-5"]],[["11:29","ROM.11.29"]],[["Acts 17:22-28","ACT.17.22-28"]]],"ec":"This is broadly compatible with evangelical mission. The Catholic tone is more explicit in naming seeds of truth in other religions.","f":"How can you honor what is true in another person's faith while still witnessing to Christ?","v":[[839,845,"P29"]]},{"n":33,"t":"'Outside the Church No Salvation'","s":846,"e":848,"c":"846–848","ph":2,"k":"The axiom, stated positively, means that all salvation comes from Christ through his Body, the Church. Those who knowingly refuse the Church cannot be saved. Those who, through no fault of their own, do not know Christ but sincerely seek God may attain salvation.","w":"extra ecclesiam nulla salus (Latin, Cyprian), 'outside the Church, no salvation'.","sc":[[["Acts 4:12","ACT.4.12"]],[["1 Tim 2:4-6","1TI.2.4-6"]],[["Rom 2:14-16","ROM.2.14-16"]]],"ec":"This is more nuanced than strict exclusivism and also rejects relativism. Notice how paragraph 847 changes the tone.","f":"How does 847 change how you hear 'no salvation outside the Church'?","v":[[846,848,"P29"]]},{"n":34,"t":"The Church Is Missionary","s":849,"e":856,"c":"849–856","ph":2,"k":"The Church exists to evangelize. Mission flows from the Trinity's love and involves proclaiming the Gospel, bearing witness, inculturation and dialogue, all with patience.","w":"missio (Latin) / apostellō (ἀποστέλλω), 'to send'.","sc":[[["Matt 28:18-20","MAT.28.18-20"]],[["2 Cor 5:14-20","2CO.5.14-20"]]],"ec":"Evangelical zeal for mission is fully shared. Catholic mission also includes inculturation and dialogue alongside proclamation.","f":"Who in your circle might be waiting to hear the Gospel through you?","v":[[849,856,"P29"]]},{"n":35,"t":"The Church Is Apostolic","s":857,"e":865,"c":"857–865","ph":2,"k":"The Church is built on the apostles, keeps their teaching, and is guided by their successors, the bishops. The whole Church is sent, so every member shares in the apostolate.","w":"apostolos (ἀπόστολος), 'one sent'. diadochē (διαδοχή), 'succession'.","sc":[[["Eph 2:19-20","EPH.2.19-20"]],[["Acts 1:20-26","ACT.1.20-26"]],[["2 Tim 1:6","2TI.1.6"]]],"ec":"Anglicans claim apostolic succession. Leo XIII (Apostolicae Curae, 1896) judged Anglican orders invalid, which is a live question for converts from Anglicanism.","f":"Why might unbroken succession matter for knowing which teaching is truly apostolic?","v":[[857,865,"P29"]]},{"n":36,"t":"Why a Hierarchy?","s":866,"e":879,"c":"866–879","ph":2,"k":"Christ himself instituted ordained ministry as service. No one can give himself the mandate to preach or to sanctify; it is received through the sacrament. Ministry is both collegial and personal.","w":"hierarchia (ἱεραρχία), 'sacred order'. diakonia (διακονία), 'service'.","sc":[[["Mark 10:42-45","MRK.10.42-45"]],[["John 20:21-23","JHN.20.21-23"]],[["Rom 10:14-15","ROM.10.14-15"]]],"ec":"The Brethren reject any clergy-laity distinction. Catholic teaching sees ordained ministry as Christ's gift for the service of all.","f":"How does Mark 10:42-45 shape what authority in the Church should look like?","v":[[866,870,"P29"],[871,879,"P2A"]]},{"n":37,"t":"Peter and the College of Bishops","s":880,"e":887,"c":"880–887","ph":2,"k":"Christ made Peter the rock and chief shepherd. The Pope, as Peter's successor, is the visible source of unity. Bishops govern their local churches as a college, with and under Peter.","w":"kêphā (Aramaic, כֵּיפָא), 'rock'. Rendered in Greek as Petros/petra.","sc":[[["Matt 16:13-19","MAT.16.13-19"]],[["Luke 22:31-32","LUK.22.31-32"]],[["John 21:15-17","JHN.21.15-17"]],[["Isa 22:20-22","ISA.22.20-22"]]],"ec":"This is the central point of dispute. Read Matthew 16 alongside Isaiah 22: the 'keys' recall the royal steward, an office with successors.","f":"Read Isaiah 22:20-22 next to Matthew 16:19. What connection do you see?","v":[[880,887,"P2A"]]},{"n":38,"t":"The Teaching Office and Infallibility","s":888,"e":892,"c":"888–892","ph":2,"k":"Bishops' first task is preaching the Gospel. Infallibility belongs to the Pope when he defines doctrine ex cathedra, and to the bishops in union with him, especially in ecumenical councils. Ordinary teaching calls for religious assent.","w":"infallibilis (Latin), 'unable to fail'. ex cathedra means 'from the chair'.","sc":[[["Luke 10:16","LUK.10.16"]],[["John 16:13","JHN.16.13"]],[["Matt 28:20","MAT.28.20"]]],"ec":"Infallibility is not sinlessness. Papal ex cathedra definitions are rare: the Immaculate Conception (1854) and the Assumption (1950) are the clear examples.","f":"What is the difference between infallibility and sinlessness, and why does it matter?","v":[[888,892,"P2A"]]},{"n":39,"t":"Sanctifying and Governing","s":893,"e":896,"c":"893–896","ph":2,"k":"Bishops are stewards of grace, above all in the Eucharist. They govern their churches as pastors and vicars of Christ, not as delegates of the Pope, with authority exercised as service.","w":"episkopos (ἐπίσκοπος), 'overseer'.","sc":[[["1 Pet 5:1-4","1PE.5.1-4"]],[["Acts 20:28","ACT.20.28"]]],"ec":"Anglican episcopacy is structurally close to this. The Brethren prefer a plurality of local elders.","f":"Do you know your bishop's name? Pray for him today.","v":[[893,896,"P2A"]]},{"n":40,"t":"The Lay Faithful","s":897,"e":903,"c":"897–903","ph":2,"k":"Laypeople are the baptized who are not ordained or in consecrated life. Their proper task is to sanctify the world from within: work, family and society. They share in Christ's priesthood by offering their whole lives.","w":"laikos (λαϊκός), 'of the people'.","sc":[[["Rom 12:1","ROM.12.1"]],[["1 Pet 2:5","1PE.2.5"]]],"ec":"A robust lay vocation in the world resonates with Protestant teaching on the dignity of ordinary work.","f":"In which 'secular' place (work, home, community) are you the only one who can bring Christ?","v":[[897,903,"P2A"]]},{"n":41,"t":"Lay Prophets and Kings","s":904,"e":913,"c":"904–913","ph":2,"k":"Laypeople share Christ's prophetic office through word and witness, and his kingly office through self-mastery and by making society more just. They may also cooperate in church governance.","w":"basileia (βασιλεία), 'kingdom, reign'. It begins with ruling oneself.","sc":[[["Matt 5:13-16","MAT.5.13-16"]],[["1 Pet 3:15","1PE.3.15"]]],"ec":"Here Catholic and evangelical emphases on lay witness meet.","f":"Where do you need the 'kingly' discipline of self-mastery most right now?","v":[[904,913,"P2A"]]},{"n":42,"t":"Consecrated Life","s":914,"e":933,"c":"914–933","ph":2,"k":"Some are called to follow Christ more closely by vowing poverty, chastity and obedience. This takes many forms, including hermits, consecrated virgins, religious orders and secular institutes. Their lives are a sign of the Kingdom to come.","w":"consecratio (Latin), 'being made sacred'. Being set apart wholly for God.","sc":[[["Matt 19:12","MAT.19.12"],["21","MAT.19.21"]],[["1 Cor 7:32-35","1CO.7.32-35"]]],"ec":"Anglican religious communities exist. Brethren and Pentecostal traditions have little equivalent.","f":"What could poverty, chastity and obedience teach someone who is not called to vows?","v":[[914,933,"P2A"]]},{"n":43,"t":"In Brief: Hierarchy, Laity, Consecrated","s":934,"e":945,"c":"934–945","ph":2,"k":"These are summary paragraphs: one People with different callings, all sharing one dignity and one mission. It is a short day, good for reviewing the week.","w":"vocatio (Latin), 'calling'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 12:4-7","1CO.12.4-7"]],[["Eph 4:11-13","EPH.4.11-13"]]],"ec":"The varied gifts of Ephesians 4 are common ground, and the CCC maps them onto stable states of life.","f":"In a sentence, what do you sense your own calling within the Body to be?","v":[[934,945,"P2A"]]},{"n":44,"t":"Communion of Saints I: Holy Things","s":946,"e":953,"c":"946–953","ph":2,"k":"The 'communion of saints' means sharing in holy things (faith, sacraments, charisms, goods) and the communion of holy people. No act of charity or sin is purely private.","w":"koinōnia (κοινωνία), 'communion, sharing'. In Latin, sancta (holy things) and sancti (holy people).","sc":[[["Acts 2:42-44","ACT.2.42-44"]],[["1 Cor 12:26","1CO.12.26"]]],"ec":"The phrase is in the Apostles' Creed, which Anglicans recite. The Catholic reading includes sharing across death.","f":"Which spiritual gifts have you received through other people's prayers?","v":[[946,953,"P2B"]]},{"n":45,"t":"Communion of Saints II: Heaven and Earth","s":954,"e":962,"c":"954–962","ph":2,"k":"The Church exists in three states: pilgrims on earth, those being purified, and those in glory. The saints intercede for us, and we pray for the dead. Love binds all three together.","w":"nephos martyrōn (νέφος μαρτύρων), 'cloud of witnesses' (Heb 12:1).","sc":[[["Heb 12:1","HEB.12.1"]],[["Rev 5:8","REV.5.8"]],[["2 Macc 12:43-46","2MA.12.43-46"]]],"ec":"Invoking the saints and praying for the dead were rejected by the Reformers (Anglican Article XXII). Prayer for the dead appears in early catacomb inscriptions.","f":"How is asking a saint to pray for you like, and unlike, asking a friend?","v":[[954,962,"P2B"]]},{"n":46,"t":"Mary, Mother of the Church","s":963,"e":970,"c":"963–970","ph":2,"k":"Mary is mother of the members of Christ in the order of grace. Her role never rivals Christ's unique mediation; it flows from it and shows its power.","w":"Mediatrix (Latin), always understood as a participation in the one Mediator (1 Tim 2:5).","sc":[[["John 19:25-27","JHN.19.25-27"]],[["Acts 1:14","ACT.1.14"]],[["Rev 12:1-6","REV.12.1-6"]]],"ec":"This is a major Protestant concern. Paragraph 970 places Mary's role beneath Christ's, the same way any Christian's intercession is.","f":"How does John 19:26-27 speak to you as a disciple whom Christ 'gives' a mother?","v":[[963,970,"P2C"]]},{"n":47,"t":"Devotion to Mary; Icon of Our Hope","s":971,"e":975,"c":"971–975","ph":2,"k":"Veneration of Mary differs in kind from the adoration given to God alone. Taken up body and soul, she is the image of what the whole Church will become.","w":"latria / dulia / hyperdulia (Greek via Latin). Worship for God alone, honor for the saints, and special honor for Mary.","sc":[[["Luke 1:48","LUK.1.48"]],[["Rev 12:1","REV.12.1"]]],"ec":"The latria/dulia distinction is the key to the 'Mary worship' objection.","f":"Where have you seen Marian devotion done well, and where poorly?","v":[[971,975,"P2C"]]},{"n":48,"t":"Forgiveness of Sins; The Power of the Keys","s":976,"e":987,"c":"976–987","ph":2,"k":"Baptism is the first sacrament of forgiveness. For sins after Baptism, Christ gave the apostles and their successors authority to forgive in his name. No sin is too great to be forgiven.","w":"aphesis (ἄφεσις), 'release, letting go'.","sc":[[["John 20:22-23","JHN.20.22-23"]],[["Matt 16:19","MAT.16.19"]],[["18:18","MAT.18.18"]]],"ec":"This is new terrain: forgiveness ministered sacramentally. The BCP includes priestly absolution, but private confession is optional for Anglicans.","f":"Read John 20:22-23 slowly. What exactly is Christ giving the apostles?","v":[[976,976,"P2D"],[977,980,"P2E"],[981,987,"P2F"]]},{"n":49,"t":"The Resurrection of the Body","s":988,"e":1001,"c":"988–1001","ph":3,"k":"God revealed the resurrection progressively, and Christ's own rising is its source. All the dead will rise with real bodies, transformed. How this happens exceeds our imagination.","w":"anastasis (ἀνάστασις), 'standing up again'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 15:12-22","1CO.15.12-22"],["35-44","1CO.15.35-44"]],[["2 Macc 7:9-14","2MA.7.9-14"]],[["Dan 12:2","DAN.12.2"]]],"ec":"This is shared historic faith. The Catholic respect for the body (burial, relics) flows from it.","f":"Why does it matter that we rise with bodies and not as souls only?","v":[[988,991,"P2G"],[992,1001,"P2H"]]},{"n":50,"t":"Dying in Christ","s":1002,"e":1019,"c":"1002–1019","ph":3,"k":"We are already risen with Christ through Baptism. Death ends our earthly pilgrimage: we die once, with no reincarnation. The Church teaches us to prepare for death and to entrust ourselves to God.","w":"koimaomai (κοιμάομαι), 'to fall asleep'. It is the root of 'cemetery' (sleeping place).","sc":[[["Phil 1:21-23","PHP.1.21-23"]],[["Heb 9:27","HEB.9.27"]],[["2 Cor 5:1-8","2CO.5.1-8"]]],"ec":"This is common ground. Catholic practice adds viaticum and prayers for a holy death.","f":"If today were your last, what would you want to have settled?","v":[[1002,1004,"P2H"],[1005,1014,"P2I"],[1015,1019,"P2J"]]},{"n":51,"t":"Particular Judgment and Heaven","s":1020,"e":1029,"c":"1020–1029","ph":3,"k":"At death each person is judged at once and enters heaven (directly or after purification), or damnation. Heaven is perfect communion with the Trinity and the blessed: seeing God face to face.","w":"visio beatifica (Latin), 'beatific vision'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 13:12","1CO.13.12"]],[["1 John 3:2","1JN.3.2"]],[["Rev 22:4","REV.22.4"]]],"ec":"The hope is shared. Catholic teaching specifies the immediate judgment of each person at death.","f":"How does the promise of seeing God face to face shape what you long for?","v":[[1020,1020,"P2K"],[1021,1022,"P2L"],[1023,1029,"P2M"]]},{"n":52,"t":"Purgatory","s":1030,"e":1032,"c":"1030–1032","ph":3,"k":"Those who die in God's grace but are still imperfectly purified are assured of salvation. They undergo a final purification so as to enter heaven's joy. It is not a second chance. The Church has prayed for the dead since ancient times.","w":"purgatorium (Latin), 'place of cleansing'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 3:13-15","1CO.3.13-15"]],[["2 Macc 12:43-46","2MA.12.43-46"]],[["Matt 12:32","MAT.12.32"]]],"ec":"Anglican Article XXII calls purgatory a 'Romish doctrine'. C. S. Lewis, an Anglican, still believed in purgation as a cleansing.","f":"How does purgatory express God's holiness and love, not only his justice?","v":[[1030,1032,"P2N"]]},{"n":53,"t":"Hell","s":1033,"e":1037,"c":"1033–1037","ph":3,"k":"Hell is definitive self-exclusion from communion with God, chosen by refusing love. Scripture's warnings call us to conversion. God predestines no one to hell.","w":"Gê-Hinnōm (גֵּיא הִנֹּם), the 'Valley of Hinnom' outside Jerusalem. It is the root of 'Gehenna'.","sc":[[["Matt 25:31-46","MAT.25.31-46"]],[["Mark 9:43-48","MRK.9.43-48"]],[["2 Pet 3:9","2PE.3.9"]]],"ec":"Paragraph 1037 rejects double predestination, a Calvinist doctrine.","f":"What does it mean that hell is self-exclusion rather than God's preference?","v":[[1033,1037,"P2O"]]},{"n":54,"t":"Last Judgment and New Creation","s":1038,"e":1050,"c":"1038–1050","ph":3,"k":"At Christ's return the whole truth of every life will be revealed. God will make a new heaven and earth, and creation itself will be set free. This hope gives weight to our work now.","w":"parousia (παρουσία), 'arrival, royal visit'.","sc":[[["Matt 25:31-46","MAT.25.31-46"]],[["Rev 21:1-5","REV.21.1-5"]],[["Rom 8:19-23","ROM.8.19-23"]]],"ec":"There is broad agreement. Catholic eschatology emphasizes the renewal of creation, not its abandonment.","f":"How does hope for a renewed creation change how you treat the present world?","v":[[1038,1041,"P2P"],[1042,1050,"P2Q"]]},{"n":55,"t":"In Brief; Amen","s":1051,"e":1065,"c":"1051–1065","ph":3,"k":"This closes the Creed. Christ himself is God's 'Amen', and our Amen is our personal yes to everything we have professed.","w":"amen (אָמֵן), from the root aman, 'firm, faithful, reliable'.","sc":[[["2 Cor 1:20","2CO.1.20"]],[["Rev 3:14","REV.3.14"]],[["Isa 65:16","ISA.65.16"]]],"ec":"Shared by all Christians.","f":"What would it mean to make your whole life an 'Amen' to God?","v":[[1051,1060,"P2R"],[1061,1065,"P2S"]]},{"n":56,"t":"The Annunciation; Mary's Calling","s":484,"e":489,"c":"484–489","ph":3,"k":"Mary was chosen from all eternity and prepared by grace to be the Mother of God's Son. Holy women of Israel prepared her mission.","w":"kecharitōmenē (κεχαριτωμένη), 'having been graced' (Luke 1:28). A perfect passive participle: grace already given and lasting.","sc":[[["Luke 1:26-38","LUK.1.26-38"]],[["Gen 3:15","GEN.3.15"]]],"ec":"Protestants honor the Annunciation. The Catholic reading of kecharitōmenē underlies later Marian doctrine.","f":"How does God's preparation of Mary reflect the way he prepares you?","v":[[484,489,"P1K"]]},{"n":57,"t":"The Immaculate Conception; Mother of God","s":490,"e":495,"c":"490–495","ph":3,"k":"From the first moment of her conception Mary was preserved from original sin, by the merits of Christ. She too is redeemed, in a more exalted way. Because her Son is God, she is truly Mother of God (Ephesus, 431).","w":"Theotokos (Θεοτόκος), 'God-bearer'.","sc":[[["Luke 1:28","LUK.1.28"],["42-43","LUK.1.42-43"]],[["Gal 4:4","GAL.4.4"]]],"ec":"The Reformers accepted 'Theotokos'. The Immaculate Conception (defined 1854) was discussed by Anglican and Catholic theologians in the ARCIC statement 'Mary: Grace and Hope in Christ' (2005).","f":"How does 'redeemed in a more exalted way' keep Christ at the center of this doctrine?","v":[[490,495,"P1K"]]},{"n":58,"t":"Mary's Virginity","s":496,"e":501,"c":"496–501","ph":3,"k":"Jesus was conceived by the Holy Spirit without a human father, a sign that he is God's Son. The Church teaches Mary's perpetual virginity. The 'brothers' of Jesus were close relatives.","w":"adelphos (ἀδελφός), 'brother'. Used for a wide range of kin, as Hebrew 'ach' is.","sc":[[["Matt 1:18-25","MAT.1.18-25"]],[["Mark 6:3","MRK.6.3"]],[["Matt 27:56","MAT.27.56"]]],"ec":"Luther, Calvin and Wesley all held Mary's perpetual virginity. Most later Protestants dropped it.","f":"Why might the Reformers have kept a belief that their descendants later abandoned?","v":[[496,501,"P1K"]]},{"n":59,"t":"Mary in God's Plan: The New Eve","s":502,"e":511,"c":"502–511","ph":3,"k":"Mary's virginity signals the new beginning in Christ and her undivided gift of self. Her 'let it be' undoes Eve's disobedience, so she becomes the 'new Eve'.","w":"fiat (Latin), 'let it be' (Luke 1:38).","sc":[[["Luke 1:38","LUK.1.38"]],[["John 2:1-5","JHN.2.1-5"]],[["Gen 3:15","GEN.3.15"]]],"ec":"The Eve-Mary parallel comes from Irenaeus (2nd century), long before any division in the Church.","f":"Where is God asking for your 'fiat' in this season of your life?","v":[[502,511,"P1K"]]},{"n":60,"r":1,"q":"Which claim about the Church (sacrament, 'subsists in', Peter, infallibility) is your biggest obstacle, and which is your biggest draw?","items":[[31,"Who Belongs to the Church?"],[32,"The Church and Non-Christians"],[33,"'Outside the Church No Salvation'"],[34,"The Church Is Missionary"],[35,"The Church Is Apostolic"],[36,"Why a Hierarchy?"],[37,"Peter and the College of Bishops"],[38,"The Teaching Office and Infallibility"],[39,"Sanctifying and Governing"],[40,"The Lay Faithful"],[41,"Lay Prophets and Kings"],[42,"Consecrated Life"],[43,"In Brief: Hierarchy, Laity, Consecrated"],[44,"Communion of Saints I: Holy Things"],[45,"Communion of Saints II: Heaven and Earth"],[46,"Mary, Mother of the Church"],[47,"Devotion to Mary; Icon of Our Hope"],[48,"Forgiveness of Sins; The Power of the Keys"],[49,"The Resurrection of the Body"],[50,"Dying in Christ"],[51,"Particular Judgment and Heaven"],[52,"Purgatory"],[53,"Hell"],[54,"Last Judgment and New Creation"],[55,"In Brief; Amen"],[56,"The Annunciation; Mary's Calling"],[57,"The Immaculate Conception; Mother of God"],[58,"Mary's Virginity"],[59,"Mary in God's Plan: The New Eve"]]},{"n":61,"t":"Good News: God Has Sent His Son","s":422,"e":429,"c":"422–429","ph":3,"k":"Catechesis has one center: the person of Jesus Christ. Its aim is to bring people into communion with him.","w":"euangelion (εὐαγγέλιον), 'good news'.","sc":[[["Gal 4:4-5","GAL.4.4-5"]],[["Mark 1:1","MRK.1.1"]],[["1 John 1:1-4","1JN.1.1-4"]]],"ec":"A Christ-centered catechesis is common ground across traditions.","f":"If someone asked you who Jesus is, what would you say in three sentences?","v":[[422,429,"P1D"]]},{"n":62,"t":"The Name of Jesus","s":430,"e":435,"c":"430–435","ph":3,"k":"'Jesus' means 'God saves'. His name contains the Divine Name, and it is the heart of Christian prayer.","w":"Yeshua (יֵשׁוּעַ), 'YHWH saves'.","sc":[[["Matt 1:21","MAT.1.21"]],[["Phil 2:9-11","PHP.2.9-11"]],[["Acts 4:12","ACT.4.12"]]],"ec":"Devotion to the Holy Name and the Jesus Prayer are shared with the Orthodox and loved by many evangelicals.","f":"Pray the name of Jesus slowly today. What happens?","v":[[430,435,"P1E"]]},{"n":63,"t":"Christ, the Anointed","s":436,"e":440,"c":"436–440","ph":3,"k":"'Christ' means 'anointed'. Jesus is the Messiah, anointed as priest, prophet and king. He fulfills Israel's hope but reinterprets it through the Cross.","w":"Mashiach (מָשִׁיחַ) / Christos (Χριστός), 'anointed one'.","sc":[[["Luke 4:16-21","LUK.4.16-21"]],[["Isa 61:1","ISA.61.1"]],[["Mark 8:27-33","MRK.8.27-33"]]],"ec":"Shared confession.","f":"What kind of Messiah were you hoping for, and how has Jesus surprised you?","v":[[436,440,"P1F"]]},{"n":64,"t":"The Only Son of God","s":441,"e":445,"c":"441–445","ph":3,"k":"Jesus is the Son of God in a unique sense: eternal, of one being with the Father. Peter confessed it by revelation, and the resurrection displays it.","w":"monogenēs (μονογενής), 'only-begotten, one-of-a-kind'.","sc":[[["John 1:14","JHN.1.14"],["18","JHN.1.18"]],[["Matt 16:16-17","MAT.16.16-17"]],[["Rom 1:3-4","ROM.1.3-4"]]],"ec":"Shared Nicene faith.","f":"How is Jesus' sonship the source of your own adoption?","v":[[441,445,"P1G"]]},{"n":65,"t":"Jesus Is Lord","s":446,"e":455,"c":"446–455","ph":3,"k":"'Lord' (Kyrios) is the Greek translation of God's own name. Calling Jesus 'Lord' confesses his divinity and his rule over our lives.","w":"Kyrios (Κύριος), used in the Greek Old Testament for YHWH.","sc":[[["Phil 2:11","PHP.2.11"]],[["1 Cor 12:3","1CO.12.3"]],[["John 20:28","JHN.20.28"]]],"ec":"Shared confession, and the earliest Christian creed ('Jesus is Lord').","f":"Which area of your life most resists the confession 'Jesus is Lord'?","v":[[446,451,"P1H"],[452,455,"P1I"]]},{"n":66,"t":"Why the Word Became Flesh","s":456,"e":460,"c":"456–460","ph":3,"k":"The Word became flesh to save us, to reveal God's love, to be our model of holiness, and to make us 'partakers of the divine nature'.","w":"theōsis (θέωσις), 'deification'. Sharing in God's life by grace (2 Pet 1:4).","sc":[[["John 3:16","JHN.3.16"]],[["2 Pet 1:4","2PE.1.4"]],[["1 John 4:9-10","1JN.4.9-10"]]],"ec":"Deification is patristic and shared with the Orthodox. It is less familiar in Western Protestantism, though present in Wesley and in Anglicans such as Andrewes.","f":"What does it mean to you that God wants to share his own life, not merely pardon you?","v":[[456,460,"P1J"]]},{"n":67,"t":"The Incarnation","s":461,"e":463,"c":"461–463","ph":3,"k":"The Son assumed a full human nature in order to save us in it. Belief in the Incarnation is the distinctive sign of Christian faith.","w":"kenōsis (κένωσις), 'self-emptying' (Phil 2:7).","sc":[[["Phil 2:5-8","PHP.2.5-8"]],[["Heb 10:5-7","HEB.10.5-7"]],[["1 John 4:2","1JN.4.2"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Meditate on Philippians 2:5-8. Where is God inviting your own self-emptying?","v":[[461,463,"P1J"]]},{"n":68,"t":"True God and True Man","s":464,"e":469,"c":"464–469","ph":3,"k":"Jesus is one divine Person in two natures, divine and human, without confusion or division. Councils from Nicaea to Constantinople III defended this against errors.","w":"homoousios (ὁμοούσιος), 'of the same substance'.","sc":[[["John 1:1","JHN.1.1"],["14","JHN.1.14"]],[["Col 2:9","COL.2.9"]],[["Heb 2:14-17","HEB.2.14-17"]]],"ec":"Chalcedon (451) is accepted by Catholics, Anglicans and most Protestants, including the Brethren.","f":"Which do you instinctively underplay, Jesus' divinity or his humanity?","v":[[464,469,"P1J"]]},{"n":69,"t":"Christ's Human Soul and Knowledge","s":470,"e":474,"c":"470–474","ph":3,"k":"Christ has a real human soul and human knowledge, which grew in wisdom. United to the divine Word, his human knowledge also shared in divine insight.","w":"prokoptō (προκόπτω), 'to advance, grow' (Luke 2:52).","sc":[[["Luke 2:52","LUK.2.52"]],[["Mark 13:32","MRK.13.32"]],[["John 2:25","JHN.2.25"]]],"ec":"A delicate area for all Christologies. The CCC holds real human growth together with divine knowledge.","f":"How does Jesus' real human growth dignify your own slow growth?","v":[[470,474,"P1J"]]},{"n":70,"t":"Christ's Will, Body and Heart","s":475,"e":478,"c":"475–478","ph":3,"k":"Christ has two wills, divine and human, and the human will freely follows the divine. His body makes the invisible God visible. His heart loves each of us personally.","w":"thelēma (θέλημα), 'will' (Constantinople III, 681: two wills).","sc":[[["Luke 22:42","LUK.22.42"]],[["Gal 2:20","GAL.2.20"]],[["John 19:34","JHN.19.34"]]],"ec":"Devotion to the Sacred Heart (478) is distinctively Catholic. Galatians 2:20 ('who loved me') is its root.","f":"Read Galatians 2:20 as a sentence addressed to you personally. What do you notice?","v":[[475,478,"P1J"]]},{"n":71,"t":"In Brief: The Incarnate Word","s":479,"e":483,"c":"479–483","ph":3,"k":"Summary: one divine Person, two natures, truly God and truly man. Jesus is the one Mediator.","w":"hypostasis (ὑπόστασις), 'person, concrete subsistence'.","sc":[[["1 Tim 2:5","1TI.2.5"]],[["John 1:14","JHN.1.14"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Write your own one-paragraph summary of who Jesus is.","v":[[479,483,"P1J"]]},{"n":72,"t":"The Mysteries of Christ's Life","s":512,"e":517,"c":"512–517","ph":3,"k":"Christ's whole life, not only his death, reveals the Father, redeems us, and 'recapitulates' humanity, summing it up and restoring it.","w":"anakephalaiōsis (ἀνακεφαλαίωσις), 'recapitulation, summing up' (Eph 1:10; Irenaeus).","sc":[[["John 14:9","JHN.14.9"]],[["Eph 1:10","EPH.1.10"]],[["2 Cor 8:9","2CO.8.9"]]],"ec":"Recapitulation is patristic and less emphasized in Protestant atonement-centered teaching.","f":"Which ordinary moment in Jesus' life speaks to your ordinary life?","v":[[512,517,"P1L"]]},{"n":73,"t":"Christ Lives His Mysteries in Us","s":518,"e":521,"c":"518–521","ph":3,"k":"Christ lived everything for us, so that we might live it in him. We are called to share in his mysteries.","w":"en Christō (ἐν Χριστῷ), 'in Christ'. Paul's favorite phrase.","sc":[[["Gal 2:20","GAL.2.20"]],[["Rom 6:4","ROM.6.4"]],[["Col 3:3-4","COL.3.3-4"]]],"ec":"This 'in Christ' union is dear to evangelicals too.","f":"Which mystery of Christ's life do you most need to share in this week?","v":[[518,521,"P1L"]]},{"n":74,"t":"Preparations: John the Baptist","s":522,"e":524,"c":"522–524","ph":3,"k":"God prepared for the Incarnation for centuries. John the Baptist is the last and greatest of the prophets. Each Advent the Church relives that expectation.","w":"mal'akh (מַלְאָךְ), 'messenger' (Mal 3:1).","sc":[[["Mal 3:1","MAL.3.1"]],[["Luke 1:76-79","LUK.1.76-79"]],[["John 3:30","JHN.3.30"]]],"ec":"Liturgical churches keep Advent. Brethren assemblies often do not observe the Church year.","f":"What needs to 'decrease' so that Christ may increase?","v":[[522,524,"P1L"]]},{"n":75,"t":"The Christmas Mystery","s":525,"e":526,"c":"525–526","ph":3,"k":"God's Son is born poor in a stable. To enter the Kingdom we must become like children, and this 'wondrous exchange' (God becomes man so that we might share his divinity) is the heart of Christmas.","w":"Immanuel (עִמָּנוּאֵל), 'God with us'.","sc":[[["Luke 2:1-20","LUK.2.1-20"]],[["Isa 9:6","ISA.9.6"]],[["Matt 1:23","MAT.1.23"]]],"ec":"Shared joy.","f":"What would it look like to receive Christ today with a child's simplicity?","v":[[525,526,"P1L"]]},{"n":76,"t":"Circumcision, Epiphany, Presentation, Nazareth","s":527,"e":534,"c":"527–534","ph":4,"k":"Jesus is circumcised into Israel, manifested to the nations (the Magi), presented in the Temple, flees into Egypt, and lives a hidden, obedient life at Nazareth that sanctifies ordinary life.","w":"epiphaneia (ἐπιφάνεια), 'manifestation, appearing'.","sc":[[["Matt 2:1-12","MAT.2.1-12"]],[["Luke 2:22-40","LUK.2.22-40"],["51-52","LUK.2.51-52"]]],"ec":"The Epiphany is shared. The hidden years of Nazareth give a theology of ordinary daily life.","f":"What does Nazareth say about the value of your unseen, everyday faithfulness?","v":[[527,534,"P1L"]]},{"n":77,"t":"Baptism and Temptation of Jesus","s":535,"e":540,"c":"535–540","ph":4,"k":"In his baptism Jesus accepts his mission as the Suffering Servant, and the Trinity is revealed. In the desert he conquers the tempter where Israel (and Adam) failed.","w":"baptizō (βαπτίζω), 'to immerse, plunge'.","sc":[[["Matt 3:13-17","MAT.3.13-17"]],[["Matt 4:1-11","MAT.4.1-11"]],[["Heb 4:15","HEB.4.15"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Which of Jesus' three temptations most resembles your own?","v":[[535,540,"P1L"]]},{"n":78,"t":"The Kingdom of God and Its Signs","s":541,"e":550,"c":"541–550","ph":4,"k":"Jesus inaugurates the Kingdom and invites all, especially sinners and the poor. His miracles are signs that the Kingdom has come and that Satan is defeated.","w":"basileia tou theou (βασιλεία τοῦ θεοῦ), 'reign of God'.","sc":[[["Mark 1:15","MRK.1.15"]],[["Matt 11:2-6","MAT.11.2-6"]],[["Luke 11:20","LUK.11.20"]]],"ec":"Charismatic Christians will feel at home with signs and wonders as Kingdom signs.","f":"Where have you seen signs of God's reign breaking in?","v":[[541,550,"P1L"]]},{"n":79,"t":"The Keys; The Transfiguration","s":551,"e":556,"c":"551–556","ph":4,"k":"Jesus chooses the Twelve, gives Peter the keys, and is transfigured as a foretaste of the Kingdom and of his glory beyond the Cross.","w":"metamorphoō (μεταμορφόω), 'to transform'.","sc":[[["Matt 16:18-19","MAT.16.18-19"]],[["Mark 9:2-8","MRK.9.2-8"]],[["2 Pet 1:16-18","2PE.1.16-18"]]],"ec":"The keys are disputed (see CCC 880-887); the Transfiguration is shared.","f":"What glimpse of glory has strengthened you for a hard road?","v":[[551,556,"P1L"]]},{"n":80,"t":"Jesus Goes Up to Jerusalem","s":557,"e":570,"c":"557–570","ph":4,"k":"Jesus resolutely sets out for Jerusalem and enters as a humble, messianic king. The Church relives this each Palm Sunday.","w":"Hosanna (הוֹשִׁיעָה נָּא), 'save, we pray' (Ps 118:25).","sc":[[["Luke 9:51","LUK.9.51"]],[["Mark 11:1-10","MRK.11.1-10"]],[["Ps 118:25-26","PSA.118.25-26"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where must you 'set your face' resolutely toward God's will?","v":[[557,570,"P1L"]]},{"n":81,"t":"Jesus, Israel and the Law","s":571,"e":582,"c":"571–582","ph":4,"k":"The Paschal Mystery is at the center of the Gospel. Jesus did not abolish the Law but fulfilled it perfectly, which led to conflict with some religious authorities.","w":"Torah (תּוֹרָה), 'instruction', more than 'law'.","sc":[[["Matt 5:17-19","MAT.5.17-19"]],[["Gal 4:4-5","GAL.4.4-5"]]],"ec":"This is relevant to Law and Gospel debates. Catholic teaching stresses fulfillment rather than opposition.","f":"How does Jesus fulfill the Law, rather than abolish it, in your own life?","v":[[571,573,"P1M"],[574,582,"P1N"]]},{"n":82,"t":"Jesus, the Temple and Israel's Faith","s":583,"e":594,"c":"583–594","ph":4,"k":"Jesus revered the Temple yet identified himself with it. His claim to forgive sins was a scandal because it was a claim to divinity.","w":"naos (ναός), 'sanctuary'. Jesus calls his body the temple (John 2:21).","sc":[[["John 2:19-21","JHN.2.19-21"]],[["Mark 2:7","MRK.2.7"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What does it mean that Jesus is now the place where God dwells?","v":[[583,594,"P1N"]]},{"n":83,"t":"Who Is Responsible for Jesus' Death?","s":595,"e":598,"c":"595–598","ph":4,"k":"Jews as a people, then or now, are not collectively responsible for Christ's death. All sinners are its authors, and Christians most of all when they betray him.","w":"hyper hēmōn (ὑπὲρ ἡμῶν), 'for us'.","sc":[[["Luke 23:34","LUK.23.34"]],[["Acts 3:17-18","ACT.3.17-18"]],[["Heb 6:6","HEB.6.6"]]],"ec":"There is a shared repudiation of antisemitism. Paragraph 598 cites the 16th-century Roman Catechism.","f":"How does it change Good Friday to say 'my sins' rather than 'their crime'?","v":[[595,598,"P1O"]]},{"n":84,"t":"Christ's Death in God's Plan","s":599,"e":605,"c":"599–605","ph":4,"k":"Jesus' death was no accident: God permitted human freedom while fulfilling his plan of love foretold in the Suffering Servant. Christ died for all people without exception.","w":"paradidōmi (παραδίδωμι), 'to hand over'. It is used of Judas, of the Father and of Jesus himself.","sc":[[["1 Cor 15:3","1CO.15.3"]],[["Isa 53","ISA.53"]],[["Rom 5:8","ROM.5.8"]]],"ec":"Paragraph 605 explicitly rejects limited atonement (the 'L' of Calvinist TULIP).","f":"What does 'no one is excluded' (605) mean for how you see others?","v":[[599,605,"P1O"]]},{"n":85,"t":"Christ Offered Himself","s":606,"e":612,"c":"606–612","ph":4,"k":"Jesus freely embraced the Father's will. At the Last Supper he anticipated his sacrifice, and in Gethsemane he accepted the cup.","w":"potērion (ποτήριον), 'cup'. It is an image of destiny and of God's judgment.","sc":[[["John 10:17-18","JHN.10.17-18"]],[["Luke 22:19-20","LUK.22.19-20"]],[["Mark 14:36","MRK.14.36"]]],"ec":"Shared. Notice the link between the Supper and the Cross; it matters later for the Eucharist.","f":"What 'cup' are you being asked to accept?","v":[[606,612,"P1O"]]},{"n":86,"t":"The Sacrifice of the Cross","s":613,"e":618,"c":"613–618","ph":4,"k":"Christ's death is the Passover sacrifice and the sacrifice of the New Covenant. His loving obedience makes satisfaction for our sins. He invites us to share in his cross.","w":"hilastērion (ἱλαστήριον), 'mercy seat, place of atonement' (Rom 3:25).","sc":[[["Rom 3:24-25","ROM.3.24-25"]],[["Isa 53:10-12","ISA.53.10-12"]],[["1 Pet 2:21","1PE.2.21"]]],"ec":"Catholic 'satisfaction' (Anselm, Aquinas) emphasizes loving obedience. Penal substitution, common among evangelicals, emphasizes punishment borne. Paragraph 615 holds substitution in a satisfaction frame.","f":"Paragraph 618 says we can share in Christ's sacrifice. How?","v":[[613,618,"P1O"]]},{"n":87,"t":"Christ Was Buried","s":619,"e":630,"c":"619–630","ph":4,"k":"Christ truly died and his body rested in the tomb without corruption. Holy Saturday is the 'Sabbath rest' of God, and Baptism buries us with him.","w":"Shabbat (שַׁבָּת), 'rest'.","sc":[[["Rom 6:4","ROM.6.4"]],[["Acts 2:27","ACT.2.27"]],[["Ps 16:10","PSA.16.10"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What part of your old self needs burying with Christ?","v":[[619,623,"P1O"],[624,630,"P1P"]]},{"n":88,"t":"He Descended into Hell","s":631,"e":637,"c":"631–637","ph":4,"k":"Christ went down to the realm of the dead (Sheol), not to free the damned but to open heaven to the righteous who had died before him.","w":"Sheol (שְׁאוֹל) / Hadēs (ᾅδης), 'abode of the dead'.","sc":[[["1 Pet 3:18-19","1PE.3.18-19"]],[["4:6","1PE.4.6"]],[["Eph 4:9","EPH.4.9"]]],"ec":"Anglican Article III affirms the descent. Some evangelical churches omit the line from the Apostles' Creed.","f":"Christ reaches even the depths. Where do you need to hear that?","v":[[631,631,"P1Q"],[632,637,"P1R"]]},{"n":89,"t":"The Resurrection: A Historical Event","s":638,"e":644,"c":"638–644","ph":4,"k":"The empty tomb and the appearances are real events in history. The apostles were not credulous; they had to be convinced.","w":"ōphthē (ὤφθη), 'he appeared, was seen' (1 Cor 15:5-8).","sc":[[["1 Cor 15:3-8","1CO.15.3-8"]],[["Luke 24:36-43","LUK.24.36-43"]],[["John 20:24-29","JHN.20.24-29"]]],"ec":"Shared core of the faith.","f":"What evidence for the resurrection matters most to you?","v":[[638,644,"P1S"]]},{"n":90,"r":1,"q":"How has seeing Christ's whole life, and not only the Cross, as saving changed the way you pray?","items":[[61,"Good News: God Has Sent His Son"],[62,"The Name of Jesus"],[63,"Christ, the Anointed"],[64,"The Only Son of God"],[65,"Jesus Is Lord"],[66,"Why the Word Became Flesh"],[67,"The Incarnation"],[68,"True God and True Man"],[69,"Christ's Human Soul and Knowledge"],[70,"Christ's Will, Body and Heart"],[71,"In Brief: The Incarnate Word"],[72,"The Mysteries of Christ's Life"],[73,"Christ Lives His Mysteries in Us"],[74,"Preparations: John the Baptist"],[75,"The Christmas Mystery"],[76,"Circumcision, Epiphany, Presentation, Nazareth"],[77,"Baptism and Temptation of Jesus"],[78,"The Kingdom of God and Its Signs"],[79,"The Keys; The Transfiguration"],[80,"Jesus Goes Up to Jerusalem"],[81,"Jesus, Israel and the Law"],[82,"Jesus, the Temple and Israel's Faith"],[83,"Who Is Responsible for Jesus' Death?"],[84,"Christ's Death in God's Plan"],[85,"Christ Offered Himself"],[86,"The Sacrifice of the Cross"],[87,"Christ Was Buried"],[88,"He Descended into Hell"],[89,"The Resurrection: A Historical Event"]]},{"n":91,"t":"The Risen Body and Its Meaning","s":645,"e":655,"c":"645–655","ph":4,"k":"Christ's risen body is real but glorified. The resurrection confirms his divinity, fulfills the promises, and is the source of our justification and future resurrection.","w":"sōma pneumatikon (σῶμα πνευματικόν), 'spiritual body' (1 Cor 15:44).","sc":[[["1 Cor 15:44","1CO.15.44"]],[["Rom 4:25","ROM.4.25"]],[["Rom 6:4","ROM.6.4"]]],"ec":"Romans 4:25 links resurrection and justification, which is less stressed in some Protestant teaching.","f":"How does the resurrection, not only the cross, give you new life?","v":[[645,655,"P1S"]]},{"n":92,"t":"The Ascension","s":656,"e":667,"c":"656–667","ph":4,"k":"Christ ascends with his humanity into glory, opening heaven to us. He reigns at the Father's right hand and intercedes for us forever.","w":"sedet ad dexteram (Latin), 'he sits at the right hand'. It means shared rule.","sc":[[["Acts 1:9-11","ACT.1.9-11"]],[["Heb 7:25","HEB.7.25"]],[["Eph 4:8-10","EPH.4.8-10"]]],"ec":"Christ's heavenly intercession (Heb 7:25) is common ground.","f":"Christ is praying for you right now. How does that change today?","v":[[656,658,"P1S"],[659,664,"P1T"],[665,667,"P1U"]]},{"n":93,"t":"He Will Come to Judge","s":668,"e":682,"c":"668–682","ph":4,"k":"Christ already reigns through the Church. Before his return the Church will pass through a final trial. The Kingdom will not come through a political triumph within history but at his return, when he will judge.","w":"Maranatha (מָרַנָא תָא, Aramaic), 'Our Lord, come!' (1 Cor 16:22).","sc":[[["1 Cor 16:22","1CO.16.22"]],[["Rev 22:20","REV.22.20"]],[["2 Thess 2:3-12","2TH.2.3-12"]]],"ec":"Paragraph 676 rejects 'millenarianism'. Catholic teaching does not adopt the dispensational rapture schemes popularized by J. N. Darby, a founder of the Plymouth Brethren.","f":"How does 'Maranatha' shape your hopes and fears about the future?","v":[[668,677,"P1V"],[678,679,"P1W"],[680,682,"P1X"]]},{"n":94,"t":"Knowing the Holy Spirit","s":683,"e":690,"c":"683–690","ph":4,"k":"No one can say 'Jesus is Lord' except by the Spirit. We know the Spirit through Scripture, Tradition, liturgy, charisms, prayer and mission. The Son and the Spirit are sent together.","w":"Paraklētos (Παράκλητος), 'advocate, one called alongside'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 12:3","1CO.12.3"]],[["John 14:16-17","JHN.14.16-17"],["26","JHN.14.26"]],[["Gal 4:6","GAL.4.6"]]],"ec":"Pentecostal and charismatic Christians have much to share here. The CCC adds that the Spirit is known through the sacraments and the liturgy.","f":"Where have you most clearly known the Spirit's presence?","v":[[683,686,"P1Y"],[687,688,"P1Z"],[689,690,"P20"]]},{"n":95,"t":"Names and Symbols of the Spirit","s":691,"e":701,"c":"691–701","ph":4,"k":"The Spirit is called Paraclete, Spirit of Truth, Spirit of promise. Symbols include water, anointing, fire, cloud, seal, hand, finger and dove.","w":"ruach (רוּחַ) / pneuma (πνεῦμα), 'breath, wind, spirit'.","sc":[[["John 3:5-8","JHN.3.5-8"]],[["Acts 2:3","ACT.2.3"]],[["Gen 1:2","GEN.1.2"]]],"ec":"Shared biblical imagery. Note how many symbols become sacramental signs (water, oil, laying on of hands).","f":"Which symbol of the Spirit best describes his work in your life?","v":[[691,701,"P21"]]},{"n":96,"t":"The Spirit in the Time of the Promises","s":702,"e":716,"c":"702–716","ph":4,"k":"The Spirit was at work from creation, through the promises, theophanies, Law, exile and prophets, preparing for the Messiah.","w":"ruach Elohim (רוּחַ אֱלֹהִים), 'Spirit of God' hovering over the waters (Gen 1:2).","sc":[[["Gen 1:2","GEN.1.2"]],[["Ezek 36:25-27","EZK.36.25-27"]],[["Isa 11:1-2","ISA.11.1-2"]],[["Joel 2:28-29","JOL.2.28-29"]]],"ec":"Shared reading of the Old Testament.","f":"How does seeing the Spirit throughout the Old Testament change how you read it?","v":[[702,716,"P22"]]},{"n":97,"t":"The Spirit, John the Baptist and Mary","s":717,"e":726,"c":"717–726","ph":4,"k":"The Spirit fills John the Baptist as forerunner. In Mary the Spirit completes the Father's preparations and overshadows her to conceive the Son.","w":"episkiazō (ἐπισκιάζω), 'to overshadow', recalling the cloud over the Tabernacle.","sc":[[["Luke 1:15-17","LUK.1.15-17"],["35","LUK.1.35"]],[["John 1:33","JHN.1.33"]]],"ec":"The overshadowing echoes Exodus 40:35, a link Catholics read as Mary being the new Ark.","f":"How does Mary's receptivity to the Spirit model your own?","v":[[717,726,"P23"]]},{"n":98,"t":"Christ Anointed by the Spirit","s":727,"e":730,"c":"727–730","ph":4,"k":"Jesus is anointed by the Spirit and promises to send him. Glorified, he breathes the Spirit on the apostles.","w":"chriō (χρίω), 'to anoint'.","sc":[[["Luke 4:18","LUK.4.18"]],[["John 7:39","JHN.7.39"]],[["John 20:22","JHN.20.22"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What would change if you lived as one sharing in Christ's anointing?","v":[[727,730,"P23"]]},{"n":99,"t":"Pentecost and the Spirit in the Church","s":731,"e":741,"c":"731–741","ph":4,"k":"At Pentecost the Spirit is poured out, and the Church is manifested. The Spirit's love is poured into our hearts, he prays in us, and his gifts build up the Body. He is the pledge of our inheritance.","w":"arrabōn (ἀρραβών), 'down payment, pledge' (2 Cor 1:22).","sc":[[["Acts 2:1-21","ACT.2.1-21"]],[["Rom 5:5","ROM.5.5"]],[["Rom 8:26","ROM.8.26"]],[["2 Cor 1:22","2CO.1.22"]]],"ec":"This is where Pentecostal emphasis meets Catholic sacramental life: the Spirit's gifts within the Body.","f":"Where do you need the Spirit to 'pray in you' when you lack words?","v":[[731,741,"P24"]]},{"n":100,"t":"In Brief: The Holy Spirit","s":742,"e":747,"c":"742–747","ph":4,"k":"Summary: the Spirit is Lord and Giver of life, sent by Father and Son, the soul of the Church.","w":"zōopoios (ζωοποιός), 'life-giving' (Nicene Creed).","sc":[[["Gal 4:6","GAL.4.6"]],[["Rom 8:9-11","ROM.8.9-11"]]],"ec":"Shared Nicene faith (the filioque question comes later).","f":"Pray 'Come, Holy Spirit' three times today, slowly.","v":[[742,747,"P25"]]},{"n":101,"t":"Moral Law and Natural Law","s":1949,"e":1960,"c":"1949–1960","ph":4,"k":"God's law is fatherly instruction. Natural law, the light of reason written on the heart, is universal, unchanging, and the basis of moral and civil law.","w":"lex naturalis (Latin), 'natural law'.","sc":[[["Rom 2:14-15","ROM.2.14-15"]],[["Ps 19:7-11","PSA.19.7-11"]]],"ec":"Natural law runs from Aquinas to the Anglican Richard Hooker. Some Reformed and Lutheran theology is more wary of it.","f":"What moral truths do you think every human being can know?","v":[[1949,1949,"P6S"],[1950,1953,"P6T"],[1954,1960,"P6U"]]},{"n":102,"t":"The Old Law and the New Law","s":1961,"e":1974,"c":"1961–1974","ph":4,"k":"The Old Law is holy and prepared for the Gospel. The New Law is the grace of the Holy Spirit given through faith in Christ, expressed in the Sermon on the Mount.","w":"lex nova (Latin), 'new law'. Above all, the Spirit's grace (1966).","sc":[[["Matt 5-7","MAT.5"]],[["Jer 31:33","JER.31.33"]],[["Rom 8:2","ROM.8.2"]]],"ec":"Luther contrasted Law and Gospel. For Catholics the New Law is grace itself, working from within.","f":"How is the Sermon on the Mount a gift and not only a demand?","v":[[1961,1964,"P6V"],[1965,1974,"P6W"]]},{"n":103,"t":"In Brief: The Moral Law","s":1975,"e":1986,"c":"1975–1986","ph":4,"k":"Summary: natural law, the Old Law, and the New Law as the perfection of the divine law. This is a lighter day for review.","w":"nomos (νόμος), 'law'.","sc":[[["Gal 5:13-14","GAL.5.13-14"]],[["Rom 13:10","ROM.13.10"]]],"ec":"Shared emphasis on love as fulfilling the law.","f":"Where does love fulfill a commandment you find hard?","v":[[1975,1986,"P6X"]]},{"n":104,"t":"Justification: Cleansing and New Life","s":1987,"e":1990,"c":"1987–1990","ph":4,"k":"Justification cleanses us from sin and communicates God's righteousness through faith in Christ and Baptism. It begins with conversion, separating us from sin and reconciling us with God.","w":"dikaiosynē (δικαιοσύνη), 'righteousness, justice'. dikaioō, 'to justify, make or declare righteous'.","sc":[[["Rom 3:21-26","ROM.3.21-26"]],[["Rom 6:3-4","ROM.6.3-4"]],[["Matt 4:17","MAT.4.17"]]],"ec":"The 1999 Catholic-Lutheran Joint Declaration on Justification (later affirmed by Methodists and Anglicans) agrees that we are justified by grace alone, through faith in Christ, not by merit. The debate over imputed versus infused righteousness remains.","f":"In your own words: how is a person put right with God?","v":[[1987,1990,"P6Y"]]},{"n":105,"t":"Justification: Faith, Hope, Love and Freedom","s":1991,"e":1995,"c":"1991–1995","ph":4,"k":"In justification, faith, hope and charity are poured into our hearts. It is merited by Christ's Passion, given in Baptism, and sets up a cooperation between God's grace and human freedom. Augustine held it to be a greater work than creation.","w":"synergos (συνεργός), 'co-worker' (1 Cor 3:9).","sc":[[["Phil 2:12-13","PHP.2.12-13"]],[["1 Cor 3:9","1CO.3.9"]],[["Rom 5:5","ROM.5.5"]]],"ec":"Trent rejected 'faith alone' when it means faith without love. James 2:24 is the only biblical use of 'faith alone', and it is negated there.","f":"How do you hold together 'all is grace' and 'work out your salvation'?","v":[[1991,1995,"P6Y"]]},{"n":106,"t":"Grace","s":1996,"e":2005,"c":"1996–2005","ph":4,"k":"Grace is God's free gift, a share in his life. Sanctifying grace is a lasting gift that heals and divinizes; actual graces are God's interventions moment by moment. God's initiative always comes first. We judge our state by its fruits, not by feelings.","w":"charis (χάρις), 'gift, favor'.","sc":[[["Eph 2:8-10","EPH.2.8-10"]],[["John 1:16","JHN.1.16"]],[["2 Pet 1:3-4","2PE.1.3-4"]]],"ec":"Paragraph 2005 differs from evangelical 'assurance': Catholics speak of confident hope, not certainty from feelings.","f":"What fruits in your life point to God's grace at work?","v":[[1996,2005,"P6Z"]]},{"n":107,"t":"Merit","s":2006,"e":2011,"c":"2006–2011","ph":4,"k":"Before God we have no strict right to anything. Any 'merit' comes from his free decision to associate us with his grace, so God crowns his own gifts. No one can merit the first grace.","w":"misthos (μισθός), 'reward, wages'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 15:10","1CO.15.10"]],[["Matt 5:12","MAT.5.12"]],[["2 Tim 4:8","2TI.4.8"]]],"ec":"Protestants fear 'merit' means earning. Paragraphs 2007-2011 say merit is itself a fruit of grace.","f":"How does 'God crowns his own gifts' (Augustine) change how you hear 'merit'?","v":[[2006,2011,"P70"]]},{"n":108,"t":"Christian Holiness","s":2012,"e":2016,"c":"2012–2016","ph":4,"k":"All are called to holiness, the fullness of Christian life. The path goes through the Cross. We hope for final perseverance as a gift.","w":"teleios (τέλειος), 'complete, mature' (Matt 5:48).","sc":[[["Matt 5:48","MAT.5.48"]],[["Rom 8:28-30","ROM.8.28-30"]],[["Phil 3:12-14","PHP.3.12-14"]]],"ec":"Wesley's teaching on Christian perfection is a near relative. 'Eternal security' differs from paragraph 2016.","f":"What would holiness look like in your ordinary week?","v":[[2012,2016,"P71"]]},{"n":109,"t":"In Brief: Grace and Justification","s":2017,"e":2029,"c":"2017–2029","ph":4,"k":"Summary of justification, grace and merit. Use today to consolidate.","w":"gratia gratis data (Latin), 'grace freely given'.","sc":[[["Rom 3:22-24","ROM.3.22-24"]],[["Titus 3:4-7","TIT.3.4-7"]]],"ec":"Compare your understanding with the Joint Declaration of 1999.","f":"Write one sentence on how a Catholic is saved, then check it against 1987-1995.","v":[[2017,2029,"P72"]]},{"n":110,"t":"The Church, Mother and Teacher","s":2030,"e":2040,"c":"2030–2040","ph":4,"k":"The Christian moral life is lived in the Church. The Magisterium teaches on morals, including natural law, and moral conscience is formed with its help.","w":"mater et magistra (Latin), 'mother and teacher'.","sc":[[["1 Tim 3:15","1TI.3.15"]],[["Rom 12:2","ROM.12.2"]]],"ec":"This is new terrain: the Church's teaching authority extends to morality, not only doctrine.","f":"Where do you most need the Church's guidance in moral questions?","v":[[2030,2031,"P73"],[2032,2040,"P74"]]},{"n":111,"t":"Precepts of the Church; Witness","s":2041,"e":2051,"c":"2041–2051","ph":4,"k":"There are five minimal precepts: attend Mass on Sundays and holy days; confess at least once a year; receive Communion at least during the Easter season; keep the days of fasting and abstinence; and support the Church's needs. A moral life is itself evangelization.","w":"praeceptum (Latin), 'instruction, command'.","sc":[[["Heb 10:25","HEB.10.25"]],[["Matt 5:16","MAT.5.16"]]],"ec":"The Brethren practice weekly breaking of bread, a close parallel to the Sunday obligation.","f":"Which precept will require the biggest change in your routine?","v":[[2041,2043,"P75"],[2044,2046,"P76"],[2047,2051,"P77"]]},{"n":112,"t":"Conscience","s":1776,"e":1782,"c":"1776–1782","ph":4,"k":"Conscience is the voice of God in the heart's inner sanctuary. It judges our acts in light of the moral law, and we must follow it.","w":"syneidēsis (συνείδησις), 'co-knowledge'.","sc":[[["Rom 2:14-16","ROM.2.14-16"]],[["2 Cor 1:12","2CO.1.12"]]],"ec":"Paragraph 1778 quotes Newman, an Anglican convert, calling conscience the 'aboriginal Vicar of Christ'.","f":"When did your conscience last speak clearly? Did you listen?","v":[[1776,1776,"P5Y"],[1777,1782,"P5Z"]]},{"n":113,"t":"Forming and Following Conscience","s":1783,"e":1789,"c":"1783–1789","ph":4,"k":"Conscience must be educated by the Word, the Church's teaching, prayer and examination. Rules: never do evil that good may come, follow the Golden Rule, and respect others' consciences.","w":"examen (Latin), 'examination'. A daily review of conscience.","sc":[[["Ps 139:23-24","PSA.139.23-24"]],[["Rom 3:8","ROM.3.8"]],[["Matt 7:12","MAT.7.12"]]],"ec":"The Ignatian examen is a great preparation for a first Confession.","f":"Begin a nightly two-minute examen this week.","v":[[1783,1785,"P60"],[1786,1789,"P61"]]},{"n":114,"t":"Erroneous Conscience","s":1790,"e":1802,"c":"1790–1802","ph":4,"k":"Conscience can err. When ignorance is our own fault, we are responsible; when it is invincible, the evil is not imputed, but it is still evil. Conversion clarifies conscience.","w":"ignorantia invincibilis (Latin), 'unavoidable ignorance'.","sc":[[["1 Tim 1:5","1TI.1.5"]],[["1 John 3:19-21","1JN.3.19-21"]]],"ec":"Shared moral reasoning.","f":"Where might your conscience need correction rather than confirmation?","v":[[1790,1794,"P62"],[1795,1802,"P63"]]},{"n":115,"t":"Mercy and Sin","s":1846,"e":1853,"c":"1846–1853","ph":4,"k":"The Gospel reveals mercy, and receiving it requires admitting our faults. Sin is an offense against God and against reason, love and truth.","w":"chata (חָטָא) / hamartia (ἁμαρτία), 'to miss the mark'.","sc":[[["Rom 5:20","ROM.5.20"]],[["1 John 1:8-9","1JN.1.8-9"]],[["Gal 5:19-21","GAL.5.19-21"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Why is admitting sin the doorway to mercy rather than to despair?","v":[[1846,1848,"P69"],[1849,1851,"P6A"],[1852,1853,"P6B"]]},{"n":116,"t":"Mortal and Venial Sin","s":1854,"e":1864,"c":"1854–1864","ph":4,"k":"Mortal sin destroys charity and requires grave matter, full knowledge and deliberate consent. Venial sin weakens charity. Both are real, but they differ in gravity.","w":"peccatum mortale (Latin), 'deadly sin'. Compare 1 John 5:16-17.","sc":[[["1 John 5:16-17","1JN.5.16-17"]],[["Matt 12:31-32","MAT.12.31-32"]],[["Gal 5:19-21","GAL.5.19-21"]]],"ec":"Many Protestants say 'all sin is sin'. The distinction rests on 1 John 5:16-17 and matters for Confession.","f":"Learn the three conditions for mortal sin. Why does each matter?","v":[[1854,1864,"P6C"]]},{"n":117,"t":"Capital Sins; Social Sin","s":1865,"e":1876,"c":"1865–1876","ph":4,"k":"Sin breeds sin. The capital sins are pride, avarice, envy, wrath, lust, gluttony and sloth. We share responsibility when we cooperate in others' sins, and sin can build 'structures of sin' in society.","w":"akēdia (ἀκηδία), 'sloth, spiritual listlessness'.","sc":[[["Gen 4:10","GEN.4.10"]],[["Jas 5:4","JAS.5.4"]],[["Rom 1:28-32","ROM.1.28-32"]]],"ec":"Shared tradition (the seven deadly sins predate the Reformation).","f":"Which capital sin is your most persistent root?","v":[[1865,1869,"P6D"],[1870,1876,"P6E"]]},{"n":118,"t":"What Is Liturgy?","s":1066,"e":1076,"c":"1066–1076","ph":5,"k":"Liturgy is the Church's participation in the work of God: Christ continuing his redemption through his Church. It is the source of the Church's life, though not its only activity.","w":"leitourgia (λειτουργία), 'public work, service on behalf of the people'.","sc":[[["Heb 8:1-2","HEB.8.1-2"]],[["Acts 13:2","ACT.13.2"]]],"ec":"Anglicans share a liturgical heritage. Brethren worship is non-liturgical in form, though it does follow a fixed pattern.","f":"How might seeing worship as God's work, not only ours, change how you pray at Mass?","v":[[1066,1075,"P2T"],[1076,1076,"P2U"]]},{"n":119,"t":"The Father: Source and Goal of Liturgy","s":1077,"e":1083,"c":"1077–1083","ph":5,"k":"All liturgy flows from the Father's blessing and returns to him in praise and thanksgiving, in the Son and through the Spirit.","w":"berakah (בְּרָכָה) / eulogia (εὐλογία), 'blessing'.","sc":[[["Eph 1:3-14","EPH.1.3-14"]],[["Ps 103:1-5","PSA.103.1-5"]]],"ec":"Shared biblical theology of blessing.","f":"Read Ephesians 1:3-14 as a prayer. Which blessing do you need to receive today?","v":[[1077,1083,"P2V"]]},{"n":120,"r":1,"q":"Summarize the Catholic teaching on justification in three sentences, then check them against CCC 1987-1995.","items":[[91,"The Risen Body and Its Meaning"],[92,"The Ascension"],[93,"He Will Come to Judge"],[94,"Knowing the Holy Spirit"],[95,"Names and Symbols of the Spirit"],[96,"The Spirit in the Time of the Promises"],[97,"The Spirit, John the Baptist and Mary"],[98,"Christ Anointed by the Spirit"],[99,"Pentecost and the Spirit in the Church"],[100,"In Brief: The Holy Spirit"],[101,"Moral Law and Natural Law"],[102,"The Old Law and the New Law"],[103,"In Brief: The Moral Law"],[104,"Justification: Cleansing and New Life"],[105,"Justification: Faith, Hope, Love and Freedom"],[106,"Grace"],[107,"Merit"],[108,"Christian Holiness"],[109,"In Brief: Grace and Justification"],[110,"The Church, Mother and Teacher"],[111,"Precepts of the Church; Witness"],[112,"Conscience"],[113,"Forming and Following Conscience"],[114,"Erroneous Conscience"],[115,"Mercy and Sin"],[116,"Mortal and Venial Sin"],[117,"Capital Sins; Social Sin"],[118,"What Is Liturgy?"],[119,"The Father: Source and Goal of Liturgy"]]},{"n":121,"t":"Christ's Work in the Liturgy","s":1084,"e":1090,"c":"1084–1090","ph":5,"k":"The risen Christ acts through the sacraments. His Paschal Mystery happened once in history, yet it does not pass away; it is made present.","w":"ephapax (ἐφάπαξ), 'once for all' (Heb 7:27; 9:12).","sc":[[["Heb 9:11-12","HEB.9.11-12"],["24-28","HEB.9.24-28"]],[["Matt 18:20","MAT.18.20"]]],"ec":"Catholics agree that Christ's sacrifice is once for all. The liturgy makes it present; it does not repeat it (1085).","f":"How can something that happened once be truly present now?","v":[[1084,1090,"P2W"]]},{"n":122,"t":"The Spirit Prepares Us","s":1091,"e":1098,"c":"1091–1098","ph":5,"k":"The Spirit prepares the assembly to meet Christ, using Old Testament types and the Jewish liturgical heritage.","w":"typos (τύπος), 'pattern, prefiguration' (1 Cor 10:6).","sc":[[["1 Cor 10:1-6","1CO.10.1-6"]],[["Joel 2:12-18","JOL.2.12-18"]]],"ec":"Brethren typology fits well here.","f":"What do you want the Spirit to prepare in you this Lent?","v":[[1091,1098,"P2X"]]},{"n":123,"t":"The Spirit Recalls the Mystery","s":1099,"e":1103,"c":"1099–1103","ph":5,"k":"In the liturgy the Spirit 'remembers' Christ's saving acts. This memorial makes them present, not just recalled.","w":"anamnēsis (ἀνάμνησις), 'remembrance' (Luke 22:19). It is active, effective memorial.","sc":[[["Luke 22:19","LUK.22.19"]],[["1 Cor 11:24-26","1CO.11.24-26"]]],"ec":"Anamnesis means more than mental recollection. It is the key to the Catholic view of the Eucharist.","f":"What changes when 'remember' means 'make present'?","v":[[1099,1103,"P2X"]]},{"n":124,"t":"Epiclesis; Communion in the Spirit","s":1104,"e":1112,"c":"1104–1112","ph":5,"k":"In the epiclesis the Church calls on the Father to send the Spirit so that the offering becomes Christ's Body and Blood and the faithful become a living offering.","w":"epiklēsis (ἐπίκλησις), 'invocation, calling down'.","sc":[[["John 14:26","JHN.14.26"]],[["2 Cor 13:14","2CO.13.14"]],[["Rom 12:1","ROM.12.1"]]],"ec":"Charismatic Christians pray 'Come, Holy Spirit' readily. The epiclesis is that prayer at the heart of the Mass.","f":"Where do you most need to invite the Spirit's transforming power?","v":[[1104,1109,"P2X"],[1110,1112,"P2Y"]]},{"n":125,"t":"Sacraments of Christ and the Church","s":1113,"e":1121,"c":"1113–1121","ph":5,"k":"Seven sacraments, instituted by Christ, are powers that come forth from his Body. Baptism, Confirmation and Holy Orders confer an indelible seal or character.","w":"sacramentum (Latin, translating mystērion). sphragis (σφραγίς), 'seal'.","sc":[[["Luke 6:19","LUK.6.19"]],[["John 20:21-23","JHN.20.21-23"]],[["2 Cor 1:21-22","2CO.1.21-22"]]],"ec":"Anglican Article XXV names two 'sacraments of the Gospel'. The Brethren call Baptism and the Lord's Supper 'ordinances'. Catholics count seven.","f":"Why might Christ have chosen visible signs to give invisible grace?","v":[[1113,1113,"P2Z"],[1114,1116,"P30"],[1117,1121,"P31"]]},{"n":126,"t":"Sacraments of Faith and Salvation","s":1122,"e":1130,"c":"1122–1130","ph":5,"k":"The sacraments presuppose faith and nourish it. They act ex opere operato, by Christ's power and not the minister's holiness, but their fruit depends on the recipient's disposition.","w":"ex opere operato (Latin), 'by the work performed'. lex orandi, lex credendi: 'the law of prayer is the law of belief'.","sc":[[["Mark 16:15-16","MRK.16.15-16"]],[["Acts 2:38","ACT.2.38"]]],"ec":"Anglican Article XXVI agrees that sacraments remain valid even if the minister is unworthy.","f":"How do you prepare your heart to receive a sacrament fruitfully?","v":[[1122,1126,"P32"],[1127,1129,"P33"],[1130,1130,"P34"]]},{"n":127,"t":"Who Celebrates the Liturgy?","s":1131,"e":1144,"c":"1131–1144","ph":5,"k":"The whole Christ, Head and Body, celebrates the liturgy, joining the heavenly liturgy of angels and saints. The assembly takes part, each person according to their role.","w":"leitourgos (λειτουργός), 'minister' (Heb 8:2, of Christ).","sc":[[["Rev 4-5","REV.4"]],[["Heb 12:22-24","HEB.12.22-24"]]],"ec":"Revelation 4-5 offers a shared vision. Catholic teaching sees the Mass as joining it.","f":"Next time you attend Mass, imagine Revelation 5 around you.","v":[[1131,1134,"P35"],[1135,1135,"P36"],[1136,1144,"P37"]]},{"n":128,"t":"Signs, Symbols, Words and Actions","s":1145,"e":1155,"c":"1145–1155","ph":5,"k":"God uses creation, human gestures and Israel's rites as signs. Christ took them up, and the liturgy unites word and action.","w":"symbolon (σύμβολον), 'something thrown together, a token'.","sc":[[["Gen 1","GEN.1"]],[["Mark 7:33-34","MRK.7.33-34"]],[["John 9:6-7","JHN.9.6-7"]]],"ec":"A sacramental view of creation. Brethren worship is more spare in its signs.","f":"Which physical sign in worship (water, oil, bread, kneeling) speaks most to you?","v":[[1145,1155,"P38"]]},{"n":129,"t":"Music and Holy Images","s":1156,"e":1162,"c":"1156–1162","ph":5,"k":"Sacred song unites the assembly and expresses prayer. Holy images are justified by the Incarnation: Christ is the image of the invisible God (Nicaea II, 787).","w":"eikōn (εἰκών), 'image' (Col 1:15).","sc":[[["Col 1:15","COL.1.15"]],[["Col 3:16","COL.3.16"]],[["Exod 25:18-22","EXO.25.18-22"]]],"ec":"Reformed iconoclasm and plain Brethren halls differ from this. Note that God himself commanded images (cherubim) in Exodus 25.","f":"Has an image or a hymn ever moved you to prayer?","v":[[1156,1162,"P38"]]},{"n":130,"t":"Liturgical Seasons and the Lord's Day","s":1163,"e":1173,"c":"1163–1173","ph":5,"k":"The liturgical year unfolds Christ's mystery. Sunday, the day of the Resurrection, is the foundation of the whole year.","w":"kyriakē hēmera (κυριακὴ ἡμέρα), 'the Lord's day' (Rev 1:10).","sc":[[["Rev 1:10","REV.1.10"]],[["Acts 20:7","ACT.20.7"]],[["1 Cor 16:2","1CO.16.2"]]],"ec":"The Brethren keep the Lord's Day breaking of bread, which is common ground.","f":"How could you make Sunday more truly the Lord's day?","v":[[1163,1173,"P39"]]},{"n":131,"t":"Liturgy of the Hours","s":1174,"e":1178,"c":"1174–1178","ph":5,"k":"The Liturgy of the Hours sanctifies the day through Psalms and readings prayed at set times. It is the prayer of the whole Church, not only clergy.","w":"horae (Latin), 'hours'. 'Seven times a day I praise you' (Ps 119:164).","sc":[[["Ps 119:164","PSA.119.164"]],[["1 Thess 5:17","1TH.5.17"]],[["Acts 3:1","ACT.3.1"]]],"ec":"The Book of Common Prayer's Morning and Evening Prayer descend from the Hours.","f":"Try praying Morning or Evening Prayer once this week.","v":[[1174,1178,"P39"]]},{"n":132,"t":"Where We Worship; In Brief","s":1179,"e":1199,"c":"1179–1199","ph":5,"k":"Worship 'in spirit and truth' is not tied to one place, yet churches are signs of the Father's house. The altar, tabernacle, chair, ambo, font and confessional each have their meaning.","w":"naos (ναός), 'temple'. We are living stones (1 Pet 2:5).","sc":[[["John 4:21-24","JHN.4.21-24"]],[["1 Pet 2:4-5","1PE.2.4-5"]]],"ec":"There is a contrast with plain meeting halls. Catholics see the building as catechesis in stone.","f":"Walk slowly through a church this week. What does each feature teach?","v":[[1179,1186,"P3A"],[1187,1199,"P3B"]]},{"n":133,"t":"One Mystery, Many Rites","s":1200,"e":1209,"c":"1200–1209","ph":5,"k":"There is one Paschal Mystery celebrated in many legitimate rites: Latin, Byzantine, Alexandrian, Syriac, Armenian, Maronite and Chaldean.","w":"ritus (Latin), 'rite, form of worship'.","sc":[[["Eph 4:3-6","EPH.4.3-6"]],[["Rev 7:9","REV.7.9"]]],"ec":"The Personal Ordinariates (2009) let former Anglicans keep elements of their liturgical patrimony within the Catholic Church.","f":"What does liturgical diversity say about the Church's catholicity?","v":[[1200,1206,"P3C"],[1207,1209,"P3D"]]},{"n":134,"t":"Sacraments of Initiation; What Is Baptism?","s":1210,"e":1216,"c":"1210–1216","ph":5,"k":"Baptism, Confirmation and the Eucharist lay the foundation of Christian life. Baptism is the gateway: rebirth in water and the Spirit.","w":"phōtismos (φωτισμός), 'enlightenment'. An early name for Baptism (Heb 6:4).","sc":[[["Titus 3:5","TIT.3.5"]],[["John 3:5","JHN.3.5"]],[["Heb 6:4","HEB.6.4"]]],"ec":"Catholic baptismal regeneration differs from the Brethren and Baptist view of baptism as an act of obedience and testimony.","f":"What did your baptism mean to you when it happened, and what might it mean now?","v":[[1210,1211,"P3E"],[1212,1212,"P3F"],[1213,1213,"P3G"],[1214,1216,"P3H"]]},{"n":135,"t":"Baptism Foreshadowed and Fulfilled","s":1217,"e":1228,"c":"1217–1228","ph":5,"k":"Creation's waters, the Flood, the Red Sea and the Jordan prefigure Baptism. Christ's own baptism and his death ('a baptism') fulfill them.","w":"antitypos (ἀντίτυπος), 'corresponding reality' (1 Pet 3:21).","sc":[[["1 Pet 3:20-21","1PE.3.20-21"]],[["1 Cor 10:1-2","1CO.10.1-2"]],[["Matt 28:19","MAT.28.19"]]],"ec":"Rich shared typology.","f":"Which Old Testament water story most deepens your sense of Baptism?","v":[[1217,1228,"P3I"]]},{"n":136,"t":"Christian Initiation","s":1229,"e":1233,"c":"1229–1233","ph":5,"k":"From the beginning, initiation involved proclaiming the Word, accepting the Gospel, conversion, faith, Baptism, the Spirit and the Eucharist. Restored after Vatican II, the adult catechumenate (now OCIA) recovers this path.","w":"katēchoumenos (κατηχούμενος), 'one being instructed'.","sc":[[["Acts 2:38-41","ACT.2.38-41"]],[["Acts 8:35-38","ACT.8.35-38"]]],"ec":"The OCIA process directly continues the ancient catechumenate.","f":"Where are you right now on this ancient path?","v":[[1229,1233,"P3J"]]},{"n":137,"t":"The Rite of Baptism Explained","s":1234,"e":1245,"c":"1234–1245","ph":5,"k":"Each sign carries meaning: the sign of the cross, the Word, exorcism, blessed water, the triple immersion or pouring, chrism, the white garment, and the candle.","w":"chrisma (χρῖσμα), 'anointing oil'.","sc":[[["Rom 6:3-4","ROM.6.3-4"]],[["Gal 3:27","GAL.3.27"]],[["Eph 5:8","EPH.5.8"]]],"ec":"Anglican baptismal rites are close. Brethren immersion stresses burial with Christ.","f":"Which part of the baptismal rite most moves you?","v":[[1234,1245,"P3J"]]},{"n":138,"t":"Who Can Be Baptized?","s":1246,"e":1255,"c":"1246–1255","ph":5,"k":"Every unbaptized person can be baptized: adults through the catechumenate, infants in the faith of the Church. Faith must grow after Baptism, supported by parents and godparents.","w":"oikos (οἶκος), 'household' (Acts 16:15, 33).","sc":[[["Acts 16:15","ACT.16.15"],["33","ACT.16.33"]],[["1 Cor 1:16","1CO.1.16"]],[["Mark 10:14","MRK.10.14"]]],"ec":"Infant baptism (Catholic, Anglican) versus believer's baptism (Brethren, Baptist) is a key difference to work through.","f":"What do the household baptisms in Acts suggest to you?","v":[[1246,1255,"P3K"]]},{"n":139,"t":"Who Baptizes? Is Baptism Necessary?","s":1256,"e":1261,"c":"1256–1261","ph":5,"k":"Bishops, priests and deacons ordinarily baptize, but in necessity anyone can. Baptism is necessary for those who have heard the Gospel. Baptism of blood and of desire are recognized, and we entrust infants who die unbaptized to God's mercy.","w":"baptismus flaminis (Latin), 'baptism of desire'.","sc":[[["John 3:5","JHN.3.5"]],[["Mark 16:16","MRK.16.16"]],[["1 Tim 2:4","1TI.2.4"]]],"ec":"This holds necessity together with hope for those beyond the sacrament's reach.","f":"How does 1261 speak to parents who have lost unbaptized children?","v":[[1256,1256,"P3L"],[1257,1261,"P3M"]]},{"n":140,"t":"The Graces of Baptism","s":1262,"e":1274,"c":"1262–1274","ph":5,"k":"Baptism forgives all sin, makes us new creatures and adopted children, incorporates us into the Church, grounds a real unity with all the baptized, and seals us with an indelible character.","w":"sphragis (σφραγίς), 'seal' (Eph 4:30).","sc":[[["2 Cor 5:17","2CO.5.17"]],[["Eph 4:30","EPH.4.30"]],[["Gal 3:27-28","GAL.3.27-28"]]],"ec":"Paragraph 1271: all validly baptized Christians are brothers and sisters in the Lord. Valid Trinitarian baptism is never repeated.","f":"Thank God today for your baptism and for the community that baptized you.","v":[[1262,1274,"P3N"]]},{"n":141,"t":"In Brief: Baptism","s":1275,"e":1284,"c":"1275–1284","ph":5,"k":"Summary of Baptism. A lighter day to consolidate.","w":"neophytos (νεόφυτος), 'newly planted' (1 Tim 3:6).","sc":[[["Rom 6:3-11","ROM.6.3-11"]]],"ec":"Shared core.","f":"Write three things Baptism does, in your own words.","v":[[1275,1284,"P3O"]]},{"n":142,"t":"Confirmation in God's Plan","s":1285,"e":1292,"c":"1285–1292","ph":5,"k":"The Spirit rested on the Messiah and was promised to all. Confirmation perfects baptismal grace. In the East it is given with Baptism; in the West it is linked to the bishop.","w":"myron (μύρον), 'perfumed oil, chrism'.","sc":[[["Acts 8:14-17","ACT.8.14-17"]],[["Acts 19:5-6","ACT.19.5-6"]],[["Isa 11:2","ISA.11.2"]]],"ec":"Anglicans confirm through the bishop, the Brethren have no equivalent rite, and Pentecostals speak of 'baptism in the Spirit'. Already-baptized candidates are usually confirmed by the priest at their reception.","f":"How does Confirmation relate to your own experiences of the Spirit?","v":[[1285,1285,"P3P"],[1286,1292,"P3Q"]]},{"n":143,"t":"Anointing and Seal","s":1293,"e":1301,"c":"1293–1301","ph":5,"k":"Anointing with chrism marks the confirmed as belonging to Christ and sent on his mission. The essential rite is the anointing with the words 'Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.'","w":"sphragis (σφραγίς), 'seal'. A mark of ownership and protection.","sc":[[["2 Cor 1:21-22","2CO.1.21-22"]],[["Eph 1:13","EPH.1.13"]],[["Isa 61:1","ISA.61.1"]]],"ec":"Shared biblical imagery.","f":"What does it mean that you are 'sealed' as Christ's own?","v":[[1293,1301,"P3R"]]},{"n":144,"t":"Effects of Confirmation; Who Receives It","s":1302,"e":1311,"c":"1302–1311","ph":5,"k":"Confirmation deepens baptismal grace, roots us as God's children, unites us more firmly to Christ, increases the gifts of the Spirit and strengthens us to witness. It requires the state of grace, which is why Confession is recommended beforehand.","w":"The seven gifts: wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety and fear of the Lord (from Isa 11:2-3, LXX/Vulgate).","sc":[[["Isa 11:1-3","ISA.11.1-3"]],[["Acts 1:8","ACT.1.8"]]],"ec":"Notice the order: Reconciliation before Confirmation.","f":"Which of the seven gifts do you most need for your mission?","v":[[1302,1305,"P3S"],[1306,1311,"P3T"]]},{"n":145,"t":"The Minister; In Brief","s":1312,"e":1321,"c":"1312–1321","ph":5,"k":"The bishop is the original minister. Priests confirm by faculty, as at an adult's reception into the Church.","w":"episkopos (ἐπίσκοπος), 'overseer'.","sc":[[["Acts 8:14-17","ACT.8.14-17"]]],"ec":"Because Rome does not recognize Anglican orders, Anglican confirmation is not received as the sacrament, so Anglican-confirmed candidates are confirmed at reception.","f":"Who will be your sponsor, and why?","v":[[1312,1314,"P3U"],[1315,1321,"P3V"]]},{"n":146,"t":"Why Penance After Baptism?","s":1420,"e":1426,"c":"1420–1426","ph":5,"k":"We carry new life 'in earthen vessels'. Post-baptismal sin is real, and Christ instituted a sacrament of healing for it. It goes by several names: conversion, Penance, Confession, forgiveness, Reconciliation.","w":"exomologēsis (ἐξομολόγησις), 'confession, acknowledgment'.","sc":[[["2 Cor 4:7","2CO.4.7"]],[["2 Cor 5:17-21","2CO.5.17-21"]],[["1 John 1:8-9","1JN.1.8-9"]]],"ec":"This is distinctively Catholic, and one of the areas of genuinely new terrain for most Protestants.","f":"Which name for this sacrament draws you, and which daunts you?","v":[[1420,1421,"P45"],[1422,1422,"P46"],[1423,1424,"P47"],[1425,1426,"P48"]]},{"n":147,"t":"Conversion and Interior Penance","s":1427,"e":1433,"c":"1427–1433","ph":5,"k":"The first conversion happens at Baptism. A second conversion is the lifelong task of the Church. Interior penance is a radical reorientation of the heart, moved by grace.","w":"shuv (שׁוּב), 'to turn back'. metanoia (μετάνοια), 'change of mind'.","sc":[[["Joel 2:12-13","JOL.2.12-13"]],[["Ps 51","PSA.51"]],[["Luke 15:11-24","LUK.15.11-24"]]],"ec":"Evangelical teaching on repentance is close kin.","f":"Pray Psalm 51 slowly today.","v":[[1427,1429,"P49"],[1430,1433,"P4A"]]},{"n":148,"t":"Many Forms of Penance","s":1434,"e":1439,"c":"1434–1439","ph":5,"k":"Fasting, prayer and almsgiving express conversion, along with reconciliation, care for the poor, and accepting suffering. Lent and Fridays are privileged times.","w":"nēsteia (νηστεία), 'fasting'.","sc":[[["Tob 12:8","TOB.12.8"]],[["Matt 6:1-18","MAT.6.1-18"]],[["Jas 5:20","JAS.5.20"]]],"ec":"Lenten discipline is shared with Anglicans and many Protestants.","f":"What one penitential practice will you take up or deepen?","v":[[1434,1439,"P4B"]]},{"n":149,"t":"God Forgives and Entrusts the Ministry","s":1440,"e":1449,"c":"1440–1449","ph":5,"k":"Only God forgives sin, but Christ gave this power to the apostles. Reconciliation with the Church and with God go together.","w":"asar / sherā (Aramaic), 'bind / loose'. These were rabbinic terms for authoritative decisions.","sc":[[["Matt 16:19","MAT.16.19"]],[["18:18","MAT.18.18"]],[["John 20:21-23","JHN.20.21-23"]],[["2 Cor 5:18","2CO.5.18"]]],"ec":"Brethren and most Protestants confess to God directly. The Anglican saying on confession is 'all may, some should, none must'.","f":"Why might Christ want forgiveness to be spoken aloud by another person?","v":[[1440,1449,"P4C"]]},{"n":150,"r":1,"q":"Have you written an examination of conscience yet? What still holds you back from Confession?","items":[[121,"Christ's Work in the Liturgy"],[122,"The Spirit Prepares Us"],[123,"The Spirit Recalls the Mystery"],[124,"Epiclesis; Communion in the Spirit"],[125,"Sacraments of Christ and the Church"],[126,"Sacraments of Faith and Salvation"],[127,"Who Celebrates the Liturgy?"],[128,"Signs, Symbols, Words and Actions"],[129,"Music and Holy Images"],[130,"Liturgical Seasons and the Lord's Day"],[131,"Liturgy of the Hours"],[132,"Where We Worship; In Brief"],[133,"One Mystery, Many Rites"],[134,"Sacraments of Initiation; What Is Baptism?"],[135,"Baptism Foreshadowed and Fulfilled"],[136,"Christian Initiation"],[137,"The Rite of Baptism Explained"],[138,"Who Can Be Baptized?"],[139,"Who Baptizes? Is Baptism Necessary?"],[140,"The Graces of Baptism"],[141,"In Brief: Baptism"],[142,"Confirmation in God's Plan"],[143,"Anointing and Seal"],[144,"Effects of Confirmation; Who Receives It"],[145,"The Minister; In Brief"],[146,"Why Penance After Baptism?"],[147,"Conversion and Interior Penance"],[148,"Many Forms of Penance"],[149,"God Forgives and Entrusts the Ministry"]]},{"n":151,"t":"Contrition","s":1450,"e":1454,"c":"1450–1454","ph":5,"k":"Contrition is sorrow for sin with the resolve not to sin again. Perfect contrition comes from love of God; imperfect contrition (attrition) comes from fear or the ugliness of sin, and is enough in the sacrament. Examination of conscience prepares the heart.","w":"contritio (Latin), 'a crushing'. Compare 'a broken and contrite heart' (Ps 51:17).","sc":[[["Ps 51:17","PSA.51.17"]],[["Luke 18:13","LUK.18.13"]]],"ec":"There is a parallel in Protestant revival preaching, but the distinction between perfect and imperfect contrition is distinctively Catholic.","f":"Use an examination of conscience today, perhaps one based on the Ten Commandments.","v":[[1450,1454,"P4D"]]},{"n":152,"t":"Confession of Sins","s":1455,"e":1458,"c":"1455–1458","ph":5,"k":"Naming sins frees us and opens us to communion. All mortal sins must be confessed by kind and number, and confessing venial sins is recommended. Confession is required at least once a year.","w":"exomologeō (ἐξομολογέω), 'to confess openly' (Jas 5:16).","sc":[[["Jas 5:16","JAS.5.16"]],[["Prov 28:13","PRO.28.13"]],[["1 John 1:9","1JN.1.9"]]],"ec":"James 5:16 ('confess your sins to one another') is common ground. The sacrament gives it a definite form.","f":"What makes confessing aloud hard for you, and what might make it freeing?","v":[[1455,1458,"P4D"]]},{"n":153,"t":"Satisfaction (Penance)","s":1459,"e":1460,"c":"1459–1460","ph":5,"k":"Forgiveness removes the guilt, but the harm and the disorder remain. Satisfaction (the 'penance') makes amends and helps healing. It does not pay for forgiveness; it unites us to Christ's satisfaction.","w":"satisfactio (Latin), 'doing enough'.","sc":[[["Luke 19:8","LUK.19.8"]],[["Col 1:24","COL.1.24"]]],"ec":"Many Protestants hear 'satisfaction' as earning forgiveness. Paragraph 1460 explicitly grounds it in Christ.","f":"Is there any harm you have caused that you need to repair?","v":[[1459,1460,"P4D"]]},{"n":154,"t":"The Confessor; Effects of Reconciliation","s":1461,"e":1470,"c":"1461–1470","ph":5,"k":"Bishops and priests forgive in Christ's name as healers and judges, bound by the absolute seal of confession. The effects are reconciliation with God and the Church, peace and spiritual strength.","w":"sigillum (Latin), 'seal'. The inviolable secrecy of confession.","sc":[[["Luke 15:20-24","LUK.15.20-24"]],[["2 Cor 5:20","2CO.5.20"]]],"ec":"The absolute seal is a strong safeguard for anyone who is hesitant.","f":"Imagine hearing the words of absolution. What would they release in you?","v":[[1461,1467,"P4E"],[1468,1470,"P4F"]]},{"n":155,"t":"Indulgences","s":1471,"e":1479,"c":"1471–1479","ph":5,"k":"An indulgence remits temporal punishment due to sins already forgiven, drawing on the Church's treasury of merits (Christ's and the saints'). It is never forgiveness of sin, and it can be applied to the dead.","w":"indulgentia (Latin), 'kindness, remission'.","sc":[[["Matt 16:19","MAT.16.19"]],[["Col 1:24","COL.1.24"]],[["2 Macc 12:45","2MA.12.45"]]],"ec":"Abuses of indulgences sparked the Reformation in 1517. Trent condemned the abuses (such as selling them) but upheld the doctrine.","f":"How does the communion of saints make sense of indulgences?","v":[[1471,1479,"P4G"]]},{"n":156,"t":"Celebrating Reconciliation; In Brief","s":1480,"e":1498,"c":"1480–1498","ph":5,"k":"The rite: greeting, Word of God, confession, penance, act of contrition, absolution, and dismissal. Individual confession remains the ordinary way.","w":"Ego te absolvo (Latin), 'I absolve you'.","sc":[[["Luke 7:47-50","LUK.7.47-50"]],[["Ps 32:1-5","PSA.32.1-5"]]],"ec":"The BCP also contains a form of absolution, but the CCC makes individual confession normative.","f":"Find out when confessions are heard at your parish.","v":[[1480,1484,"P4H"],[1485,1498,"P4I"]]},{"n":157,"t":"The Eucharist: Source and Summit","s":1322,"e":1327,"c":"1322–1327","ph":5,"k":"The Eucharist completes Christian initiation. It is the source and summit of the Christian life, containing Christ himself, and the sum of our faith.","w":"eucharistia (εὐχαριστία), 'thanksgiving'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 10:16-17","1CO.10.16-17"]],[["John 6:51","JHN.6.51"]]],"ec":"Many Protestants see the Lord's Supper as one ordinance among others. For Catholics it is the summit of the whole life of the Church.","f":"How would your week change if the Eucharist were truly its summit?","v":[[1322,1323,"P3W"],[1324,1327,"P3X"]]},{"n":158,"t":"Names and Prefigurations","s":1328,"e":1336,"c":"1328–1336","ph":5,"k":"It goes by many names: Eucharist, Lord's Supper, Breaking of Bread, Holy Sacrifice, Holy Communion, Holy Mass. It was prefigured by Melchizedek, the manna and Cana. John 6 caused some disciples to leave.","w":"klasis tou artou (κλάσις τοῦ ἄρτου), 'breaking of the bread' (Acts 2:42).","sc":[[["Gen 14:18","GEN.14.18"]],[["Exod 16","EXO.16"]],[["John 2:1-11","JHN.2.1-11"]],[["Acts 2:42","ACT.2.42"]]],"ec":"The Brethren 'Breaking of Bread' uses one of the oldest names.","f":"Read John 6:60-69. Why did some leave, and why did Peter stay?","v":[[1328,1332,"P3Y"],[1333,1336,"P3Z"]]},{"n":159,"t":"Institution: 'Do This'","s":1337,"e":1344,"c":"1337–1344","ph":5,"k":"At the Last Supper, Jesus instituted the Eucharist as the memorial of his death and resurrection and commanded the apostles to celebrate it until he comes.","w":"touto poieite (τοῦτο ποιεῖτε), 'do this' (Luke 22:19).","sc":[[["Luke 22:7-20","LUK.22.7-20"]],[["1 Cor 11:23-26","1CO.11.23-26"]]],"ec":"This shared command is interpreted differently: memorial (Zwingli) versus real sacramental presence.","f":"What does it mean to obey 'do this' today?","v":[[1337,1344,"P3Z"]]},{"n":160,"t":"The Mass: Shape Since Justin Martyr","s":1345,"e":1355,"c":"1345–1355","ph":5,"k":"Justin Martyr (c. 155) describes Sunday worship: readings, a homily, intercessions, the offering of bread and wine, the Eucharistic prayer and communion. This is still the shape of the Mass.","w":"anaphora (ἀναφορά), 'offering up'. The Eucharistic prayer.","sc":[[["Luke 24:13-35","LUK.24.13-35"]],[["Acts 20:7-11","ACT.20.7-11"]]],"ec":"This is a strong historical argument: the 2nd-century pattern matches the Mass.","f":"Read Luke 24:13-35. Notice the Word, then the breaking of bread.","v":[[1345,1355,"P40"]]},{"n":161,"t":"Thanksgiving and Praise","s":1356,"e":1361,"c":"1356–1361","ph":5,"k":"The Eucharist is thanksgiving to the Father for creation and redemption, and praise offered through Christ.","w":"todah (תּוֹדָה), 'thank-offering'.","sc":[[["Ps 116:12-17","PSA.116.12-17"]],[["Heb 13:15","HEB.13.15"]]],"ec":"Shared praise.","f":"What 'thank-offering' can you bring this week?","v":[[1356,1361,"P41"]]},{"n":162,"t":"The Sacrifice Made Present","s":1362,"e":1367,"c":"1362–1367","ph":5,"k":"The Eucharist is a memorial that makes Christ's one sacrifice present. The sacrifice of the Cross and of the Mass are one sacrifice; only the manner of offering differs.","w":"thysia (θυσία), 'sacrifice'. Compare Malachi 1:11, 'a pure offering among the nations'.","sc":[[["Mal 1:11","MAL.1.11"]],[["Heb 9:24-28","HEB.9.24-28"]],[["1 Cor 11:26","1CO.11.26"]]],"ec":"Anglican Article XXXI condemns 'sacrifices of Masses'. Catholic teaching: one sacrifice, not repeated but made present (1366-1367).","f":"How does 'made present, not repeated' answer the objection from Hebrews?","v":[[1362,1367,"P41"]]},{"n":163,"t":"The Church's Offering","s":1368,"e":1372,"c":"1368–1372","ph":5,"k":"The Church offers herself with Christ. The Mass is offered for the living and the dead, in communion with the saints.","w":"per ipsum (Latin), 'through him'. The words of the final doxology.","sc":[[["Rom 12:1","ROM.12.1"]],[["Col 1:24","COL.1.24"]]],"ec":"Offering Mass for the dead is distinctively Catholic.","f":"What will you 'place on the altar' at your next Mass?","v":[[1368,1372,"P41"]]},{"n":164,"t":"Real Presence and Transubstantiation","s":1373,"e":1381,"c":"1373–1381","ph":5,"k":"Christ is present in many ways, but in the Eucharist uniquely: truly, really and substantially. The whole substance of the bread and wine becomes his Body and Blood (Trent's 'transubstantiation'). Adoration follows.","w":"ousia (οὐσία) / substantia (Latin), 'substance, being'.","sc":[[["John 6:51-58","JHN.6.51-58"]],[["1 Cor 11:27-29","1CO.11.27-29"]]],"ec":"Anglican Article XXVIII rejects transubstantiation, though many Anglicans affirm a real presence. Brethren practice is closer to Zwingli's memorialism.","f":"What objection to the Real Presence do you find hardest, and how does John 6 answer it?","v":[[1373,1381,"P41"]]},{"n":165,"t":"The Paschal Banquet: Receiving Worthily","s":1382,"e":1390,"c":"1382–1390","ph":5,"k":"The Mass is sacrifice and banquet. Receiving requires preparation: those conscious of grave sin must go to Confession first, and there is a one-hour fast. Frequent Communion is encouraged.","w":"Domine, non sum dignus (Latin), 'Lord, I am not worthy' (Matt 8:8).","sc":[[["1 Cor 11:27-29","1CO.11.27-29"]],[["Matt 8:8","MAT.8.8"]],[["John 13:1-15","JHN.13.1-15"]]],"ec":"1 Corinthians 11 is read by all, but its 'discerning the body' is interpreted differently.","f":"Pray Matthew 8:8 as your own prayer before your next Communion.","v":[[1382,1390,"P42"]]},{"n":166,"t":"Fruits of Communion; Unity","s":1391,"e":1401,"c":"1391–1401","ph":5,"k":"Communion deepens union with Christ, separates us from sin, builds the Church and commits us to the poor. Intercommunion is generally not possible with communities that lack valid orders. On Good Friday, no Mass is celebrated.","w":"koinōnia (κοινωνία), 'communion, sharing'.","sc":[[["John 6:56","JHN.6.56"]],[["1 Cor 10:16-17","1CO.10.16-17"]]],"ec":"Paragraphs 1398-1401 explain why Communion follows full unity rather than creating it.","f":"What does the Eucharist's absence on Good Friday teach about longing?","v":[[1391,1401,"P42"]]},{"n":167,"t":"Pledge of Glory; In Brief","s":1402,"e":1419,"c":"1402–1419","ph":5,"k":"The Eucharist anticipates the heavenly banquet. Ignatius of Antioch called it the 'medicine of immortality'. The Easter Vigil is the Church's traditional night of Christian initiation.","w":"pharmakon athanasias (φάρμακον ἀθανασίας), 'medicine of immortality'.","sc":[[["John 6:54","JHN.6.54"]],[["Rev 19:9","REV.19.9"]]],"ec":"Shared hope of the heavenly banquet.","f":"What are you waiting for, as the Church waits at the Easter Vigil?","v":[[1402,1405,"P43"],[1406,1419,"P44"]]},{"n":168,"t":"Illness and Christ the Physician","s":1499,"e":1505,"c":"1499–1505","ph":6,"k":"Illness confronts us with our limits and can lead to God or away from him. Christ the physician heals, and he gives suffering a redemptive meaning.","w":"iatros (ἰατρός), 'physician' (Mark 2:17).","sc":[[["Matt 8:16-17","MAT.8.16-17"]],[["Mark 2:17","MRK.2.17"]],[["Isa 53:4","ISA.53.4"]]],"ec":"Shared. Charismatic healing ministry has a natural connection here.","f":"How has illness, yours or someone else's, drawn you toward or away from God?","v":[[1499,1499,"P4J"],[1500,1505,"P4K"]]},{"n":169,"t":"Heal the Sick; Institution","s":1506,"e":1513,"c":"1506–1513","ph":6,"k":"Christ sent the apostles to heal. James 5 describes the elders anointing the sick. The sacrament is for those seriously ill or aged, not only the dying.","w":"presbyteroi (πρεσβύτεροι), 'elders' (Jas 5:14).","sc":[[["Jas 5:14-15","JAS.5.14-15"]],[["Mark 6:13","MRK.6.13"]]],"ec":"Charismatic healing prayer and the sacrament complement each other.","f":"Who do you know who needs this sacrament?","v":[[1506,1513,"P4K"]]},{"n":170,"t":"Who, How, Effects; Viaticum","s":1514,"e":1525,"c":"1514–1525","ph":6,"k":"Anointing unites the sick to Christ's passion, gives strength and peace, forgives sins, and sometimes restores health. Viaticum is the last Communion, 'food for the journey'.","w":"viaticum (Latin), 'provision for a journey'.","sc":[[["John 6:54","JHN.6.54"]],[["2 Cor 4:16-18","2CO.4.16-18"]]],"ec":"Distinctively Catholic.","f":"How would you want to be accompanied at the end of life?","v":[[1514,1516,"P4L"],[1517,1519,"P4M"],[1520,1523,"P4N"],[1524,1525,"P4O"]]},{"n":171,"t":"In Brief: Anointing of the Sick","s":1526,"e":1532,"c":"1526–1532","ph":6,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"elaion (ἔλαιον), 'olive oil'.","sc":[[["Jas 5:14-15","JAS.5.14-15"]]],"ec":"Shared reading of James 5.","f":"Pray for someone who is seriously ill today.","v":[[1526,1532,"P4P"]]},{"n":172,"t":"Sacraments at the Service of Communion","s":1533,"e":1538,"c":"1533–1538","ph":6,"k":"Holy Orders and Matrimony are directed toward the salvation of others. 'Order' meant an established body in Rome, and ordination incorporates a man into one.","w":"ordo (Latin) / taxis (τάξις), 'order, rank'.","sc":[[["Heb 5:1-6","HEB.5.1-6"]],[["1 Pet 2:9","1PE.2.9"]]],"ec":"Anglicans keep the three orders. The Brethren have no ordained ministry.","f":"How is your own vocation a service to others' salvation?","v":[[1533,1535,"P4Q"],[1536,1536,"P4R"],[1537,1538,"P4S"]]},{"n":173,"t":"One Priesthood, Two Participations","s":1539,"e":1553,"c":"1539–1553","ph":6,"k":"The Levitical priesthood was a shadow fulfilled in Christ, the one Priest. All the baptized share his priesthood. Ministerial priests share it differently: in essence, not just degree, and for the service of the common priesthood.","w":"hiereus (ἱερεύς), 'priest' (sacrificial). presbyteros (πρεσβύτερος), 'elder', the root of the English 'priest'.","sc":[[["Heb 7:23-28","HEB.7.23-28"]],[["1 Pet 2:5","1PE.2.5"]],[["Rom 15:16","ROM.15.16"]]],"ec":"The Brethren reject any priestly class. Paragraph 1547 holds both priesthoods without collapsing either into the other.","f":"What would be lost if there were no ordained priests?","v":[[1539,1553,"P4T"]]},{"n":174,"t":"Bishops","s":1554,"e":1561,"c":"1554–1561","ph":6,"k":"Bishops receive the fullness of Orders as successors of the apostles, sharing responsibility for the whole Church with the Pope.","w":"episkopos (ἐπίσκοπος), 'overseer'.","sc":[[["Acts 20:28","ACT.20.28"]],[["1 Tim 3:1-7","1TI.3.1-7"]]],"ec":"Anglican bishops are similar in structure; the question is validity.","f":"Read 1 Timothy 3:1-7. What qualities matter most in a shepherd?","v":[[1554,1561,"P4U"]]},{"n":175,"t":"Priests and Deacons","s":1562,"e":1571,"c":"1562–1571","ph":6,"k":"Priests are co-workers of the bishops and act in persona Christi, especially in the Eucharist. Deacons are ordained for service (Word, liturgy and charity), and men can be permanent deacons.","w":"diakonos (διάκονος), 'servant'.","sc":[[["Acts 6:1-6","ACT.6.1-6"]],[["1 Tim 3:8-13","1TI.3.8-13"]],[["Titus 1:5","TIT.1.5"]]],"ec":"The permanent diaconate is a newer opening for married men.","f":"Pray for the priests and deacons of your parish by name.","v":[[1562,1571,"P4U"]]},{"n":176,"t":"Ordination; Who Can Be Ordained?","s":1572,"e":1580,"c":"1572–1580","ph":6,"k":"The essential rite is the laying on of hands with the consecratory prayer. Bishops ordain. Ordination is reserved to baptized men. In the Latin Church priests are normally celibate, though there are exceptions for some former Anglican and Protestant clergy.","w":"epithesis tōn cheirōn (ἐπίθεσις τῶν χειρῶν), 'laying on of hands'.","sc":[[["1 Tim 4:14","1TI.4.14"]],[["2 Tim 1:6","2TI.1.6"]],[["Matt 19:12","MAT.19.12"]]],"ec":"The Anglican Communion ordains women in many provinces. The Catholic Church holds that she has no authority to do so.","f":"What do you understand of the reasons given in 1577?","v":[[1572,1574,"P4V"],[1575,1576,"P4W"],[1577,1580,"P4X"]]},{"n":177,"t":"Effects of Orders: The Indelible Character","s":1581,"e":1589,"c":"1581–1589","ph":6,"k":"Ordination configures a man to Christ the Head and leaves an indelible character. A sinful minister does not invalidate the sacraments, but he is called to great holiness.","w":"in persona Christi capitis (Latin), 'in the person of Christ the Head'.","sc":[[["2 Cor 5:20","2CO.5.20"]],[["John 10:11","JHN.10.11"]]],"ec":"This agrees with Anglican Article XXVI on unworthy ministers.","f":"How can you support your priests in holiness?","v":[[1581,1589,"P4Y"]]},{"n":178,"t":"In Brief: Holy Orders","s":1590,"e":1600,"c":"1590–1600","ph":6,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"presbyterion (πρεσβυτέριον), 'body of elders'.","sc":[[["2 Tim 2:2","2TI.2.2"]]],"ec":"Shared emphasis on faithful transmission.","f":"Pray for vocations in your diocese.","v":[[1590,1600,"P4Z"]]},{"n":179,"t":"Marriage in God's Plan","s":1601,"e":1611,"c":"1601–1611","ph":6,"k":"Marriage is written into creation: man and woman made for each other. Sin wounded it, and the Law protected it as it prepared for Christ.","w":"ezer kenegdo (עֵזֶר כְּנֶגְדּוֹ), 'a helper corresponding to him' (Gen 2:18).","sc":[[["Gen 1:27-28","GEN.1.27-28"]],[["2:18-24","GEN.2.18-24"]]],"ec":"This creation-based view of marriage is shared.","f":"What does Genesis 2 reveal about God's design for love?","v":[[1601,1601,"P50"],[1602,1611,"P51"]]},{"n":180,"r":1,"q":"What did the readings on the Eucharist change in how you understand the Lord's Supper?","items":[[151,"Contrition"],[152,"Confession of Sins"],[153,"Satisfaction (Penance)"],[154,"The Confessor; Effects of Reconciliation"],[155,"Indulgences"],[156,"Celebrating Reconciliation; In Brief"],[157,"The Eucharist: Source and Summit"],[158,"Names and Prefigurations"],[159,"Institution: 'Do This'"],[160,"The Mass: Shape Since Justin Martyr"],[161,"Thanksgiving and Praise"],[162,"The Sacrifice Made Present"],[163,"The Church's Offering"],[164,"Real Presence and Transubstantiation"],[165,"The Paschal Banquet: Receiving Worthily"],[166,"Fruits of Communion; Unity"],[167,"Pledge of Glory; In Brief"],[168,"Illness and Christ the Physician"],[169,"Heal the Sick; Institution"],[170,"Who, How, Effects; Viaticum"],[171,"In Brief: Anointing of the Sick"],[172,"Sacraments at the Service of Communion"],[173,"One Priesthood, Two Participations"],[174,"Bishops"],[175,"Priests and Deacons"],[176,"Ordination; Who Can Be Ordained?"],[177,"Effects of Orders: The Indelible Character"],[178,"In Brief: Holy Orders"],[179,"Marriage in God's Plan"]]},{"n":181,"t":"Marriage in the Lord; Virginity for the Kingdom","s":1612,"e":1620,"c":"1612–1620","ph":6,"k":"Christ restores marriage's original indissolubility and makes it a sign of his love for the Church. Virginity for the Kingdom and marriage support each other.","w":"mystērion mega (μυστήριον μέγα), 'great mystery' (Eph 5:32).","sc":[[["Eph 5:25-32","EPH.5.25-32"]],[["Matt 19:3-12","MAT.19.3-12"]]],"ec":"Article XXV does not count marriage among the sacraments. Ephesians 5:32 is the key text.","f":"How does marriage image Christ and the Church?","v":[[1612,1620,"P51"]]},{"n":182,"t":"Celebrating Marriage; Consent","s":1621,"e":1632,"c":"1621–1632","ph":6,"k":"Spouses are the ministers of the sacrament, by free consent. If consent was defective, the Church may declare a marriage null. That is not 'Catholic divorce'.","w":"consensus (Latin), 'agreement'.","sc":[[["Gen 2:24","GEN.2.24"]],[["Mark 10:9","MRK.10.9"]]],"ec":"The process for declarations of nullity is distinctive and often misunderstood.","f":"What makes consent free and full?","v":[[1621,1624,"P52"],[1625,1632,"P53"]]},{"n":183,"t":"Mixed Marriages; Effects","s":1633,"e":1642,"c":"1633–1642","ph":6,"k":"Marriages between Catholics and other Christians need care and permission. Marriage creates a permanent bond and gives grace to perfect the couple's love.","w":"disparitas cultus (Latin), 'disparity of worship'. A Catholic marrying an unbaptized person.","sc":[[["1 Cor 7:12-16","1CO.7.12-16"]]],"ec":"This is relevant for interchurch families.","f":"What graces does a marriage need most?","v":[[1633,1637,"P53"],[1638,1642,"P54"]]},{"n":184,"t":"Unity, Indissolubility, Fidelity","s":1643,"e":1651,"c":"1643–1651","ph":6,"k":"Conjugal love requires unity, indissolubility and fidelity. The Church accompanies the divorced and remarried with care, though sacramental Communion depends on their situation.","w":"chesed (חֶסֶד), 'steadfast covenant love'.","sc":[[["Mark 10:2-12","MRK.10.2-12"]],[["Hos 2:19-20","HOS.2.19-20"]]],"ec":"Many Protestant churches permit divorce and remarriage more readily.","f":"Where does fidelity in any relationship cost you something?","v":[[1643,1651,"P55"]]},{"n":185,"t":"Openness to Life; The Domestic Church","s":1652,"e":1658,"c":"1652–1658","ph":6,"k":"Marriage is ordered to the good of the spouses and to having and educating children. The family is the 'domestic church', the first school of faith. Single people also have a place in the Church family.","w":"ecclesia domestica (Latin), 'domestic church'.","sc":[[["Ps 127:3-5","PSA.127.3-5"]],[["Acts 16:31-34","ACT.16.31-34"]]],"ec":"Shared emphasis on the household.","f":"How can your home become more of a 'domestic church'?","v":[[1652,1654,"P55"],[1655,1658,"P56"]]},{"n":186,"t":"In Brief: Matrimony","s":1659,"e":1666,"c":"1659–1666","ph":6,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"covenant (Latin foedus).","sc":[[["Eph 5:31-32","EPH.5.31-32"]]],"ec":"Shared biblical vision of covenant.","f":"Pray for a married couple you know.","v":[[1659,1666,"P57"]]},{"n":187,"t":"Sacramentals","s":1667,"e":1673,"c":"1667–1673","ph":6,"k":"Sacramentals are signs instituted by the Church, such as blessings, holy water and exorcisms. They prepare us for grace, and every baptized person can bless in some way.","w":"benedictio (Latin) / eulogia (εὐλογία), 'blessing'.","sc":[[["1 Tim 4:4-5","1TI.4.4-5"]],[["Num 6:22-27","NUM.6.22-27"]]],"ec":"Charismatic deliverance ministry should be distinguished from formal exorcism (1673).","f":"Bless someone today using Numbers 6:24-26.","v":[[1667,1673,"P58"]]},{"n":188,"t":"Popular Piety","s":1674,"e":1679,"c":"1674–1679","ph":6,"k":"Popular devotions such as pilgrimages, the Rosary, medals and processions extend the liturgy but do not replace it. They need to be discerned.","w":"pietas (Latin), 'dutiful devotion'.","sc":[[["Acts 19:11-12","ACT.19.11-12"]],[["Ps 122:1","PSA.122.1"]]],"ec":"Protestants sometimes find this an area of concern. The CCC sets it under the liturgy.","f":"Which devotion nourishes you, and how does it lead you to Mass?","v":[[1674,1676,"P58"],[1677,1679,"P59"]]},{"n":189,"t":"Christian Funerals","s":1680,"e":1690,"c":"1680–1690","ph":6,"k":"Funerals express Paschal hope and communion with the dead. The rite includes welcome, the Word, the Eucharist and the farewell.","w":"requiem (Latin), 'rest'.","sc":[[["John 11:25-26","JHN.11.25-26"]],[["1 Thess 4:13-18","1TH.4.13-18"]]],"ec":"Prayer for the dead is distinctive (see CCC 958).","f":"How would you want your own funeral to proclaim hope?","v":[[1680,1680,"P5A"],[1681,1683,"P5B"],[1684,1690,"P5C"]]},{"n":190,"t":"The Creeds","s":185,"e":197,"c":"185–197","ph":7,"k":"Creeds are summaries of the faith, our 'password' of belief. The Apostles' Creed (the Roman baptismal creed) and the Nicene-Constantinopolitan Creed are the most authoritative.","w":"symbolon (σύμβολον), 'token'. Half of a broken object matched for identification.","sc":[[["1 Cor 15:3-5","1CO.15.3-5"]],[["Rom 10:9","ROM.10.9"]]],"ec":"Both creeds are shared with Anglicans and most Protestants.","f":"Pray the Apostles' Creed slowly, pausing at each line.","v":[[185,197,"P14"]]},{"n":191,"t":"I Believe in One God","s":198,"e":202,"c":"198–202","ph":7,"k":"The confession of one God is the root of all others. Israel's Shema becomes the Church's creed.","w":"Shema (שְׁמַע), 'Hear!' (Deut 6:4).","sc":[[["Deut 6:4-5","DEU.6.4-5"]],[["Mark 12:29-30","MRK.12.29-30"]],[["Isa 45:22","ISA.45.22"]]],"ec":"Shared monotheism.","f":"Recite the Shema morning and evening this week.","v":[[198,198,"P15"],[199,202,"P16"]]},{"n":192,"t":"God Reveals His Name","s":203,"e":213,"c":"203–213","ph":7,"k":"God reveals his name, YHWH, at the burning bush. It is both mysterious and near: 'I AM'. He is merciful, gracious and faithful, and he alone truly is.","w":"ehyeh asher ehyeh (אֶהְיֶה אֲשֶׁר אֶהְיֶה), 'I am who I am / I will be who I will be' (Exod 3:14).","sc":[[["Exod 3:13-15","EXO.3.13-15"]],[["34:6-7","EXO.34.6-7"]],[["John 8:58","JHN.8.58"]]],"ec":"Shared. Jesus' 'I AM' sayings take on this name.","f":"Which of God's attributes in Exodus 34:6-7 do you most need today?","v":[[203,213,"P16"]]},{"n":193,"t":"God Is Truth and Love","s":214,"e":221,"c":"214–221","ph":7,"k":"God is truth itself; his words do not deceive. God is love, and his inner life is an eternal exchange of love.","w":"chesed ve'emet (חֶסֶד וֶאֱמֶת), 'steadfast love and faithfulness'.","sc":[[["Exod 34:6","EXO.34.6"]],[["1 John 4:8","1JN.4.8"],["16","1JN.4.16"]],[["John 14:6","JHN.14.6"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where do you doubt God's love, and where his truthfulness?","v":[[214,221,"P16"]]},{"n":194,"t":"Living by Faith in One God","s":222,"e":231,"c":"222–231","ph":7,"k":"Believing in one God means knowing his greatness and majesty, giving thanks, recognizing human dignity, using created things well and trusting him in every circumstance.","w":"Solo Dios basta (Spanish, Teresa of Ávila), 'God alone suffices'.","sc":[[["Ps 116:12","PSA.116.12"]],[["Job 36:26","JOB.36.26"]],[["Matt 6:24","MAT.6.24"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What would 'God alone suffices' mean in your current worries?","v":[[222,231,"P16"]]},{"n":195,"t":"The Trinity: Father Revealed by the Son","s":232,"e":242,"c":"232–242","ph":7,"k":"The Trinity is the central mystery of faith. Jesus reveals God as Father in a unique sense: eternally Father of the Son.","w":"Abba (Aramaic, אַבָּא), 'Father'. Intimate yet reverent.","sc":[[["Matt 28:19","MAT.28.19"]],[["Mark 14:36","MRK.14.36"]],[["John 14:9","JHN.14.9"]]],"ec":"Shared Trinitarian faith.","f":"Pray 'Abba' in silence for five minutes.","v":[[232,242,"P17"]]},{"n":196,"t":"The Spirit Revealed; The Filioque","s":243,"e":248,"c":"243–248","ph":7,"k":"The Spirit is revealed as a distinct Person. The Latin West confesses that he proceeds from the Father 'and the Son' (filioque). The East says 'from the Father through the Son'. Both express complementary truths.","w":"filioque (Latin), 'and from the Son'. ekporeuomai (ἐκπορεύομαι), 'proceed' (John 15:26).","sc":[[["John 15:26","JHN.15.26"]],[["16:14","JHN.16.14"]]],"ec":"Anglicans and most Protestants recite the filioque. The Orthodox object to its unilateral addition to the creed.","f":"Why does it matter how the Persons of the Trinity relate to one another?","v":[[243,248,"P17"]]},{"n":197,"t":"Formation of Trinitarian Dogma","s":249,"e":256,"c":"249–256","ph":7,"k":"The councils developed precise terms: one substance, three Persons, distinct in their relations. The Trinity is one; the Persons are really distinct and related.","w":"ousia (οὐσία), 'being'. hypostasis (ὑπόστασις), 'person'. homoousios (ὁμοούσιος), 'of one substance'.","sc":[[["2 Cor 13:14","2CO.13.14"]],[["1 Cor 12:4-6","1CO.12.4-6"]]],"ec":"Shared Nicene heritage. This is also an example of the 'development of doctrine'.","f":"How does careful language guard the mystery rather than reduce it?","v":[[249,256,"P17"]]},{"n":198,"t":"Divine Works and Missions; In Brief","s":257,"e":267,"c":"257–267","ph":7,"k":"The Trinity acts as one, yet each Person acts according to what is proper to him. The goal of all things is our entry into the Trinity's life.","w":"perichōrēsis (περιχώρησις), 'mutual indwelling'.","sc":[[["John 17:21-23","JHN.17.21-23"]],[["John 14:23","JHN.14.23"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"John 14:23: the Trinity wants to make a home in you. What would need tidying?","v":[[257,267,"P17"]]},{"n":199,"t":"God Almighty","s":268,"e":278,"c":"268–278","ph":7,"k":"God's power is universal, loving and mysterious. It shows itself above all in forgiveness and in the weakness of the Cross.","w":"El Shaddai (אֵל שַׁדַּי) / Pantokratōr (Παντοκράτωρ), 'Almighty'.","sc":[[["Gen 17:1","GEN.17.1"]],[["Luke 1:37","LUK.1.37"]],[["2 Cor 12:9","2CO.12.9"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where is God's power being made perfect in your weakness?","v":[[268,278,"P18"]]},{"n":200,"t":"Creation: Why It Matters","s":279,"e":289,"c":"279–289","ph":7,"k":"Questions of origin and purpose are fundamental. Science is welcome, and faith answers questions it cannot. Creation is the first step of salvation history.","w":"bara (בָּרָא), 'create'. In Scripture, used only with God as subject.","sc":[[["Gen 1:1","GEN.1.1"]],[["Heb 11:3","HEB.11.3"]],[["Ps 104:24","PSA.104.24"]]],"ec":"Catholic teaching has no difficulty with an old universe or evolutionary science (283-284), unlike some young-earth Brethren positions.","f":"How do faith and science help each other in your thinking about origins?","v":[[279,289,"P19"]]},{"n":201,"t":"Creation by the Trinity, Out of Nothing","s":290,"e":301,"c":"290–301","ph":7,"k":"The Father creates through the Word in the Spirit, for his glory and our good. He creates out of nothing, in wisdom and freedom, and holds all things in being.","w":"creatio ex nihilo (Latin), 'creation from nothing' (2 Macc 7:28).","sc":[[["2 Macc 7:28","2MA.7.28"]],[["John 1:3","JHN.1.3"]],[["Col 1:16-17","COL.1.16-17"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What does it mean that God holds you in being at every moment?","v":[[290,301,"P19"]]},{"n":202,"t":"Divine Providence","s":302,"e":308,"c":"302–308","ph":7,"k":"Creation is 'in a state of journeying'. God guides it with providence and gives creatures real causality and freedom, so that we share in his work.","w":"pronoia (πρόνοια), 'foresight, providence'.","sc":[[["Matt 6:31-33","MAT.6.31-33"]],[["Rom 8:28","ROM.8.28"]],[["Prov 16:9","PRO.16.9"]]],"ec":"Compare Reformed views of providence. Catholic teaching stresses God's dignity-giving use of secondary causes.","f":"Where can you see providence in your story only in hindsight?","v":[[302,308,"P19"]]},{"n":203,"t":"Providence and the Scandal of Evil","s":309,"e":314,"c":"309–314","ph":7,"k":"No quick answer suffices; the whole of faith responds. God permits evil to bring a greater good from it, as he did supremely with the Cross.","w":"theodicy (Greek theos + dikē), 'justifying God'.","sc":[[["Gen 50:20","GEN.50.20"]],[["Rom 5:20","ROM.5.20"]],[["Rom 8:28","ROM.8.28"]]],"ec":"Shared struggle.","f":"Where have you seen God bring good from evil, and where are you still waiting?","v":[[309,314,"P19"]]},{"n":204,"t":"In Brief: The Creator","s":315,"e":324,"c":"315–324","ph":7,"k":"Summary of the doctrine of creation. A lighter day.","w":"ktisis (κτίσις), 'creation'.","sc":[[["Ps 104","PSA.104"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Pray Psalm 104 outdoors if you can.","v":[[315,324,"P19"]]},{"n":205,"t":"Heaven and Earth: The Angels","s":325,"e":336,"c":"325–336","ph":7,"k":"Angels are real, personal spiritual creatures, servants and messengers of God. Each believer has a guardian angel.","w":"mal'akh (מַלְאָךְ) / angelos (ἄγγελος), 'messenger'.","sc":[[["Heb 1:14","HEB.1.14"]],[["Matt 18:10","MAT.18.10"]],[["Ps 91:11","PSA.91.11"]]],"ec":"Belief in angels is shared. Guardian angels are a more Catholic emphasis.","f":"Thank your guardian angel today.","v":[[325,336,"P1A"]]},{"n":206,"t":"The Visible World","s":337,"e":354,"c":"337–354","ph":7,"k":"God created everything good, in an ordered interdependence, with humans as its summit. Creation is a gift to be cared for. Francis of Assisi's 'Canticle of the Creatures' captures this spirit.","w":"tov me'od (טוֹב מְאֹד), 'very good' (Gen 1:31).","sc":[[["Gen 1:31","GEN.1.31"]],[["Rom 8:19-22","ROM.8.19-22"]],[["Ps 148","PSA.148"]]],"ec":"Shared creation care (see Laudato Si').","f":"What part of creation most leads you to praise God?","v":[[337,354,"P1A"]]},{"n":207,"t":"In the Image of God","s":355,"e":361,"c":"355–361","ph":7,"k":"The human person alone is made in God's image, able to know and love him, with a dignity that belongs to every person.","w":"tselem / demut (צֶלֶם / דְּמוּת), 'image / likeness'.","sc":[[["Gen 1:26-27","GEN.1.26-27"]],[["Col 1:15","COL.1.15"]],[["Col 3:10","COL.3.10"]]],"ec":"Shared foundation for human dignity.","f":"Whose dignity do you find it hardest to see?","v":[[355,361,"P1B"]]},{"n":208,"t":"Body and Soul","s":362,"e":368,"c":"362–368","ph":7,"k":"The human person is a unity of body and spiritual soul. The soul is created directly by God and is immortal.","w":"nephesh (נֶפֶשׁ), 'living being'. psychē (ψυχή), 'soul'.","sc":[[["Gen 2:7","GEN.2.7"]],[["1 Thess 5:23","1TH.5.23"]],[["Matt 10:28","MAT.10.28"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How do you treat your body as part of who you are rather than a container?","v":[[362,368,"P1B"]]},{"n":209,"t":"Male and Female; Original Holiness","s":369,"e":379,"c":"369–379","ph":7,"k":"Man and woman are equal in dignity and made for communion. Our first parents lived in original holiness and harmony.","w":"ezer kenegdo (עֵזֶר כְּנֶגְדּוֹ), 'helper fit for him'. 'Ezer' is also used of God (Ps 121:1-2).","sc":[[["Gen 2:18-25","GEN.2.18-25"]],[["Gen 1:27","GEN.1.27"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What does 'ezer', a word also used of God, say about how men and women help each other?","v":[[369,379,"P1B"]]},{"n":210,"r":1,"q":"Which sacrament of healing or service surprised you most?","items":[[181,"Marriage in the Lord; Virginity for the Kingdom"],[182,"Celebrating Marriage; Consent"],[183,"Mixed Marriages; Effects"],[184,"Unity, Indissolubility, Fidelity"],[185,"Openness to Life; The Domestic Church"],[186,"In Brief: Matrimony"],[187,"Sacramentals"],[188,"Popular Piety"],[189,"Christian Funerals"],[190,"The Creeds"],[191,"I Believe in One God"],[192,"God Reveals His Name"],[193,"God Is Truth and Love"],[194,"Living by Faith in One God"],[195,"The Trinity: Father Revealed by the Son"],[196,"The Spirit Revealed; The Filioque"],[197,"Formation of Trinitarian Dogma"],[198,"Divine Works and Missions; In Brief"],[199,"God Almighty"],[200,"Creation: Why It Matters"],[201,"Creation by the Trinity, Out of Nothing"],[202,"Divine Providence"],[203,"Providence and the Scandal of Evil"],[204,"In Brief: The Creator"],[205,"Heaven and Earth: The Angels"],[206,"The Visible World"],[207,"In the Image of God"],[208,"Body and Soul"],[209,"Male and Female; Original Holiness"]]},{"n":211,"t":"Where Sin Abounded","s":380,"e":390,"c":"380–390","ph":7,"k":"Revelation alone shows us what sin is. We must know Christ to understand sin rightly, and we must know sin to understand Christ.","w":"mysterium iniquitatis (Latin), 'mystery of lawlessness' (2 Thess 2:7).","sc":[[["Rom 5:20","ROM.5.20"]],[["2 Thess 2:7","2TH.2.7"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Why must we know Christ in order to understand sin?","v":[[380,384,"P1B"],[385,390,"P1C"]]},{"n":212,"t":"The Fall of the Angels","s":391,"e":395,"c":"391–395","ph":7,"k":"Satan and the demons were created good but freely and irrevocably rejected God. Satan's power is real but limited.","w":"satan (שָׂטָן), 'adversary'. diabolos (διάβολος), 'slanderer'.","sc":[[["John 8:44","JHN.8.44"]],[["1 John 3:8","1JN.3.8"]],[["Rev 12:9","REV.12.9"]]],"ec":"This is shared with charismatic spiritual warfare, but paragraph 395 tempers any fixation on the devil.","f":"How do you guard against both ignoring and overrating the devil?","v":[[391,395,"P1C"]]},{"n":213,"t":"The First Sin","s":396,"e":401,"c":"396–401","ph":7,"k":"Our first parents freely chose themselves over God, losing trust in him. Their harmony with God, each other and creation was broken.","w":"'adam (אָדָם), 'human, earthling', from 'adamah, 'ground'.","sc":[[["Gen 3:1-13","GEN.3.1-13"]],[["Rom 5:12","ROM.5.12"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where do you notice distrust of God in your own choices?","v":[[396,401,"P1C"]]},{"n":214,"t":"Original Sin in Us","s":402,"e":406,"c":"402–406","ph":7,"k":"All inherit original sin as a deprivation of original holiness, not a personal fault. Baptism erases it, but concupiscence (inclination to sin) remains. Human nature is wounded, not totally corrupted.","w":"concupiscentia (Latin), 'disordered desire'.","sc":[[["Rom 5:12-19","ROM.5.12-19"]],[["Ps 51:5","PSA.51.5"]]],"ec":"Paragraph 405 contrasts with Calvinist 'total depravity': nature wounded rather than totally corrupted.","f":"What difference does 'wounded but not destroyed' make for hope?","v":[[402,406,"P1C"]]},{"n":215,"t":"A Hard Battle; The First Gospel","s":407,"e":412,"c":"407–412","ph":7,"k":"Life is a spiritual battle. Immediately after the Fall God promised redemption: the 'first Gospel'. Through Christ we gain more than Adam lost.","w":"protoevangelium (Greek-Latin), 'first Gospel' (Gen 3:15).","sc":[[["Gen 3:15","GEN.3.15"]],[["Rom 5:20","ROM.5.20"]]],"ec":"Shared messianic reading of Genesis 3:15.","f":"How is Genesis 3:15 hope in the middle of the battle?","v":[[407,412,"P1C"]]},{"n":216,"t":"In Brief: The Fall","s":413,"e":421,"c":"413–421","ph":7,"k":"Summary. The Easter Exsultet sings 'O happy fault' (felix culpa): sin was the occasion for so great a Redeemer.","w":"felix culpa (Latin), 'happy fault'.","sc":[[["Rom 8:20-21","ROM.8.20-21"]],[["Rom 5:20","ROM.5.20"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Can you see a 'happy fault' in your own story?","v":[[413,421,"P1C"]]},{"n":217,"t":"Christian, Recognize Your Dignity","s":1691,"e":1698,"c":"1691–1698","ph":8,"k":"Christian morality begins with who we are in Christ: called to live worthily of the Gospel. Pope Leo the Great urged believers to recognize their dignity. Morality flows from grace, not grace from morality.","w":"agnosce, Christiane, dignitatem tuam (Latin, Leo the Great), 'Christian, recognize your dignity'.","sc":[[["2 Pet 1:4","2PE.1.4"]],[["Phil 1:27","PHP.1.27"]],[["Eph 5:8","EPH.5.8"]]],"ec":"Catholic morality, like evangelical teaching, flows from grace and identity.","f":"What would living 'worthily of the Gospel' change today?","v":[[1691,1698,"P5D"]]},{"n":218,"t":"Image of God and Our Vocation","s":1699,"e":1715,"c":"1699–1715","ph":8,"k":"Our dignity rests on being made in God's image. Christ the new Adam restores that image, and our vocation is to share his beatitude.","w":"eikōn (εἰκών), 'image'.","sc":[[["Col 1:15","COL.1.15"]],[["Rom 8:29","ROM.8.29"]],[["Gen 1:27","GEN.1.27"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How is Christ restoring God's image in you?","v":[[1699,1699,"P5E"],[1700,1700,"P5F"],[1701,1709,"P5G"],[1710,1715,"P5H"]]},{"n":219,"t":"The Beatitudes","s":1716,"e":1724,"c":"1716–1724","ph":8,"k":"The Beatitudes are the heart of Jesus' preaching. They depict his own face and answer the deepest human desire for happiness.","w":"makarios (μακάριος) / ashrei (אַשְׁרֵי), 'blessed, happy'.","sc":[[["Matt 5:3-12","MAT.5.3-12"]],[["Luke 6:20-26","LUK.6.20-26"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Which Beatitude feels most distant from your life right now?","v":[[1716,1717,"P5I"],[1718,1719,"P5J"],[1720,1724,"P5K"]]},{"n":220,"t":"Desire for Happiness; Freedom","s":1725,"e":1738,"c":"1725–1738","ph":8,"k":"God placed the desire for happiness in us in order to draw us to himself. Freedom is the power to act deliberately. Responsibility for an act depends on knowledge and freedom.","w":"eleutheria (ἐλευθερία), 'freedom'.","sc":[[["Gal 5:1","GAL.5.1"],["13","GAL.5.13"]],[["Sir 15:14","SIR.15.14"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How is real freedom different from doing whatever you want?","v":[[1725,1729,"P5L"],[1730,1730,"P5M"],[1731,1738,"P5N"]]},{"n":221,"t":"Freedom and Grace","s":1739,"e":1748,"c":"1739–1748","ph":8,"k":"Sin enslaves, and grace frees. Grace does not compete with freedom; it heals and perfects it.","w":"libertas (Latin), 'freedom'.","sc":[[["John 8:32-36","JHN.8.32-36"]],[["Rom 6:17-18","ROM.6.17-18"]]],"ec":"Luther's Bondage of the Will took a stronger line on human incapacity. Catholic teaching sees grace and freedom cooperating.","f":"Where is grace making you freer?","v":[[1739,1742,"P5O"],[1743,1748,"P5P"]]},{"n":222,"t":"The Morality of Human Acts","s":1749,"e":1761,"c":"1749–1761","ph":8,"k":"An act's morality depends on its object, the intention and the circumstances. A good intention cannot justify an evil act, and some acts are always wrong.","w":"finis operis / finis operantis (Latin), 'end of the act / end of the agent'.","sc":[[["Rom 3:8","ROM.3.8"]],[["Matt 6:2-4","MAT.6.2-4"]]],"ec":"This is shared moral reasoning.","f":"Think of a recent choice. What was the object, the intention and the circumstances?","v":[[1749,1749,"P5Q"],[1750,1754,"P5R"],[1755,1756,"P5S"],[1757,1761,"P5T"]]},{"n":223,"t":"The Passions","s":1762,"e":1775,"c":"1762–1775","ph":8,"k":"Emotions are not good or evil in themselves. They become morally significant through reason and will, and they are integrated in the virtuous person.","w":"pathos (πάθος), 'feeling, passion'.","sc":[[["Mark 3:5","MRK.3.5"]],[["John 11:35","JHN.11.35"]],[["Gal 5:24","GAL.5.24"]]],"ec":"Shared, with a positive view of emotions.","f":"Which emotion most needs to be integrated in you?","v":[[1762,1762,"P5U"],[1763,1766,"P5V"],[1767,1770,"P5W"],[1771,1775,"P5X"]]},{"n":224,"t":"The Human Virtues","s":1803,"e":1811,"c":"1803–1811","ph":8,"k":"Virtues are firm dispositions to do good. The four cardinal virtues are prudence, justice, fortitude and temperance. They grow with effort and grace.","w":"aretē (ἀρετή), 'excellence, virtue'.","sc":[[["Wis 8:7","WIS.8.7"]],[["Phil 4:8","PHP.4.8"]]],"ec":"Wisdom 8:7 lists the cardinal virtues. Virtue ethics is enjoying a revival among Protestants too.","f":"Which cardinal virtue most needs strengthening in you?","v":[[1803,1803,"P64"],[1804,1811,"P65"]]},{"n":225,"t":"Faith and Hope","s":1812,"e":1821,"c":"1812–1821","ph":8,"k":"The theological virtues come from God and lead to him. Faith believes God; hope desires the Kingdom and trusts in Christ's promises.","w":"elpis (ἐλπίς), 'hope, confident expectation'.","sc":[[["Heb 10:23","HEB.10.23"]],[["Rom 5:5","ROM.5.5"]],[["Rom 8:24-25","ROM.8.24-25"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where is your hope weakest, and what nourishes it?","v":[[1812,1821,"P66"]]},{"n":226,"t":"Charity","s":1822,"e":1829,"c":"1822–1829","ph":8,"k":"Charity is love of God above all and of neighbor for God's sake. It is the form of all the virtues and the 'bond of perfection'.","w":"agapē (ἀγάπη), 'self-giving love'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 13","1CO.13"]],[["John 15:12-13","JHN.15.12-13"]],[["Col 3:14","COL.3.14"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Read 1 Corinthians 13 replacing 'love' with your name. Where do you fall short?","v":[[1822,1829,"P66"]]},{"n":227,"t":"Gifts and Fruits of the Spirit; In Brief","s":1830,"e":1845,"c":"1830–1845","ph":8,"k":"The seven gifts of the Spirit make us docile to his promptings. The twelve fruits (in the Vulgate list) are the first-fruits of glory.","w":"karpos (καρπός), 'fruit'.","sc":[[["Gal 5:22-23","GAL.5.22-23"]],[["Isa 11:2-3","ISA.11.2-3"]]],"ec":"This connects naturally with charismatic emphasis.","f":"Which fruit of the Spirit do others see in you, and which is missing?","v":[[1830,1832,"P67"],[1833,1845,"P68"]]},{"n":228,"t":"The Person and Society; Subsidiarity","s":1877,"e":1885,"c":"1877–1885","ph":8,"k":"Persons are made for community. Under subsidiarity, larger bodies should not take over what smaller ones (families, local groups) can do.","w":"subsidium (Latin), 'help, support'.","sc":[[["Exod 18:13-26","EXO.18.13-26"]]],"ec":"Moses delegating to Jethro's judges (Exodus 18) illustrates subsidiarity.","f":"Where should responsibility be more local in your community?","v":[[1877,1877,"P6F"],[1878,1885,"P6G"]]},{"n":229,"t":"Conversion and Society","s":1886,"e":1896,"c":"1886–1896","ph":8,"k":"Social change requires personal conversion as well as just structures. Charity is the greatest social commandment.","w":"metanoia (μετάνοια), 'conversion'.","sc":[[["Mic 6:8","MIC.6.8"]],[["Amos 5:24","AMO.5.24"]]],"ec":"This bridges the personal piety and social action emphases of different traditions.","f":"What personal conversion would help heal your society?","v":[[1886,1889,"P6H"],[1890,1896,"P6I"]]},{"n":230,"t":"Authority","s":1897,"e":1904,"c":"1897–1904","ph":8,"k":"Authority is necessary for society and comes from God. It is legitimate only when it serves the common good by morally legitimate means.","w":"exousia (ἐξουσία), 'authority'.","sc":[[["Rom 13:1-7","ROM.13.1-7"]],[["Acts 5:29","ACT.5.29"]]],"ec":"Shared reading of Romans 13 and Acts 5.","f":"When must we obey God rather than people?","v":[[1897,1904,"P6J"]]},{"n":231,"t":"The Common Good","s":1905,"e":1912,"c":"1905–1912","ph":8,"k":"The common good is the conditions that allow everyone to flourish: respect for persons, social well-being and peace.","w":"bonum commune (Latin), 'common good'.","sc":[[["Jer 29:7","JER.29.7"]],[["1 Cor 12:7","1CO.12.7"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How are you seeking the welfare of your city?","v":[[1905,1912,"P6K"]]},{"n":232,"t":"Participation; In Brief","s":1913,"e":1927,"c":"1913–1927","ph":8,"k":"Everyone shares responsibility for the common good, through work, family and civic life.","w":"politeuomai (πολιτεύομαι), 'live as citizens' (Phil 1:27).","sc":[[["Phil 1:27","PHP.1.27"]],[["Rom 12:4-8","ROM.12.4-8"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What one civic responsibility could you take more seriously?","v":[[1913,1917,"P6L"],[1918,1927,"P6M"]]},{"n":233,"t":"Social Justice: Respect, Equality, Differences","s":1928,"e":1938,"c":"1928–1938","ph":8,"k":"Social justice respects the dignity and rights of every person. Equal in dignity, people differ in gifts, and differences call for solidarity, not exploitation.","w":"mishpat / tsedaqah (מִשְׁפָּט / צְדָקָה), 'justice / righteousness'.","sc":[[["Jas 2:1-9","JAS.2.1-9"]],[["Luke 16:19-31","LUK.16.19-31"]]],"ec":"Shared prophetic tradition.","f":"Where do you see injustice that you have learned to ignore?","v":[[1928,1928,"P6N"],[1929,1933,"P6O"],[1934,1938,"P6P"]]},{"n":234,"t":"Solidarity","s":1939,"e":1948,"c":"1939–1948","ph":8,"k":"Solidarity is friendship and social charity: sharing material and spiritual goods across nations.","w":"solidarity (Latin solidus, 'whole, firm').","sc":[[["Gal 6:2","GAL.6.2"]],[["Acts 4:32-35","ACT.4.32-35"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Whose burden can you help carry this week?","v":[[1939,1942,"P6Q"],[1943,1948,"P6R"]]},{"n":235,"t":"'Teacher, What Must I Do?'","s":2052,"e":2055,"c":"2052–2055","ph":9,"k":"Jesus answers the rich young man: keep the commandments, then follow me. He sums them up in love of God and neighbor.","w":"aseret ha-devarim (עֲשֶׂרֶת הַדְּבָרִים), 'the Ten Words'.","sc":[[["Matt 19:16-21","MAT.19.16-21"]],[["Matt 22:37-40","MAT.22.37-40"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What 'one thing' might Jesus still ask of you?","v":[[2052,2055,"P78"]]},{"n":236,"t":"The Decalogue in Scripture","s":2056,"e":2063,"c":"2056–2063","ph":9,"k":"The Ten Words come within the covenant. They begin with liberation ('I brought you out of Egypt'), so obedience is a response to grace.","w":"dekalogos (δεκάλογος), 'ten words'.","sc":[[["Exod 20:1-17","EXO.20.1-17"]],[["Deut 5:6-21","DEU.5.6-21"]]],"ec":"Catholics and Lutherans follow Augustine's numbering. Reformed Christians and Anglicans count the ban on images as a separate second commandment.","f":"How does 'I brought you out' change how you hear 'you shall'?","v":[[2056,2063,"P78"]]},{"n":237,"t":"Tradition, Unity and Obligation","s":2064,"e":2074,"c":"2064–2074","ph":9,"k":"The Church has always taught the Decalogue. The commandments form an organic whole and express natural law, binding gravely in serious matters.","w":"lex naturalis (Latin), 'natural law'.","sc":[[["Jas 2:10-11","JAS.2.10-11"]],[["Rom 2:15","ROM.2.15"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Why does breaking one commandment wound the whole?","v":[[2064,2074,"P78"]]},{"n":238,"t":"In Brief: The Ten Commandments","s":2075,"e":2082,"c":"2075–2082","ph":9,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"mitzvah (מִצְוָה), 'commandment'.","sc":[[["John 14:15","JHN.14.15"]],[["15:5","JHN.15.5"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Memorize the Ten Commandments in their Catholic numbering.","v":[[2075,2082,"P79"]]},{"n":239,"t":"First Commandment: Faith","s":2083,"e":2089,"c":"2083–2089","ph":9,"k":"We owe God faith. Sins against faith include voluntary doubt, incredulity, heresy, apostasy and schism.","w":"Shema (שְׁמַע), 'Hear!'.","sc":[[["Deut 6:4-5","DEU.6.4-5"],["13","DEU.6.13"]],[["Matt 4:10","MAT.4.10"]]],"ec":"Shared, with the Catholic definitions of heresy and schism.","f":"How do you guard and nourish your faith?","v":[[2083,2083,"P7A"],[2084,2089,"P7C"]]},{"n":240,"r":1,"q":"How has the doctrine of creation and the Fall shaped how you see human nature?","items":[[211,"Where Sin Abounded"],[212,"The Fall of the Angels"],[213,"The First Sin"],[214,"Original Sin in Us"],[215,"A Hard Battle; The First Gospel"],[216,"In Brief: The Fall"],[217,"Christian, Recognize Your Dignity"],[218,"Image of God and Our Vocation"],[219,"The Beatitudes"],[220,"Desire for Happiness; Freedom"],[221,"Freedom and Grace"],[222,"The Morality of Human Acts"],[223,"The Passions"],[224,"The Human Virtues"],[225,"Faith and Hope"],[226,"Charity"],[227,"Gifts and Fruits of the Spirit; In Brief"],[228,"The Person and Society; Subsidiarity"],[229,"Conversion and Society"],[230,"Authority"],[231,"The Common Good"],[232,"Participation; In Brief"],[233,"Social Justice: Respect, Equality, Differences"],[234,"Solidarity"],[235,"'Teacher, What Must I Do?'"],[236,"The Decalogue in Scripture"],[237,"Tradition, Unity and Obligation"],[238,"In Brief: The Ten Commandments"],[239,"First Commandment: Faith"]]},{"n":241,"t":"Hope and Charity","s":2090,"e":2094,"c":"2090–2094","ph":9,"k":"Despair and presumption both offend hope. Indifference, ingratitude, lukewarmness, acedia and hatred of God offend charity.","w":"acedia (Latin, from Greek ἀκηδία), 'spiritual sloth'.","sc":[[["Rom 5:5","ROM.5.5"]],[["1 John 4:19","1JN.4.19"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Do you lean toward despair or presumption?","v":[[2090,2094,"P7C"]]},{"n":242,"t":"Adoration, Prayer and Sacrifice","s":2095,"e":2100,"c":"2095–2100","ph":9,"k":"We owe God worship: adoration, prayer, and the sacrifice of a contrite heart united to Christ's sacrifice.","w":"proskyneō (προσκυνέω), 'to bow down, worship'.","sc":[[["Luke 4:8","LUK.4.8"]],[["Ps 51:17","PSA.51.17"]],[["John 4:23-24","JHN.4.23-24"]]],"ec":"Shared. Eucharistic adoration is distinctively Catholic.","f":"Spend ten minutes in adoration this week.","v":[[2095,2100,"P7D"]]},{"n":243,"t":"Promises and Vows","s":2101,"e":2103,"c":"2101–2103","ph":9,"k":"Promises made to God must be kept. A vow is a deliberate, free promise of a greater good, such as religious vows.","w":"neder (נֶדֶר), 'vow'.","sc":[[["Ps 116:14","PSA.116.14"]],[["Eccl 5:4-5","ECC.5.4-5"]]],"ec":"Shared biblical practice.","f":"Is there a promise to God you need to keep?","v":[[2101,2103,"P7D"]]},{"n":244,"t":"Religious Freedom","s":2104,"e":2109,"c":"2104–2109","ph":9,"k":"Everyone must seek the truth and embrace it. No one may be forced to act against conscience in religious matters, within just limits (Dignitatis Humanae).","w":"libertas religiosa (Latin), 'religious freedom'.","sc":[[["John 8:32","JHN.8.32"]],[["Acts 4:19-20","ACT.4.19-20"]]],"ec":"This is a Vatican II development, close to Free Church convictions.","f":"How do you share faith while respecting others' freedom?","v":[[2104,2109,"P7D"]]},{"n":245,"t":"Superstition and Idolatry","s":2110,"e":2114,"c":"2110–2114","ph":9,"k":"Superstition distorts worship. Idolatry divinizes what is not God: power, money, pleasure, the state.","w":"eidōlolatria (εἰδωλολατρία), 'idol worship'.","sc":[[["Exod 20:3-5","EXO.20.3-5"]],[["Col 3:5","COL.3.5"]],[["1 John 5:21","1JN.5.21"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What modern idol tempts you most?","v":[[2110,2114,"P7E"]]},{"n":246,"t":"Divination and Magic","s":2115,"e":2117,"c":"2115–2117","ph":9,"k":"Horoscopes, fortune-telling, spiritism and magic are forbidden because they seek power over time and others apart from God.","w":"kesem (קֶסֶם), 'divination'.","sc":[[["Deut 18:10-14","DEU.18.10-14"]],[["Acts 19:18-19","ACT.19.18-19"]]],"ec":"This is shared, and relevant to discerning practices in charismatic circles.","f":"How do you tell trust in providence from a desire to control?","v":[[2115,2117,"P7E"]]},{"n":247,"t":"Irreligion and Atheism","s":2118,"e":2128,"c":"2118–2128","ph":9,"k":"Tempting God, sacrilege and simony are sins of irreligion. Atheism takes many forms, and believers can share responsibility for it through bad witness.","w":"simonia (from Simon Magus, Acts 8:18-24), 'buying spiritual things'.","sc":[[["Acts 8:18-24","ACT.8.18-24"]],[["Ps 14:1","PSA.14.1"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How might your own witness make faith more or less credible?","v":[[2118,2128,"P7E"]]},{"n":248,"t":"Sacred Images","s":2129,"e":2132,"c":"2129–2132","ph":9,"k":"Because of the Incarnation, images of Christ and the saints are legitimate. The honor goes to the person depicted, not to the image itself (Nicaea II).","w":"proskynēsis timētikē (προσκύνησις τιμητική), 'veneration of honor', distinct from latreia (worship).","sc":[[["Exod 20:4-5","EXO.20.4-5"]],[["Num 21:8-9","NUM.21.8-9"]],[["John 3:14","JHN.3.14"]]],"ec":"This is the classic Reformed objection. Note that God commanded the bronze serpent.","f":"Can an image help you pray without becoming an idol? How?","v":[[2129,2132,"P7F"]]},{"n":249,"t":"In Brief: First Commandment","s":2133,"e":2141,"c":"2133–2141","ph":9,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"latreia (λατρεία), 'worship due to God alone'.","sc":[[["Matt 4:10","MAT.4.10"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What is first in your life, honestly?","v":[[2133,2141,"P7G"]]},{"n":250,"t":"Second Commandment: The Holy Name","s":2142,"e":2149,"c":"2142–2149","ph":9,"k":"God's name is holy. We should use it with reverence and never misuse it in blasphemy, curses or false oaths.","w":"shem (שֵׁם), 'name'. It stands for the person.","sc":[[["Exod 20:7","EXO.20.7"]],[["Ps 8:1","PSA.8.1"]],[["Matt 6:9","MAT.6.9"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How do you speak of God when no one is listening?","v":[[2142,2149,"P7I"]]},{"n":251,"t":"Oaths","s":2150,"e":2155,"c":"2150–2155","ph":9,"k":"False oaths and perjury are grave. Oaths may be taken for grave and just reasons, such as in court.","w":"horkos (ὅρκος), 'oath'.","sc":[[["Matt 5:33-37","MAT.5.33-37"]],[["Jas 5:12","JAS.5.12"]]],"ec":"Anabaptists and some Brethren refuse oaths entirely. Catholic teaching allows grave, just oaths.","f":"Is your 'yes' a reliable 'yes'?","v":[[2150,2155,"P7J"]]},{"n":252,"t":"Your Christian Name; In Brief","s":2156,"e":2167,"c":"2156–2167","ph":9,"k":"Baptism gives a Christian name. God calls each person by name.","w":"onoma (ὄνομα), 'name'.","sc":[[["Isa 43:1","ISA.43.1"]],[["Rev 2:17","REV.2.17"]]],"ec":"Shared. If you are choosing a Confirmation name, today is a good day to think about it.","f":"What saint's name might you take, and why?","v":[[2156,2159,"P7K"],[2160,2167,"P7L"]]},{"n":253,"t":"Third Commandment: The Sabbath","s":2168,"e":2173,"c":"2168–2173","ph":9,"k":"The Sabbath recalls creation and liberation. Jesus is its Lord and restored its meaning as a day for doing good.","w":"Shabbat (שַׁבָּת), 'rest, cessation'.","sc":[[["Exod 20:8-11","EXO.20.8-11"]],[["Deut 5:12-15","DEU.5.12-15"]],[["Mark 2:27-28","MRK.2.27-28"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What does Sabbath rest look like for you?","v":[[2168,2173,"P7N"]]},{"n":254,"t":"The Lord's Day and the Sunday Eucharist","s":2174,"e":2179,"c":"2174–2179","ph":9,"k":"Sunday, the day of the Resurrection and the 'eighth day', fulfills the Sabbath. The Sunday Eucharist is the heart of the Church's life. The parish is the ordinary place for it.","w":"dies Domini (Latin), 'Lord's day'.","sc":[[["Acts 20:7","ACT.20.7"]],[["Rev 1:10","REV.1.10"]],[["Heb 10:25","HEB.10.25"]]],"ec":"The Brethren keep weekly breaking of bread. This is strong common ground.","f":"How can your parish become your spiritual home?","v":[[2174,2179,"P7O"]]},{"n":255,"t":"The Sunday Obligation","s":2180,"e":2183,"c":"2180–2183","ph":9,"k":"Catholics must attend Mass on Sundays and holy days, unless excused for serious reasons. Deliberately missing is a grave sin.","w":"obligatio (Latin), 'binding'.","sc":[[["Heb 10:24-25","HEB.10.24-25"]]],"ec":"For converts this is a change from voluntary to obligatory attendance.","f":"What could make Sunday Mass more than an obligation for you?","v":[[2180,2183,"P7O"]]},{"n":256,"t":"Rest and Leisure; In Brief","s":2184,"e":2195,"c":"2184–2195","ph":9,"k":"Sunday rest protects family, culture, worship and the poor. Employers should respect it.","w":"anapausis (ἀνάπαυσις), 'rest' (Matt 11:28).","sc":[[["Matt 11:28-30","MAT.11.28-30"]],[["Isa 58:13-14","ISA.58.13-14"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What work could you lay down next Sunday?","v":[[2184,2188,"P7O"],[2189,2195,"P7P"]]},{"n":257,"t":"Fourth Commandment: Honor Parents","s":2196,"e":2200,"c":"2196–2200","ph":9,"k":"The second tablet begins with family. Honoring parents includes other legitimate authorities, and brings blessing.","w":"kabed (כַּבֵּד), 'honor'. The root means 'heavy, weighty'.","sc":[[["Exod 20:12","EXO.20.12"]],[["Eph 6:1-3","EPH.6.1-3"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How can you give 'weight' to your parents or elders?","v":[[2196,2196,"P7Q"],[2197,2200,"P7R"]]},{"n":258,"t":"The Family in God's Plan","s":2201,"e":2206,"c":"2201–2206","ph":9,"k":"The family is a communion of persons, an image of the Trinity. The Christian family is a domestic church.","w":"mishpachah (מִשְׁפָּחָה), 'family, clan'.","sc":[[["Eph 3:14-15","EPH.3.14-15"]],[["Gen 2:24","GEN.2.24"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What makes a family an image of the Trinity?","v":[[2201,2206,"P7S"]]},{"n":259,"t":"Family and Society","s":2207,"e":2213,"c":"2207–2213","ph":9,"k":"The family is the original cell of society. Society and the state must support families.","w":"domus (Latin), 'household'.","sc":[[["Ps 127:1","PSA.127.1"]],[["Sir 7:27-28","SIR.7.27-28"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How can your community better support families?","v":[[2207,2213,"P7T"]]},{"n":260,"t":"Duties of Children","s":2214,"e":2220,"c":"2214–2220","ph":9,"k":"Children owe respect, gratitude, obedience while young, and care for parents in old age.","w":"pietas (Latin), 'dutiful love'.","sc":[[["Sir 3:2-16","SIR.3.2-16"]],[["Prov 1:8","PRO.1.8"]]],"ec":"Sirach 3 is a deuterocanonical treasure on honoring parents.","f":"What care do your parents or elders need now?","v":[[2214,2220,"P7U"]]},{"n":261,"t":"Duties of Parents: Education","s":2221,"e":2226,"c":"2221–2226","ph":9,"k":"Parents are the first educators, in virtue and above all in faith.","w":"paideia (παιδεία), 'upbringing, education'.","sc":[[["Eph 6:4","EPH.6.4"]],[["Deut 6:6-7","DEU.6.6-7"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Who were your first teachers in faith?","v":[[2221,2226,"P7U"]]},{"n":262,"t":"Parents' Rights; Family and Kingdom","s":2227,"e":2233,"c":"2227–2233","ph":9,"k":"Parents have the right to choose schools. Family ties are important but not absolute: following Christ comes first.","w":"akoloutheō (ἀκολουθέω), 'to follow'.","sc":[[["Matt 10:37","MAT.10.37"]],[["Mark 3:31-35","MRK.3.31-35"]]],"ec":"This is relevant to anyone whose faith journey is unfamiliar to their family.","f":"How do you honor family while following Christ first?","v":[[2227,2231,"P7U"],[2232,2233,"P7V"]]},{"n":263,"t":"Authorities in Society","s":2234,"e":2237,"c":"2234–2237","ph":9,"k":"Authority is service. Leaders must respect rights, act justly and avoid favoritism.","w":"exousia (ἐξουσία), 'authority'.","sc":[[["Matt 20:26","MAT.20.26"]],[["Rom 13:1-4","ROM.13.1-4"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Pray for your country's leaders today.","v":[[2234,2237,"P7W"]]},{"n":264,"t":"Duties of Citizens","s":2238,"e":2243,"c":"2238–2243","ph":9,"k":"Citizens owe respect, taxes, voting and defense. Conscience may refuse unjust commands, and armed resistance is permitted only under strict conditions.","w":"politēs (πολίτης), 'citizen'.","sc":[[["Rom 13:7","ROM.13.7"]],[["Acts 5:29","ACT.5.29"]],[["1 Pet 2:13-17","1PE.2.13-17"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where does civic duty meet conscience in your context?","v":[[2238,2243,"P7W"]]},{"n":265,"t":"The Church and the Political Community; In Brief","s":2244,"e":2257,"c":"2244–2257","ph":9,"k":"Societies need an understanding of the person. The Church judges political matters morally without being tied to any party.","w":"polis (πόλις), 'city'.","sc":[[["Matt 22:21","MAT.22.21"]],[["Phil 3:20","PHP.3.20"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How do you keep your citizenship in heaven primary?","v":[[2244,2246,"P7W"],[2247,2257,"P7X"]]},{"n":266,"t":"Fifth Commandment: Respect for Life","s":2258,"e":2262,"c":"2258–2262","ph":9,"k":"Human life is sacred from its beginning because it involves God's creative action. The direct killing of the innocent is forbidden.","w":"lo tirtsach (לֹא תִּרְצָח), 'you shall not murder'.","sc":[[["Gen 4:10","GEN.4.10"]],[["Exod 20:13","EXO.20.13"]],[["Matt 5:21-22","MAT.5.21-22"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Jesus links murder to anger (Matt 5:22). Where is anger in you?","v":[[2258,2258,"P7Y"],[2259,2262,"P7Z"]]},{"n":267,"t":"Legitimate Defense; The Death Penalty","s":2263,"e":2267,"c":"2263–2267","ph":9,"k":"Self-defense is legitimate, and protecting others can be a duty. Since the 2018 revision, the CCC teaches that the death penalty is inadmissible because of the dignity of the person.","w":"legitima defensio (Latin), 'legitimate defense'.","sc":[[["Gen 9:6","GEN.9.6"]],[["Ezek 33:11","EZK.33.11"]]],"ec":"The 2018 revision is an example of development. Protestant views vary widely.","f":"How does human dignity shape your view of justice?","v":[[2263,2267,"P7Z"]]},{"n":268,"t":"Murder and Abortion","s":2268,"e":2275,"c":"2268–2275","ph":9,"k":"Murder is gravely wrong. Human life must be protected from conception, so direct abortion is gravely contrary to the moral law. The Church offers mercy to all who repent.","w":"nephesh chayyah (נֶפֶשׁ חַיָּה), 'living being'.","sc":[[["Ps 139:13-16","PSA.139.13-16"]],[["Jer 1:5","JER.1.5"]]],"ec":"Broadly shared by evangelicals; Anglican views vary.","f":"How can you support mothers and families in difficult pregnancies?","v":[[2268,2275,"P7Z"]]},{"n":269,"t":"Euthanasia and Suicide","s":2276,"e":2283,"c":"2276–2283","ph":9,"k":"Euthanasia is unacceptable, but refusing burdensome treatment is allowed. Suicide contradicts the love of self and God, yet grave psychological disturbance can diminish responsibility. The Church prays for those who have died by suicide, trusting in God's mercy.","w":"euthanasia (εὐθανασία), 'good death'. Paragraph 2283 voices real hope for those who have died by suicide.","sc":[[["Ps 31:15","PSA.31.15"]],[["Rom 14:7-8","ROM.14.7-8"]]],"ec":"Shared in its essentials.","f":"If this topic touches you personally, speak with your priest or a trusted friend.","v":[[2276,2283,"P7Z"]]},{"n":270,"r":1,"q":"Which of the commandments so far has shown you the greatest need for grace?","items":[[241,"Hope and Charity"],[242,"Adoration, Prayer and Sacrifice"],[243,"Promises and Vows"],[244,"Religious Freedom"],[245,"Superstition and Idolatry"],[246,"Divination and Magic"],[247,"Irreligion and Atheism"],[248,"Sacred Images"],[249,"In Brief: First Commandment"],[250,"Second Commandment: The Holy Name"],[251,"Oaths"],[252,"Your Christian Name; In Brief"],[253,"Third Commandment: The Sabbath"],[254,"The Lord's Day and the Sunday Eucharist"],[255,"The Sunday Obligation"],[256,"Rest and Leisure; In Brief"],[257,"Fourth Commandment: Honor Parents"],[258,"The Family in God's Plan"],[259,"Family and Society"],[260,"Duties of Children"],[261,"Duties of Parents: Education"],[262,"Parents' Rights; Family and Kingdom"],[263,"Authorities in Society"],[264,"Duties of Citizens"],[265,"The Church and the Political Community; In Brief"],[266,"Fifth Commandment: Respect for Life"],[267,"Legitimate Defense; The Death Penalty"],[268,"Murder and Abortion"],[269,"Euthanasia and Suicide"]]},{"n":271,"t":"Scandal","s":2284,"e":2287,"c":"2284–2287","ph":9,"k":"Scandal leads others into sin. It is grave when caused by those who teach, or by laws and structures.","w":"skandalon (σκάνδαλον), 'stumbling block'.","sc":[[["Matt 18:6-7","MAT.18.6-7"]],[["Rom 14:13","ROM.14.13"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Is there anything in your life that could be a stumbling block to others?","v":[[2284,2287,"P80"]]},{"n":272,"t":"Respect for Health","s":2288,"e":2291,"c":"2288–2291","ph":9,"k":"Care for health is reasonable, but the body is not an idol. Avoid excess in food, alcohol, tobacco and drugs, and avoid reckless risk.","w":"sōphrosynē (σωφροσύνη), 'temperance, sound-mindedness'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 6:19-20","1CO.6.19-20"]],[["Titus 2:12","TIT.2.12"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where do you need more temperance?","v":[[2288,2291,"P80"]]},{"n":273,"t":"Research, Bodily Integrity and the Dead","s":2292,"e":2301,"c":"2292–2301","ph":9,"k":"Scientific research must respect the person. Organ donation can be noble. The bodies of the dead deserve respect, and cremation is permitted if it does not deny the resurrection.","w":"integritas (Latin), 'wholeness'.","sc":[[["Gen 2:7","GEN.2.7"]],[["Tob 1:16-20","TOB.1.16-20"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How does belief in the resurrection shape how you treat the body?","v":[[2292,2301,"P80"]]},{"n":274,"t":"Peace and Anger","s":2302,"e":2306,"c":"2302–2306","ph":9,"k":"Christ is our peace. Anger that desires revenge is a sin, while hatred of a neighbor is gravely wrong. Peace is the 'tranquility of order'.","w":"shalom (שָׁלוֹם), 'wholeness, peace'.","sc":[[["Eph 2:14","EPH.2.14"]],[["Matt 5:9","MAT.5.9"],["44","MAT.5.44"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Who do you need to make peace with?","v":[[2302,2306,"P81"]]},{"n":275,"t":"Avoiding War","s":2307,"e":2317,"c":"2307–2317","ph":9,"k":"War is to be avoided. Strict 'just war' conditions govern legitimate defense. Arms races and atrocities are condemned.","w":"bellum iustum (Latin), 'just war'.","sc":[[["Isa 2:4","ISA.2.4"]],[["Matt 26:52","MAT.26.52"]]],"ec":"Pacifist traditions (Anabaptist, some Brethren) differ from this.","f":"How can you be a peacemaker in your corner of the world?","v":[[2307,2317,"P81"]]},{"n":276,"t":"In Brief: Fifth Commandment","s":2318,"e":2330,"c":"2318–2330","ph":9,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"eirēnē (εἰρήνη), 'peace'.","sc":[[["Matt 5:9","MAT.5.9"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Pray for peace in a specific conflict today.","v":[[2318,2330,"P82"]]},{"n":277,"t":"Sixth Commandment: Male and Female","s":2331,"e":2336,"c":"2331–2336","ph":9,"k":"God made us male and female, with sexuality integral to the person, and called us to communion.","w":"ish / ishah (אִישׁ / אִשָּׁה), 'man / woman'.","sc":[[["Gen 1:27","GEN.1.27"]],[["Gen 2:24","GEN.2.24"]],[["Matt 19:4-6","MAT.19.4-6"]]],"ec":"Shared biblical anthropology.","f":"How does seeing sexuality as a call to communion change things?","v":[[2331,2336,"P84"]]},{"n":278,"t":"The Virtue of Chastity","s":2337,"e":2345,"c":"2337–2345","ph":9,"k":"Chastity integrates sexuality into the whole person. It requires self-mastery, training and grace, and it grows in stages.","w":"castitas (Latin), 'purity, integrity'.","sc":[[["Titus 2:12","TIT.2.12"]],[["Gal 5:22-23","GAL.5.22-23"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What helps you grow in self-mastery?","v":[[2337,2345,"P85"]]},{"n":279,"t":"Chastity and Friendship","s":2346,"e":2350,"c":"2346–2350","ph":9,"k":"Chastity blossoms in friendship. Every state of life (married, single, consecrated) lives chastity in its own way.","w":"philia (φιλία), 'friendship love'.","sc":[[["John 15:13-15","JHN.15.13-15"]],[["1 Sam 18:1-3","1SA.18.1-3"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How do your friendships help you live faithfully?","v":[[2346,2350,"P85"]]},{"n":280,"t":"Offenses Against Chastity","s":2351,"e":2356,"c":"2351–2356","ph":9,"k":"Lust, masturbation, fornication, pornography, prostitution and rape are offenses against chastity. Culpability can be reduced by immaturity, habit or psychological factors.","w":"porneia (πορνεία), 'sexual immorality'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 6:18-20","1CO.6.18-20"]],[["Matt 5:27-28","MAT.5.27-28"]]],"ec":"Shared, broadly, with evangelicals.","f":"Where do you need grace and accountability?","v":[[2351,2356,"P85"]]},{"n":281,"t":"Chastity and Homosexual Inclination","s":2357,"e":2359,"c":"2357–2359","ph":9,"k":"The Church distinguishes inclination from acts. People with same-sex attraction must be treated with respect and sensitivity, and they are called to chastity.","w":"dignitas (Latin), 'worth, dignity'.","sc":[[["Rom 1:26-27","ROM.1.26-27"]],[["1 Cor 6:9-11","1CO.6.9-11"]]],"ec":"Positions differ sharply across Anglican provinces.","f":"How can the Church combine truth and love here?","v":[[2357,2359,"P85"]]},{"n":282,"t":"Conjugal Love","s":2360,"e":2365,"c":"2360–2365","ph":9,"k":"Sexual union is a sign of the covenant and achieves both union and openness to life. Fidelity is essential.","w":"berit (בְּרִית), 'covenant'.","sc":[[["Song 8:6-7","SNG.8.6-7"]],[["Eph 5:31-32","EPH.5.31-32"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How is marital love a sign of God's covenant?","v":[[2360,2365,"P86"]]},{"n":283,"t":"Fruitfulness and the Regulation of Births","s":2366,"e":2372,"c":"2366–2372","ph":9,"k":"Marriage is open to life. Natural family planning is permitted for just reasons; contraception separates the unitive and procreative meanings and is excluded.","w":"Humanae Vitae (1968), 'Of Human Life'.","sc":[[["Gen 1:28","GEN.1.28"]],[["Ps 127:3","PSA.127.3"]]],"ec":"Most Protestants and Anglicans accepted contraception after the Lambeth Conference of 1930. This is a significant difference.","f":"What questions does this teaching raise for you?","v":[[2366,2372,"P86"]]},{"n":284,"t":"The Gift of a Child; Infertility","s":2373,"e":2379,"c":"2373–2379","ph":9,"k":"A child is a gift, not a right. Some reproductive techniques are morally unacceptable. Infertile couples can live fruitfulness in other ways, such as adoption.","w":"donum (Latin), 'gift'.","sc":[[["1 Sam 1:10-11","1SA.1.10-11"]],[["Ps 113:9","PSA.113.9"]]],"ec":"Shared compassion. The Catholic position on IVF is distinctive.","f":"Pray for couples carrying the pain of infertility.","v":[[2373,2379,"P86"]]},{"n":285,"t":"Adultery and Divorce","s":2380,"e":2386,"c":"2380–2386","ph":9,"k":"Adultery breaks the covenant. Divorce does not dissolve a valid sacramental marriage. The innocent spouse suffers unjustly and is to be supported.","w":"moicheia (μοιχεία), 'adultery'.","sc":[[["Matt 5:31-32","MAT.5.31-32"]],[["Mark 10:11-12","MRK.10.11-12"]],[["Mal 2:16","MAL.2.16"]]],"ec":"Many Protestant traditions allow remarriage after divorce.","f":"How can the Church better accompany those wounded by divorce?","v":[[2380,2386,"P87"]]},{"n":286,"t":"Other Offenses; In Brief","s":2387,"e":2400,"c":"2387–2400","ph":9,"k":"Polygamy, incest, cohabitation and 'free union' offend the dignity of marriage.","w":"hagnos (ἁγνός), 'pure'.","sc":[[["Heb 13:4","HEB.13.4"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What does Hebrews 13:4 call the Church to honor?","v":[[2387,2391,"P87"],[2392,2400,"P88"]]},{"n":287,"t":"Seventh Commandment: The Universal Destination of Goods","s":2401,"e":2406,"c":"2401–2406","ph":9,"k":"The earth's goods are for everyone. Private property is legitimate but serves the common good.","w":"lo tignov (לֹא תִּגְנֹב), 'you shall not steal'.","sc":[[["Exod 20:15","EXO.20.15"]],[["Acts 4:32","ACT.4.32"]]],"ec":"Catholic social teaching has its own balance between property and the common good.","f":"Are your possessions serving others?","v":[[2401,2401,"P89"],[2402,2406,"P8A"]]},{"n":288,"t":"Respect for Goods and Persons","s":2407,"e":2414,"c":"2407–2414","ph":9,"k":"Theft, fraud, unjust wages and breaking contracts are wrong. Restitution is required. Gambling can become sinful, and slavery is always wrong.","w":"restitutio (Latin), 'restoring'.","sc":[[["Luke 19:8","LUK.19.8"]],[["Lev 19:13","LEV.19.13"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Is there anything you need to restore?","v":[[2407,2414,"P8B"]]},{"n":289,"t":"Creation and Animals","s":2415,"e":2418,"c":"2415–2418","ph":9,"k":"Stewardship of creation is a moral duty. Animals deserve kindness, though they may be used for legitimate needs.","w":"oikonomia (οἰκονομία), 'household management, stewardship'.","sc":[[["Gen 2:15","GEN.2.15"]],[["Prov 12:10","PRO.12.10"]]],"ec":"Shared creation care.","f":"What one ecological habit could you change?","v":[[2415,2418,"P8B"]]},{"n":290,"t":"The Social Doctrine of the Church","s":2419,"e":2425,"c":"2419–2425","ph":9,"k":"The Church judges economic and social matters in light of the Gospel. It rejects both atheistic collectivism and unregulated capitalism.","w":"Rerum Novarum (1891), the founding document of modern Catholic social teaching.","sc":[[["Luke 4:18-19","LUK.4.18-19"]],[["Jas 5:1-6","JAS.5.1-6"]]],"ec":"Distinctively Catholic in its systematic form.","f":"How does faith shape your economic choices?","v":[[2419,2425,"P8C"]]},{"n":291,"t":"Economic Activity and Justice","s":2426,"e":2436,"c":"2426–2436","ph":9,"k":"Work has dignity. A just wage, the right to strike under conditions, and access to employment are owed. Work should serve the person.","w":"ergon (ἔργον), 'work'.","sc":[[["2 Thess 3:10-12","2TH.3.10-12"]],[["Col 3:23","COL.3.23"]]],"ec":"There is common ground with the Protestant work ethic, plus a strong Catholic emphasis on just wages.","f":"How is your work an act of worship?","v":[[2426,2436,"P8D"]]},{"n":292,"t":"Justice Among Nations","s":2437,"e":2442,"c":"2437–2442","ph":9,"k":"Rich nations owe solidarity to poorer ones. Development must be integral, not only economic.","w":"solidarity (Latin solidus, 'whole').","sc":[[["Amos 8:4-7","AMO.8.4-7"]],[["2 Cor 8:13-15","2CO.8.13-15"]]],"ec":"This is relevant to Caribbean and global South economies.","f":"What does solidarity between nations look like in your region?","v":[[2437,2442,"P8E"]]},{"n":293,"t":"Love for the Poor","s":2443,"e":2449,"c":"2443–2449","ph":9,"k":"Love for the poor is incompatible with an immoderate love of riches. Works of mercy, corporal and spiritual, are central to Christian life.","w":"eleēmosynē (ἐλεημοσύνη), 'mercy, alms'.","sc":[[["Matt 25:31-46","MAT.25.31-46"]],[["Luke 16:19-31","LUK.16.19-31"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Pick one work of mercy to do this week.","v":[[2443,2449,"P8F"]]},{"n":294,"t":"In Brief: Seventh Commandment","s":2450,"e":2463,"c":"2450–2463","ph":9,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"mammon (Aramaic, מָמוֹנָא), 'wealth'.","sc":[[["Matt 6:24","MAT.6.24"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Who is your master, God or wealth?","v":[[2450,2463,"P8G"]]},{"n":295,"t":"Eighth Commandment: Living in the Truth","s":2464,"e":2470,"c":"2464–2470","ph":9,"k":"God is truth, and Christ is the Truth. Truthfulness is a virtue owed to others.","w":"aletheia (ἀλήθεια), 'truth', literally 'unhiddenness'.","sc":[[["John 14:6","JHN.14.6"]],[["Eph 4:25","EPH.4.25"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where do you shade the truth?","v":[[2464,2464,"P8H"],[2465,2470,"P8I"]]},{"n":296,"t":"Bearing Witness; Martyrdom","s":2471,"e":2474,"c":"2471–2474","ph":9,"k":"Christians must bear witness to the truth. Martyrdom is the supreme witness.","w":"martys (μάρτυς), 'witness'.","sc":[[["Acts 1:8","ACT.1.8"]],[["Rev 2:10","REV.2.10"]]],"ec":"Shared reverence for martyrs.","f":"Where is witness costly for you?","v":[[2471,2474,"P8J"]]},{"n":297,"t":"Offenses: Rash Judgment, Detraction, Calumny","s":2475,"e":2481,"c":"2475–2481","ph":9,"k":"False witness, rash judgment, detraction (revealing faults without reason), calumny (lies about others), flattery and boasting all offend truth.","w":"diabolē (διαβολή), 'slander'.","sc":[[["Jas 4:11","JAS.4.11"]],[["Prov 6:16-19","PRO.6.16-19"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Have you spoken of someone in a way you would regret if they heard?","v":[[2475,2481,"P8K"]]},{"n":298,"t":"Lying; Reparation","s":2482,"e":2487,"c":"2482–2487","ph":9,"k":"Lying deceives someone who has a right to the truth. Wrongs against truth require reparation.","w":"sheqer (שֶׁקֶר), 'falsehood'.","sc":[[["John 8:44","JHN.8.44"]],[["Col 3:9","COL.3.9"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Is there a falsehood you need to repair?","v":[[2482,2487,"P8K"]]},{"n":299,"t":"Respect for Truth: Secrets","s":2488,"e":2492,"c":"2488–2492","ph":9,"k":"Not everyone has a right to every truth. Professional secrets and the seal of confession must be kept.","w":"secretum (Latin), 'set apart'.","sc":[[["Sir 27:16-21","SIR.27.16-21"]],[["Prov 11:13","PRO.11.13"]]],"ec":"The absolute confessional seal is distinctive.","f":"How well do you keep confidences?","v":[[2488,2492,"P8L"]]},{"n":300,"r":1,"q":"How have the commandments on life, purity and property challenged your habits?","items":[[271,"Scandal"],[272,"Respect for Health"],[273,"Research, Bodily Integrity and the Dead"],[274,"Peace and Anger"],[275,"Avoiding War"],[276,"In Brief: Fifth Commandment"],[277,"Sixth Commandment: Male and Female"],[278,"The Virtue of Chastity"],[279,"Chastity and Friendship"],[280,"Offenses Against Chastity"],[281,"Chastity and Homosexual Inclination"],[282,"Conjugal Love"],[283,"Fruitfulness and the Regulation of Births"],[284,"The Gift of a Child; Infertility"],[285,"Adultery and Divorce"],[286,"Other Offenses; In Brief"],[287,"Seventh Commandment: The Universal Destination of Goods"],[288,"Respect for Goods and Persons"],[289,"Creation and Animals"],[290,"The Social Doctrine of the Church"],[291,"Economic Activity and Justice"],[292,"Justice Among Nations"],[293,"Love for the Poor"],[294,"In Brief: Seventh Commandment"],[295,"Eighth Commandment: Living in the Truth"],[296,"Bearing Witness; Martyrdom"],[297,"Offenses: Rash Judgment, Detraction, Calumny"],[298,"Lying; Reparation"],[299,"Respect for Truth: Secrets"]]},{"n":301,"t":"Truth and the Media","s":2493,"e":2499,"c":"2493–2499","ph":9,"k":"Media must serve truth, justice and the common good. Users need discernment and temperance.","w":"communicatio (Latin), 'sharing'.","sc":[[["Phil 4:8","PHP.4.8"]],[["Eph 4:29","EPH.4.29"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What would a media 'fast' look like for you?","v":[[2493,2499,"P8M"]]},{"n":302,"t":"Truth, Beauty and Sacred Art; In Brief","s":2500,"e":2513,"c":"2500–2513","ph":9,"k":"Beauty expresses truth. Sacred art should lead to adoration and prayer.","w":"kalos (καλός), 'beautiful, good'.","sc":[[["Wis 13:5","WIS.13.5"]],[["Ps 27:4","PSA.27.4"]]],"ec":"There is a contrast with plain Protestant aesthetics, though Anglican church music is a great shared treasure.","f":"What beauty has drawn you to God?","v":[[2500,2503,"P8N"],[2504,2513,"P8O"]]},{"n":303,"t":"Ninth Commandment: Purity of Heart","s":2514,"e":2519,"c":"2514–2519","ph":9,"k":"The ninth commandment forbids lustful desires. Purity of heart is the work of grace and of the virtues.","w":"katharos tē kardia (καθαρὸς τῇ καρδίᾳ), 'pure in heart' (Matt 5:8).","sc":[[["Matt 5:8","MAT.5.8"]],[["Ps 51:10","PSA.51.10"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What practices help guard your heart?","v":[[2514,2516,"P8P"],[2517,2519,"P8Q"]]},{"n":304,"t":"The Battle for Purity; Modesty","s":2520,"e":2527,"c":"2520–2527","ph":9,"k":"Purity requires chastity, purity of intention and of vision, and prayer. Modesty protects the intimate center of the person.","w":"aidōs (αἰδώς), 'modesty, reverence'.","sc":[[["1 Tim 2:9","1TI.2.9"]],[["Job 31:1","JOB.31.1"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What does modesty protect, beyond dress?","v":[[2520,2527,"P8R"]]},{"n":305,"t":"In Brief: Ninth Commandment","s":2528,"e":2533,"c":"2528–2533","ph":9,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"lev tahor (לֵב טָהוֹר), 'clean heart' (Ps 51:10).","sc":[[["Ps 51:10","PSA.51.10"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Pray Psalm 51:10 as your prayer for the day.","v":[[2528,2533,"P8S"]]},{"n":306,"t":"Tenth Commandment: Covetousness and Envy","s":2534,"e":2540,"c":"2534–2540","ph":9,"k":"The tenth commandment forbids greed and envy. Envy is a capital sin that rejoices in another's misfortune.","w":"chamad (חָמַד), 'to covet, desire'.","sc":[[["Exod 20:17","EXO.20.17"]],[["Jas 3:16","JAS.3.16"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Whose life do you envy, and why?","v":[[2534,2534,"P8T"],[2535,2540,"P8U"]]},{"n":307,"t":"Desires of the Spirit; Poverty of Heart","s":2541,"e":2547,"c":"2541–2547","ph":9,"k":"The Spirit's desires conquer the desires of the flesh. Jesus calls for detachment from riches: poverty of heart.","w":"ptōchoi tō pneumati (πτωχοὶ τῷ πνεύματι), 'poor in spirit'.","sc":[[["Matt 5:3","MAT.5.3"]],[["Luke 12:15","LUK.12.15"]],[["Gal 5:16-17","GAL.5.16-17"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What would poverty of heart free you from?","v":[[2541,2543,"P8V"],[2544,2547,"P8W"]]},{"n":308,"t":"'I Want to See God'; In Brief","s":2548,"e":2557,"c":"2548–2557","ph":9,"k":"The deepest human desire is to see God. Detachment from things opens the heart to him.","w":"visio Dei (Latin), 'vision of God'.","sc":[[["Ps 27:4","PSA.27.4"]],[["1 John 3:2","1JN.3.2"]]],"ec":"Shared hope.","f":"What do you most want? Is God at the root of that desire?","v":[[2548,2550,"P8X"],[2551,2557,"P8Y"]]},{"n":309,"t":"What Is Prayer?","s":2558,"e":2561,"c":"2558–2561","ph":10,"k":"Prayer is the raising of mind and heart to God, a gift that requires humility. At the well, Jesus thirsts for our thirst.","w":"proseuchē (προσευχή), 'prayer'.","sc":[[["John 4:7-10","JHN.4.7-10"]],[["Ps 130:1","PSA.130.1"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What are you thirsting for as you begin to pray?","v":[[2558,2561,"P8Z"]]},{"n":310,"t":"Prayer as Covenant and Communion","s":2562,"e":2565,"c":"2562–2565","ph":10,"k":"Prayer comes from the heart, the place of decision. It is a covenant relationship and communion with the Trinity.","w":"lev (לֵב), 'heart'. The center of the person, not only emotion.","sc":[[["Ps 51:10","PSA.51.10"]],[["Rom 8:26-27","ROM.8.26-27"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where in your daily routine is your heart most present to God?","v":[[2562,2565,"P8Z"]]},{"n":311,"t":"The Universal Call to Prayer","s":2566,"e":2569,"c":"2566–2569","ph":10,"k":"God calls every person to prayer from creation onward. Abel, Noah and Melchizedek prayed.","w":"tephillah (תְּפִלָּה), 'prayer'.","sc":[[["Gen 4:4","GEN.4.4"]],[["Gen 8:20","GEN.8.20"]],[["Gen 14:18-20","GEN.14.18-20"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Who first taught you to pray?","v":[[2566,2567,"P90"],[2568,2569,"P91"]]},{"n":312,"t":"Abraham and Moses: Prayer of Faith and Intercession","s":2570,"e":2577,"c":"2570–2577","ph":10,"k":"Abraham prays with obedient faith and bold intercession. Moses speaks with God 'face to face' and intercedes for his people.","w":"panim el panim (פָּנִים אֶל פָּנִים), 'face to face' (Exod 33:11).","sc":[[["Gen 18:16-33","GEN.18.16-33"]],[["Exod 32:11-14","EXO.32.11-14"]],[["Exod 33:11","EXO.33.11"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"For whom should you intercede as boldly as Abraham did?","v":[[2570,2577,"P91"]]},{"n":313,"t":"David, Elijah and the Prophets","s":2578,"e":2584,"c":"2578–2584","ph":10,"k":"David is the king after God's heart, and the Temple becomes a place of prayer. Elijah's prayer shows the conversion of heart God seeks.","w":"kavanah (כַּוָּנָה), 'intention, directed heart'.","sc":[[["1 Kgs 18:36-39","1KI.18.36-39"]],[["2 Sam 7:18-29","2SA.7.18-29"]],[["Jas 5:17-18","JAS.5.17-18"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What does Elijah teach about persevering prayer?","v":[[2578,2584,"P91"]]},{"n":314,"t":"The Psalms","s":2585,"e":2589,"c":"2585–2589","ph":10,"k":"The Psalms are the masterwork of prayer in the Old Testament, prayed by Christ and by the Church.","w":"tehillim (תְּהִלִּים), 'praises'.","sc":[[["Ps 22","PSA.22"]],[["Ps 23","PSA.23"]],[["Ps 150","PSA.150"]]],"ec":"Psalm-singing is a shared treasure.","f":"Which Psalm is most 'yours'?","v":[[2585,2589,"P91"]]},{"n":315,"t":"In Brief: Prayer in the Old Testament","s":2590,"e":2597,"c":"2590–2597","ph":10,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"selah (סֶלָה), probably a pause.","sc":[[["Ps 46:10","PSA.46.10"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Take a 'selah' pause of five minutes in silence.","v":[[2590,2597,"P92"]]},{"n":316,"t":"Jesus Prays","s":2598,"e":2602,"c":"2598–2602","ph":10,"k":"Jesus learned to pray from Mary and the Jewish tradition. He prays before the decisive moments of his mission and withdraws to be alone with the Father.","w":"erēmos (ἔρημος), 'desert, lonely place'.","sc":[[["Luke 3:21","LUK.3.21"]],[["Luke 6:12","LUK.6.12"]],[["Mark 1:35","MRK.1.35"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where is your 'lonely place' for prayer?","v":[[2598,2602,"P93"]]},{"n":317,"t":"Jesus' Prayer of Thanksgiving and Agony","s":2603,"e":2606,"c":"2603–2606","ph":10,"k":"Jesus thanks the Father before acting. His prayer in Gethsemane and on the Cross gathers up every human cry.","w":"Eli, Eli, lema sabachthani (Aramaic), 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?' (Ps 22:1).","sc":[[["Matt 11:25-27","MAT.11.25-27"]],[["John 11:41-42","JHN.11.41-42"]],[["Mark 15:34","MRK.15.34"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What cry can you bring to God, knowing Jesus has prayed it before you?","v":[[2603,2606,"P93"]]},{"n":318,"t":"Jesus Teaches Us to Pray","s":2607,"e":2615,"c":"2607–2615","ph":10,"k":"Jesus teaches conversion of heart, faith, persistence and praying in his name.","w":"en tō onomati mou (ἐν τῷ ὀνόματί μου), 'in my name'.","sc":[[["Luke 11:5-13","LUK.11.5-13"]],[["Luke 18:1-8","LUK.18.1-8"]],[["John 14:13-14","JHN.14.13-14"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Which parable about prayer challenges you most?","v":[[2607,2615,"P93"]]},{"n":319,"t":"Jesus Hears Prayer; Mary's Prayer","s":2616,"e":2622,"c":"2616–2622","ph":10,"k":"Jesus answers faith. Mary's prayer is shown in her 'fiat' and her Magnificat.","w":"Magnificat (Latin), 'magnifies' (Luke 1:46).","sc":[[["Luke 1:46-55","LUK.1.46-55"]],[["John 2:1-5","JHN.2.1-5"]]],"ec":"The Magnificat is sung at Anglican Evensong, so it is shared.","f":"Pray the Magnificat today.","v":[[2616,2619,"P93"],[2620,2622,"P94"]]},{"n":320,"t":"Blessing and Adoration","s":2623,"e":2628,"c":"2623–2628","ph":10,"k":"In the age of the Church, the Spirit teaches prayer. Blessing is the meeting of God's gift and our response, and adoration is creatures bowing before their Creator.","w":"barukh (בָּרוּךְ), 'blessed'.","sc":[[["Eph 1:3","EPH.1.3"]],[["Ps 95:6","PSA.95.6"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Spend five minutes simply adoring.","v":[[2623,2625,"P95"],[2626,2628,"P96"]]},{"n":321,"t":"Prayer of Petition","s":2629,"e":2633,"c":"2629–2633","ph":10,"k":"Asking is the most usual form of prayer. It begins with asking forgiveness, then the Kingdom, then our needs.","w":"deēsis (δέησις), 'petition'.","sc":[[["Matt 6:33","MAT.6.33"]],[["Phil 4:6","PHP.4.6"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What are you afraid to ask God for?","v":[[2629,2633,"P97"]]},{"n":322,"t":"Intercession and Thanksgiving","s":2634,"e":2638,"c":"2634–2638","ph":10,"k":"Intercession joins Christ's own intercession. Thanksgiving marks the whole Christian life and is perfected in the Eucharist.","w":"eucharisteō (εὐχαριστέω), 'to give thanks'.","sc":[[["1 Tim 2:1-4","1TI.2.1-4"]],[["1 Thess 5:18","1TH.5.18"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"List ten things you are thankful for today.","v":[[2634,2636,"P98"],[2637,2638,"P99"]]},{"n":323,"t":"Praise","s":2639,"e":2643,"c":"2639–2643","ph":10,"k":"Praise recognizes God for who he is, not for what he gives. All forms of prayer culminate in it.","w":"hallelu-Yah (הַלְלוּ־יָהּ), 'praise the LORD'.","sc":[[["Ps 150","PSA.150"]],[["Rev 19:1-8","REV.19.1-8"]]],"ec":"Shared, with charismatic praise a natural fit.","f":"Praise God today without asking for anything.","v":[[2639,2643,"P9A"]]},{"n":324,"t":"In Brief: Forms of Prayer","s":2644,"e":2649,"c":"2644–2649","ph":10,"k":"Summary: blessing, petition, intercession, thanksgiving and praise. A lighter day.","w":"ACTS (Adoration, Confession, Thanksgiving, Supplication), an evangelical teaching aid that maps closely onto these forms.","sc":[[["Phil 4:6","PHP.4.6"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Which form of prayer is missing from your routine?","v":[[2644,2649,"P9B"]]},{"n":325,"t":"The Tradition of Prayer; The Word of God","s":2650,"e":2654,"c":"2650–2654","ph":10,"k":"We learn to pray within the Church. Scripture is a wellspring of prayer through lectio divina.","w":"lectio, meditatio, oratio, contemplatio (Latin), the four steps of lectio divina.","sc":[[["Ps 1:2","PSA.1.2"]],[["Josh 1:8","JOS.1.8"]]],"ec":"Evangelical 'quiet times' are close kin to this.","f":"Try lectio divina with tomorrow's Gospel reading.","v":[[2650,2651,"P9C"],[2652,2654,"P9D"]]},{"n":326,"t":"Liturgy, Virtues and Today","s":2655,"e":2662,"c":"2655–2662","ph":10,"k":"The liturgy, the theological virtues and the present moment are all wellsprings of prayer.","w":"hodie (Latin), 'today' (Heb 3:7).","sc":[[["Heb 3:7-8","HEB.3.7-8"]],[["Ps 95:7-8","PSA.95.7-8"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How can today, as it is, become prayer?","v":[[2655,2660,"P9D"],[2661,2662,"P9E"]]},{"n":327,"t":"Prayer to the Father and to Jesus","s":2663,"e":2669,"c":"2663–2669","ph":10,"k":"We pray to the Father through Jesus. The Jesus Prayer and devotion to the Sacred Heart are both forms of prayer to Jesus.","w":"Kyrie eleison (Κύριε ἐλέησον), 'Lord, have mercy'.","sc":[[["Luke 18:13","LUK.18.13"],["38","LUK.18.38"]],[["Phil 2:9-11","PHP.2.9-11"]]],"ec":"The Jesus Prayer is loved across traditions.","f":"Pray the Jesus Prayer slowly for ten minutes.","v":[[2663,2669,"P9F"]]},{"n":328,"t":"'Come, Holy Spirit'","s":2670,"e":2672,"c":"2670–2672","ph":10,"k":"The Church invites us to call on the Spirit daily, especially before beginning anything.","w":"Veni, Sancte Spiritus (Latin), 'Come, Holy Spirit'.","sc":[[["Luke 11:13","LUK.11.13"]],[["Rom 8:26","ROM.8.26"]]],"ec":"This meets the charismatic emphasis on the Spirit.","f":"Begin each task today with 'Come, Holy Spirit'.","v":[[2670,2672,"P9F"]]},{"n":329,"t":"Prayer with Mary","s":2673,"e":2679,"c":"2673–2679","ph":10,"k":"Mary prays with us and for us. The Hail Mary joins Scripture (Luke 1:28, 42) with a petition, and the Rosary meditates on Christ's mysteries.","w":"Ave Maria (Latin), 'Hail Mary'.","sc":[[["Luke 1:28","LUK.1.28"],["42","LUK.1.42"]],[["John 2:5","JHN.2.5"]]],"ec":"The first half of the Hail Mary is pure Scripture.","f":"Pray one decade of the Rosary meditating on one mystery.","v":[[2673,2679,"P9F"]]},{"n":330,"r":1,"q":"What do the commandments on truth and desire reveal about your heart?","items":[[301,"Truth and the Media"],[302,"Truth, Beauty and Sacred Art; In Brief"],[303,"Ninth Commandment: Purity of Heart"],[304,"The Battle for Purity; Modesty"],[305,"In Brief: Ninth Commandment"],[306,"Tenth Commandment: Covetousness and Envy"],[307,"Desires of the Spirit; Poverty of Heart"],[308,"'I Want to See God'; In Brief"],[309,"What Is Prayer?"],[310,"Prayer as Covenant and Communion"],[311,"The Universal Call to Prayer"],[312,"Abraham and Moses: Prayer of Faith and Intercession"],[313,"David, Elijah and the Prophets"],[314,"The Psalms"],[315,"In Brief: Prayer in the Old Testament"],[316,"Jesus Prays"],[317,"Jesus' Prayer of Thanksgiving and Agony"],[318,"Jesus Teaches Us to Pray"],[319,"Jesus Hears Prayer; Mary's Prayer"],[320,"Blessing and Adoration"],[321,"Prayer of Petition"],[322,"Intercession and Thanksgiving"],[323,"Praise"],[324,"In Brief: Forms of Prayer"],[325,"The Tradition of Prayer; The Word of God"],[326,"Liturgy, Virtues and Today"],[327,"Prayer to the Father and to Jesus"],[328,"'Come, Holy Spirit'"],[329,"Prayer with Mary"]]},{"n":331,"t":"In Brief; Cloud of Witnesses","s":2680,"e":2684,"c":"2680–2684","ph":10,"k":"The saints are witnesses and teachers of prayer. Their 'schools' of spirituality (Benedictine, Franciscan, Ignatian, Carmelite) guide us.","w":"schola (Latin), 'school'.","sc":[[["Heb 12:1","HEB.12.1"]]],"ec":"Shared admiration for the saints.","f":"Which saint's way of prayer attracts you?","v":[[2680,2682,"P9G"],[2683,2684,"P9H"]]},{"n":332,"t":"Servants and Places of Prayer","s":2685,"e":2691,"c":"2685–2691","ph":10,"k":"The family, ordained ministers, religious, catechists, prayer groups and spiritual directors all help us pray. Churches, monasteries, shrines and a prayer corner at home are good places for it.","w":"oratorium (Latin), 'place of prayer'.","sc":[[["Matt 18:20","MAT.18.20"]],[["Matt 6:6","MAT.6.6"]]],"ec":"Prayer groups are common ground with charismatic and evangelical practice.","f":"Could you set up a simple prayer corner at home?","v":[[2685,2691,"P9H"]]},{"n":333,"t":"In Brief: Guides for Prayer","s":2692,"e":2696,"c":"2692–2696","ph":10,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"hodēgos (ὁδηγός), 'guide' (John 16:13).","sc":[[["John 16:13","JHN.16.13"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Do you need a spiritual director?","v":[[2692,2696,"P9I"]]},{"n":334,"t":"The Life of Prayer","s":2697,"e":2699,"c":"2697–2699","ph":10,"k":"We pray as we live because we live as we pray. Daily, weekly and yearly rhythms all have their place.","w":"ora et labora (Latin, Benedictine), 'pray and work'.","sc":[[["1 Thess 5:17","1TH.5.17"]],[["Ps 55:17","PSA.55.17"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What rhythm of prayer can you actually sustain?","v":[[2697,2699,"P9J"]]},{"n":335,"t":"Vocal Prayer","s":2700,"e":2704,"c":"2700–2704","ph":10,"k":"Words matter. Vocal prayer, taught by Christ himself, involves body and soul and is suited to groups.","w":"vox (Latin), 'voice'.","sc":[[["Matt 6:9-13","MAT.6.9-13"]],[["Mark 14:36","MRK.14.36"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Pray aloud today, even if alone.","v":[[2700,2704,"P9K"]]},{"n":336,"t":"Meditation","s":2705,"e":2708,"c":"2705–2708","ph":10,"k":"Meditation is a seeking: the mind engages Scripture, icons, the liturgy and the 'book of life'. It mobilizes thought, imagination, emotion and desire.","w":"meditatio (Latin) / hagah (הָגָה), 'to murmur, ponder' (Ps 1:2).","sc":[[["Ps 1:2","PSA.1.2"]],[["Luke 2:19","LUK.2.19"]]],"ec":"This is close kin to evangelical Bible meditation.","f":"Meditate on one Gospel scene using your imagination.","v":[[2705,2708,"P9L"]]},{"n":337,"t":"Contemplative Prayer I","s":2709,"e":2714,"c":"2709–2714","ph":10,"k":"Teresa of Ávila describes contemplation as a close sharing between friends. It is a gaze of faith fixed on Jesus.","w":"contemplatio (Latin), 'gazing'.","sc":[[["Ps 27:4","PSA.27.4"]],[["2 Cor 3:18","2CO.3.18"]]],"ec":"There are some parallels in Quaker and charismatic 'soaking' prayer.","f":"Sit for ten minutes simply looking at Jesus in faith.","v":[[2709,2714,"P9M"]]},{"n":338,"t":"Contemplative Prayer II","s":2715,"e":2719,"c":"2715–2719","ph":10,"k":"Contemplation is a gaze, a hearing of the Word, silence and union. A peasant once told the Curé of Ars, 'I look at him and he looks at me.'","w":"hesychia (ἡσυχία), 'stillness'.","sc":[[["Ps 46:10","PSA.46.10"]],[["1 Kgs 19:11-13","1KI.19.11-13"]]],"ec":"Shared mystical heritage.","f":"Where do you find stillness?","v":[[2715,2719,"P9M"]]},{"n":339,"t":"In Brief: Expressions of Prayer","s":2720,"e":2724,"c":"2720–2724","ph":10,"k":"Summary of vocal, meditative and contemplative prayer. A lighter day.","w":"silentium (Latin), 'silence'.","sc":[[["Hab 2:20","HAB.2.20"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Which expression of prayer comes most naturally to you?","v":[[2720,2724,"P9N"]]},{"n":340,"t":"The Battle of Prayer: Objections","s":2725,"e":2728,"c":"2725–2728","ph":10,"k":"Prayer is a battle against ourselves, the world and the tempter. Common objections include 'no time', 'it's useless' and 'I'm not religious'.","w":"agōnizomai (ἀγωνίζομαι), 'to struggle' (Col 4:12).","sc":[[["Col 4:12","COL.4.12"]],[["Eph 6:18","EPH.6.18"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Which objection do you use most?","v":[[2725,2725,"P9O"],[2726,2728,"P9P"]]},{"n":341,"t":"Distraction, Dryness and Acedia","s":2729,"e":2733,"c":"2729–2733","ph":10,"k":"Distractions reveal attachments, and we can return to God through them. Dryness tests faith. Acedia is spiritual sloth.","w":"acedia (Latin, from Greek ἀκηδία), 'listlessness'.","sc":[[["Ps 63:1","PSA.63.1"]],[["Mark 14:38","MRK.14.38"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How do you respond when prayer feels dry?","v":[[2729,2733,"P9Q"]]},{"n":342,"t":"Filial Trust: Unanswered Prayer","s":2734,"e":2737,"c":"2734–2737","ph":10,"k":"When prayer seems unanswered, examine what kind of God we pray to. We ask for good things, and God gives the Spirit.","w":"parrēsia (παρρησία), 'bold confidence'.","sc":[[["Rom 8:26","ROM.8.26"]],[["Jas 4:3","JAS.4.3"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What unanswered prayer still troubles you?","v":[[2734,2737,"P9R"]]},{"n":343,"t":"The Power of Prayer","s":2738,"e":2741,"c":"2738–2741","ph":10,"k":"Prayer is effective because it is united to Jesus' prayer. Christ prays in us and for us.","w":"energeō (ἐνεργέω), 'to be at work'.","sc":[[["Jas 5:16","JAS.5.16"]],[["Heb 7:25","HEB.7.25"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where have you seen prayer make a real difference?","v":[[2738,2741,"P9R"]]},{"n":344,"t":"Pray Constantly","s":2742,"e":2745,"c":"2742–2745","ph":10,"k":"Prayer can be continual: it is a disposition of love. Prayer and Christian life are inseparable.","w":"adialeiptōs (ἀδιαλείπτως), 'without ceasing' (1 Thess 5:17).","sc":[[["1 Thess 5:17","1TH.5.17"]],[["Eph 5:20","EPH.5.20"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What practice could weave prayer through your day?","v":[[2742,2745,"P9S"]]},{"n":345,"t":"The Prayer of the Hour of Jesus","s":2746,"e":2751,"c":"2746–2751","ph":10,"k":"John 17, Jesus' high-priestly prayer, gathers up the whole economy of salvation: glory, consecration and unity.","w":"hagiason (ἁγίασον), 'consecrate' (John 17:17).","sc":[[["John 17","JHN.17"]]],"ec":"Christian unity (John 17:21) belongs to all Christians.","f":"Read John 17 slowly. Where do you hear yourself in it?","v":[[2746,2751,"P9T"]]},{"n":346,"t":"In Brief: The Battle of Prayer","s":2752,"e":2758,"c":"2752–2758","ph":10,"k":"Summary. A lighter day.","w":"hypomonē (ὑπομονή), 'perseverance'.","sc":[[["Luke 18:1","LUK.18.1"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Recommit to one practice of prayer.","v":[[2752,2758,"P9U"]]},{"n":347,"t":"The Lord's Prayer: Summary of the Gospel","s":2759,"e":2764,"c":"2759–2764","ph":10,"k":"The Lord's Prayer is at the center of Scripture. Tertullian called it a summary of the whole Gospel.","w":"Pater Noster (Latin), 'Our Father'.","sc":[[["Matt 6:9-13","MAT.6.9-13"]],[["Luke 11:2-4","LUK.11.2-4"]]],"ec":"Shared by every Christian tradition.","f":"Pray the Our Father as slowly as you can.","v":[[2759,2760,"P9V"],[2761,2761,"P9W"],[2762,2764,"P9X"]]},{"n":348,"t":"The Lord's Prayer and the Church","s":2765,"e":2772,"c":"2765–2772","ph":10,"k":"The Lord's Prayer is the prayer of the Church, central to Baptism and the Eucharist. It is prayed with eschatological hope.","w":"oratio dominica (Latin), 'the Lord's prayer'.","sc":[[["Matt 6:9-13","MAT.6.9-13"]],[["Didache 8 (an early Christian text, not Scripture)",null]]],"ec":"Protestants add the doxology ('for thine is the kingdom') directly to the prayer. At Mass it follows a short prayer called the embolism.","f":"Pray the Our Father three times today, as the Didache taught.","v":[[2765,2766,"P9Y"],[2767,2772,"P9Z"]]},{"n":349,"t":"In Brief; 'We Dare to Say'","s":2773,"e":2778,"c":"2773–2778","ph":10,"k":"We approach God with bold confidence and simplicity. It is a gift to be able to call God 'Father'.","w":"parrēsia (παρρησία), 'boldness'.","sc":[[["Eph 3:12","EPH.3.12"]],[["Heb 4:16","HEB.4.16"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Do you approach God timidly or boldly?","v":[[2773,2776,"PA0"],[2777,2778,"PA1"]]},{"n":350,"t":"Abba, Father","s":2779,"e":2785,"c":"2779–2785","ph":10,"k":"Before calling God 'Father', we purify our images of fatherhood. We are adopted children, and this calls for humility and a child's trust.","w":"Abba (Aramaic, אַבָּא), 'Father'.","sc":[[["Rom 8:15","ROM.8.15"]],[["Gal 4:6","GAL.4.6"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Do you need to purify your image of God as Father?","v":[[2779,2785,"PA2"]]},{"n":351,"t":"'Our'","s":2786,"e":2793,"c":"2786–2793","ph":10,"k":"'Our' expresses the new covenant relationship and the communion of believers. It commits us to unity and to all people.","w":"hēmōn (ἡμῶν), 'our'.","sc":[[["John 20:17","JHN.20.17"]],[["Eph 4:6","EPH.4.6"]]],"ec":"'Our' unites all Christians.","f":"Who do you include when you pray 'our'?","v":[[2786,2793,"PA3"]]},{"n":352,"t":"'Who Art in Heaven'; In Brief","s":2794,"e":2802,"c":"2794–2802","ph":10,"k":"'Heaven' is not a place but God's majesty and presence in the hearts of the just. It is our true homeland.","w":"ouranos (οὐρανός), 'heaven'.","sc":[[["Phil 3:20","PHP.3.20"]],[["Col 3:1-3","COL.3.1-3"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How does knowing your homeland is heaven shape your life now?","v":[[2794,2796,"PA4"],[2797,2802,"PA5"]]},{"n":353,"t":"The Seven Petitions","s":2803,"e":2806,"c":"2803–2806","ph":10,"k":"The first three petitions are about God's glory, and the last four are about our needs.","w":"hepta (ἑπτά), 'seven'.","sc":[[["Matt 6:9-13","MAT.6.9-13"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Which petition do you rush through?","v":[[2803,2806,"PA6"]]},{"n":354,"t":"'Hallowed Be Thy Name'","s":2807,"e":2815,"c":"2807–2815","ph":10,"k":"We ask that God's name be made holy in us and in the world. It is both praise and petition.","w":"hagiasthētō (ἁγιασθήτω), 'let it be hallowed'.","sc":[[["Ezek 36:20-23","EZK.36.20-23"]],[["John 17:11","JHN.17.11"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How can your life make God's name holy today?","v":[[2807,2815,"PA7"]]},{"n":355,"t":"'Thy Kingdom Come'","s":2816,"e":2821,"c":"2816–2821","ph":10,"k":"We long for Christ's return and for the Kingdom to grow now through the Spirit.","w":"Maranatha (Aramaic), 'Our Lord, come'.","sc":[[["Rev 22:20","REV.22.20"]],[["Rom 14:17","ROM.14.17"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What would the Kingdom's coming change in your home?","v":[[2816,2821,"PA8"]]},{"n":356,"t":"'Thy Will Be Done'","s":2822,"e":2827,"c":"2822–2827","ph":10,"k":"The Father wills that all be saved. We unite our will to Christ's, as he did in Gethsemane.","w":"genēthētō (γενηθήτω), 'let it be done'.","sc":[[["Luke 22:42","LUK.22.42"]],[["1 Tim 2:4","1TI.2.4"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where is your will resisting God's?","v":[[2822,2827,"PA9"]]},{"n":357,"t":"'Give Us This Day Our Daily Bread' I","s":2828,"e":2833,"c":"2828–2833","ph":10,"k":"We ask with filial trust for what we need. The petition also commits us to share with those who hunger.","w":"lechem (לֶחֶם), 'bread'.","sc":[[["Matt 6:25-34","MAT.6.25-34"]],[["Exod 16","EXO.16"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"How can your 'daily bread' feed someone else?","v":[[2828,2833,"PAA"]]},{"n":358,"t":"'Daily Bread' II: Epiousios","s":2834,"e":2837,"c":"2834–2837","ph":10,"k":"'Daily' (epiousios) is a rare word. It points also to the Word of God and the Eucharist, the bread of the Kingdom.","w":"epiousios (ἐπιούσιος), 'daily' or 'supersubstantial'. Its meaning is uncertain.","sc":[[["John 6:32-35","JHN.6.32-35"]],[["Matt 4:4","MAT.4.4"]]],"ec":"The Eucharistic reading of 'daily bread' is patristic (Jerome, Cyprian).","f":"Which 'bread' do you most need: physical, the Word, or the Eucharist?","v":[[2834,2837,"PAA"]]},{"n":359,"t":"'Forgive Us Our Trespasses' I","s":2838,"e":2841,"c":"2838–2841","ph":10,"k":"We confess ourselves sinners and trust in mercy, which needs a heart open to forgive.","w":"opheilēma (ὀφείλημα), 'debt' (Matt 6:12).","sc":[[["Matt 6:12","MAT.6.12"]],[["Luke 15:18-24","LUK.15.18-24"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What debt do you most need forgiven?","v":[[2838,2841,"PAB"]]},{"n":360,"r":1,"q":"Which form of prayer has grown in you? What will you carry into the final days?","items":[[331,"In Brief; Cloud of Witnesses"],[332,"Servants and Places of Prayer"],[333,"In Brief: Guides for Prayer"],[334,"The Life of Prayer"],[335,"Vocal Prayer"],[336,"Meditation"],[337,"Contemplative Prayer I"],[338,"Contemplative Prayer II"],[339,"In Brief: Expressions of Prayer"],[340,"The Battle of Prayer: Objections"],[341,"Distraction, Dryness and Acedia"],[342,"Filial Trust: Unanswered Prayer"],[343,"The Power of Prayer"],[344,"Pray Constantly"],[345,"The Prayer of the Hour of Jesus"],[346,"In Brief: The Battle of Prayer"],[347,"The Lord's Prayer: Summary of the Gospel"],[348,"The Lord's Prayer and the Church"],[349,"In Brief; 'We Dare to Say'"],[350,"Abba, Father"],[351,"'Our'"],[352,"'Who Art in Heaven'; In Brief"],[353,"The Seven Petitions"],[354,"'Hallowed Be Thy Name'"],[355,"'Thy Kingdom Come'"],[356,"'Thy Will Be Done'"],[357,"'Give Us This Day Our Daily Bread' I"],[358,"'Daily Bread' II: Epiousios"],[359,"'Forgive Us Our Trespasses' I"]]},{"n":361,"t":"'As We Forgive Those Who Trespass Against Us' II","s":2842,"e":2845,"c":"2842–2845","ph":10,"k":"Our forgiving does not earn forgiveness, but an unforgiving heart blocks mercy. Forgiving enemies is the summit of prayer.","w":"aphiēmi (ἀφίημι), 'to release'.","sc":[[["Matt 18:21-35","MAT.18.21-35"]],[["Luke 23:34","LUK.23.34"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Whom do you need to release today?","v":[[2842,2845,"PAB"]]},{"n":362,"t":"'Lead Us Not into Temptation'","s":2846,"e":2849,"c":"2846–2849","ph":10,"k":"We ask not to be allowed to enter into temptation. This requires discernment and vigilance.","w":"peirasmos (πειρασμός), 'trial, temptation'.","sc":[[["1 Cor 10:13","1CO.10.13"]],[["Jas 1:13-14","JAS.1.13-14"]],[["Mark 14:38","MRK.14.38"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"Where do you need to watch and pray?","v":[[2846,2849,"PAC"]]},{"n":363,"t":"'Deliver Us from Evil'","s":2850,"e":2854,"c":"2850–2854","ph":10,"k":"'Evil' here is the Evil One, Satan, whom Christ has already defeated. We pray for deliverance from all evils.","w":"tou ponērou (τοῦ πονηροῦ), 'from the evil one'.","sc":[[["John 17:15","JHN.17.15"]],[["1 John 5:18-19","1JN.5.18-19"]]],"ec":"Shared.","f":"What evil do you most need deliverance from?","v":[[2850,2854,"PAC"]]},{"n":364,"t":"The Doxology and Amen","s":2855,"e":2856,"c":"2855–2856","ph":10,"k":"The final doxology returns the kingdom, power and glory to God. Our Amen seals the prayer.","w":"amen (אָמֵן), 'so be it, it is firm'.","sc":[[["1 Chr 29:11","1CH.29.11"]],[["Rev 5:13","REV.5.13"]]],"ec":"The doxology is a shared heritage.","f":"Say 'Amen' with your whole heart today.","v":[[2855,2856,"PAD"]]},{"n":365,"t":"In Brief: The Lord's Prayer — Amen","s":2857,"e":2865,"c":"2857–2865","ph":10,"k":"This is the last paragraph of the Catechism. The whole faith comes to rest in the Lord's Prayer and in the 'Amen' of everyone who prays it.","w":"Amen (Hebrew), the last word of the Catechism and of the Bible (Rev 22:21).","sc":[[["Rev 22:20-21","REV.22.20-21"]]],"ec":"This is the end of the reading plan.","f":"Looking back over the year, what has God taught you?","v":[[2857,2865,"PAE"]]}];
